Ceviri.ekle("en","ekokuma",{
"tartisma-karadeniz-kirim-tatar-nogay":{"oneri": ["In the 1770s about nine-tenths of the population of the Crimea were Crimean Tatars.", "fdcee668"], "ad": ["Crimean Tatars and Nogais: from majority to minority on the peninsula", "508e3c93"], "metin": ["The Crimean Tatars were settled in the mountainous south of the peninsula, on its plains and in its towns; in the northern steppe, together with the Nogais, they led a semi-nomadic life. The Nogais were subject to the Crimean khan, but in the words of the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam this tie was \"loose\". At the beginning of the 18th century the Yedisan, Jamboyluk, Budjak and Kuban Nogais were under the rule of the Crimean Khanate. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, some of these hordes had moved from the Kuban basin to the north of the Black Sea under Kalmyk pressure.\n\nAccording to the estimates given by the Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine, about 400,000 Crimean Tatars lived in the Crimea in 1770, and they made up some 90 per cent of the population. In 1795 their number fell to 137,000 (87.6 per cent); in the census of 1897 they numbered 194,300 and made up 35.6 per cent of the population. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, the migrations that continued without interruption for 150 years after the annexation came in mass waves in 1812, 1828-1829, 1860-1861, 1874, 1890 and 1902. In 1860 alone nearly 200,000 Crimeans migrated to Ottoman lands, and from that date on the Crimean Tatars were a minority on the peninsula.\n\nThe fate of the Nogais was harsher still. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, in 1783 forces commanded by General Suvorov and Leontyev inflicted a crushing defeat and a massacre on the Nogais. According to the Entsyklopediia istorii Ukrainy, the Nogai horde that nomadized in the Kuban was partly destroyed and partly deported to the east of the Caucasus governorship. At the end of the 1850s the Nogais of the Kuban, Stavropol and Taurida migrated en masse to Ottoman lands and were settled in places such as Çukurova, Ankara, Konya, Kırşehir and Sivas. In the census of 1897 speakers of Nogai made up only 2.3 per cent of the population of Stavropol Governorate.", "6ff54bfc"], "kisa": ["In the 1770s about nine-tenths of the population of the Crimea were Crimean Tatars. By the census of 1897 this share had fallen to one-third. Waves of migration spanning more than a century explain the difference.", "e060e89f"], "not": ["Treat the figures with care: the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam writes that it is 'estimated' that at least 1,800,000 Crimean Tatars migrated to the Ottoman lands between 1783 and 1922. This total is disputed and cannot be compared directly with the Russian censuses. The figures for 1770 and 1795 given here are estimates provided by the IEU, not census data.", "c9a7ea8f"]},
"tartisma-karadeniz-ruslar-ukraynalilar":{"oneri": ["In 1897 the most widely spoken language in every one of the governorates north of the Black Sea was either Ukrainian or Russian.", "87e9be13"], "ad": ["The population of 'New Russia': Ukrainians, Russians and imported colonists", "c068097f"], "metin": ["The Russian state did not merely conquer the south; it also opened it to settlement. According to the Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine, Empress Elizabeth established New Serbia in 1752 in the northwest of the Zaporozhian lands. The 4,008 Cossack and peasant households there were removed, and in their place Serbian, and later Bulgarian, Wallachian and Greek military colonists were brought from the Habsburg lands. According to the same source, the Orthodox Greeks of the Crimea were resettled on the Azov coast, around present-day Mariupol. Some of them spoke Greek and some Turkish or Crimean Tatar, and they gave their villages the names of their settlements in the Crimea. Armenians were settled on the lower Don, in Nakhichevan near Rostov. At the beginning of the 19th century the government invited Germans, Bulgarians and other colonists to the empty steppes of Southern Ukraine and the Crimea.\n\nAccording to the mother-tongue tables of the 1897 census (as a percentage of each governorate total):\n- Taurida (1,447,790): Ukrainian 42.2 · Russian 27.9 · Tatar 13.6 · German 5.4 · Yiddish 3.8 · Bulgarian 2.8 · Greek 1.2\n- Kherson (2,733,612): Ukrainian 53.5 · Russian 21.0 · Yiddish 11.8 · Moldavian-Romanian 5.4 · German 4.5\n- Yekaterinoslav (2,113,674): Ukrainian 68.9 · Russian 17.3 · Yiddish 4.7 · German 3.8 · Greek 2.3\n- Don Host Oblast (2,564,238): Russian 66.8 · Ukrainian 28.1 · German 1.4 · Kalmyk 1.3 · Armenian 1.1\n- Kuban Oblast (1,918,881): Ukrainian 47.4 · Russian 42.6 · Circassian 2.0 · Karachay 1.4\n- Stavropol (873,301): Russian 55.2 · Ukrainian 36.6 · Nogai 2.3 · Turkmen 1.7 · Kalmyk 1.2\n\nThe general pattern is clear: Ukrainian predominated in the old Zaporozhian and Crimean steppe in the west, and Russian in the Don and Stavropol in the east. The Kuban was divided between the two. The towns, however, were far more mixed: according to the IEU, in Mariupol in 1897 speakers of Russian made up 63 per cent, of Yiddish 15 per cent, of Ukrainian 10 per cent, of Greek 5 per cent and of Turkish 3 per cent.", "fce67984"], "kisa": ["In 1897 the most widely spoken language in every one of the governorates north of the Black Sea was either Ukrainian or Russian. Alongside their speakers, however, there were also German, Jewish, Greek, Bulgarian, Serbian, Moldavian and Armenian communities; most had come at the invitation of the state.", "e9678ce5"], "not": ["The census of 1897 asked not about nationality but about mother tongue; the official name of the 'Ukrainian' column is 'Little Russian' and that of the 'Russian' column 'Great Russian'. For this reason the IEU writes that the official figures for the Crimea are unreliable: according to the same census, those born in the Ukrainian governorates were twice as numerous as speakers of Ukrainian. The IEU's estimate is that there were 180-200 thousand Ukrainians (most of them in the north) and 180-200 thousand Russians (most of them in the towns) on the peninsula.", "538f447b"]},
"tartisma-karadeniz-ukraynalilar-kimdir":{"ad": ["Who are the Ukrainians? The people of the \"borderland\"", "febfd27e"], "metin": ["The meaning of the name. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, the word \"Ukraine\" means \"march, border region\". The history of the country is the shared history of all the states and communities that have lived there; nor would it run counter to the historical evidence to say that its people were born of a mixture of these.\n\nROOTS: The area between the Middle Dnieper, the Pripet, the Carpathians and the Vistula is regarded as the original homeland of the early Slavs. The early history of Ukraine is linked to the Principality of Kyiv, which was destroyed by the Mongol-Tatar invasion in 1240. Earlier, the same steppe had been ruled by the Scythians, Sarmatians, Goths and Huns, followed by the Khazars, Pechenegs, Uzes and Cumans.\n\nDIVERGENCE: After Kyiv broke up, the eastern regions passed to the Golden Horde, while the western principalities passed first to Lithuania (Galicia-Volhynia 1323) and then to Poland (Galicia 1352). After the Union of Lublin (1569), the Polish nobility's imposition of Catholicism on the Orthodox people provoked a reaction: with the founding of the Kyiv Academy and the restoration of the Orthodox church hierarchy (1620), \"the idea of Ukrainianness began to take root.\"\n\nTHE COSSACKS and the Ukrainians: When Poland could not protect the region from Crimean raids, the people set up their own armed organization. The Cossacks, who grew stronger from the 16th century onward under their elected hetmans, made their centre the Sich (Zaporozhian Sich), founded in 1552 at the Dnieper rapids (Porohy). The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam refers to the Cossacks as the force representing Ukraine's national reaction; that is, the Zaporozhian Cossacks were a military and social section of the Ukrainians, not a separate nation. The Don Cossacks, on the other hand, are a separate community (see the card \"Were the Cossacks a people?\").\n\nNAMING: The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam records that the Ukrainians, who belong to the Slavic ethnic group, are also known by the name \"Little Russians\" and that their official language is Ukrainian; in the 2001 census they made up 77.8% of the country's population.", "643e17c8"], "kisa": ["The Ukrainians are an East Slavic people; their name comes from the word Ukraine, meaning \"march, border region\". Their identity was shaped by the legacy of Kyiv, Orthodox resistance under Lithuanian-Polish rule and the Cossack tradition.", "acb78b39"]},
"tartisma-kasrisirin-tarih-yazimi":{"oneri": ["1639 is generally regarded as the date of birth of one of the world's oldest borders.", "0c1b9b6c"], "ad": ["Founding document or founding myth? Two views of Qasr-e Shirin", "ff81c0c1"], "metin": ["The widespread account is this: in 1639 the frontier between the Ottomans and Safavid Iran was drawn, and from that day to this it has stood almost unchanged. In Turkish historiography this account is read together with Murad IV's victories at Yerevan and Baghdad: the sultan recovered Iraq, forced Iran to make peace and permanently secured the eastern frontier. The Ottoman side's information rests largely on the records in Kâtib Çelebi's Fezleke, Naîmâ's History and Feridun Bey's Münşeât. From the Iranian point of view, the same treaty marks the close of a period in which the reign of Shah Safi lost Baghdad as well as Kandahar; the TDV's article on the Safavids mentions it as a treaty that both determined the frontier to a large extent and began a long peace between the two countries. The historian Sabri Ateş objects to this picture. In his view, the idea of a '1639 border' is a founding myth firmly embedded in the historiographies and nationalisms of the countries that share this border. Yet there exist copies of the treaty that differ from one another; it took nearly four centuries for an ill-defined frontier zone to become a clearly defined and controlled line, and only through the work of the mixed commissions between 1843 and 1914 did the line become an internationally recognised border. The two views are not entirely opposed: 1639 really is the document to which all later treaties referred; the debate is about how definite a line that document drew.", "e2b609d1"], "kisa": ["1639 is generally regarded as the date of birth of one of the world's oldest borders. Some historians think this account is too neat.", "3bce2102"], "not": ["Read with care: 'taking a treaty as the basis' and applying every detail of its text exactly are not the same thing. The Kerden and Erzurum negotiations rested on 1639, but which copy the parties read, and how, became a matter of dispute.", "a30010da"]},
"tartisma-kibris-1878-beklenen-ingiliz-destegi":{"baslik": ["Did the British support promised in return for Cyprus ever come?", "b6b0fde2"], "metin": ["■ What the agreement said\nIn the Cyprus Convention of 4 June 1878, Britain gave an assurance that it would form a defensive alliance with the Ottoman State against a possible Russian invasion aimed at Anatolia. In return, the island was left to British administration. The arrangement was framed not as a cession of the island but as the handover of its administration: the surplus of revenue after administrative expenses would be paid to the Ottomans, the sharia courts would continue to function, and the religious endowments would be managed jointly.\n\n■ THE TWO SIDES OF THE DEBATE\nThe reading in favor: right after the War of '93, when a Russian descent into Anatolia was a concrete possibility, binding a great power by a commitment of defense was the best thing Ottoman diplomacy could obtain; this rapprochement had a share in the Ottomans' not being left alone at the Berlin table.\nThe reading against: the commitment was never actually put into operation; the island, on the other hand, never came back. Four years after the agreement the same state landed troops in Egypt, and on 5 November 1914 it also formally annexed Cyprus. At Lausanne, Turkey accepted the island's status under British administration.\n\n■ WHAT CAN BE MEASURED\nWhat can be said without taking sides in this debate is this: what the agreement promised the Ottomans was not territory but a GUARANTEE, and the entry on which this card relies shows no case in which the guarantee operated. The loss of the island, by contrast, is measurable and certain. This is the question to ask when assessing a diplomatic move: what slips out of one's hands is concrete, while what is received in return depends on a condition.\n\n■ WHAT CANNOT BE MEASURED\nNo explicit assessment of whether the agreement secured the expected support for the Ottomans was found in the entry examined; the two readings above are constructed by comparing the dates in the entry.", "373ba98c"], "kisa": ["An island was given in return for a promise of alliance. Was the promise kept? To look for the answer, waiting four years is enough.", "daacd894"]},
"tartisma-kirimrus-carlik-sovyet":{"oneri": ["For a long time Russian historiography portrayed the Crimean Khanate as the continuation of the Golden Horde, a raiding and plundering state…", "39f2a361"], "ad": ["From the 'Tatar yoke' to the 'nest of plunder': the Crimea in Tsarist and Soviet historiography", "285a3f9c"], "metin": ["In Russian historiography Mongol-Tatar rule is referred to as the 'Tatar yoke', and this period is held to have ended on the banks of the Ugra river in 1480. The Crimean khans, however, regarded themselves as the heirs of precisely that Golden Horde. For this reason, in the Russian account the Khanate was easily seen as the 'remnant of the yoke'. As Brian Glyn Williams points out, both Western and Soviet sources for a long time presented the Crimean Tatars as the 'Mongol invaders' of the 13th century. The Tsarist period: according to Johannes Remy, Russia legitimised the annexation of 1783 by a claim to the superiority of European culture and by leaning on the peninsula's past before the Tatars; it was even asserted that the Russians were descendants of the Scythians who had lived in the Crimea in antiquity. The cities were given names of Greek origin: Simferopol, Yevpatoria, Sevastopol, Feodosia. The Soviet period: the account grew harsher still. The Soviet historian P. N. Nadinskii, in his history of the Crimea published in Simferopol in 1951, wrote that the Crimea had been reduced under the wings of the Turkish sultan to a 'nest of plunder', and that the Tatars engaged in peaceful production only little and reluctantly; according to Williams, this thesis indirectly rejected the idea that the Crimean Tatars had a legitimate past of statehood. It must also be remembered that this book was written in the years when the Crimean Tatars had been driven from their homeland: as Remy reports, after the war the Soviet government deported all the Crimean Tatars, about 194,000 people, to Uzbekistan on a charge of collaboration; at least one in five died in the first eighteen months of exile, and Tatar place names were replaced by Russian ones. The Encyclopedia of Ukraine also notes that after the war Soviet researchers concerned themselves only with the Volga-Ural (Kazan) Tatars and ignored the Crimean Tatars. The same pressure was also felt in Soviet Ukrainian historiography: Ocakli shows that the historian Holobuc'kyj, who was obliged to follow the Marxist and Russocentric line, interpreted the events of the 1650s as the khan's fear that Ukraine's 'reunification' with Russia would put an end to the Tatar plundering raids.", "97fd94dc"], "kisa": ["For a long time Russian historiography portrayed the Crimean Khanate as the continuation of the Golden Horde, a raiding and plundering state. This account was used to explain not only the past but also the annexation of 1783 and the deportation of 1944.", "bb340b37"], "not": ["In present-day Russian memory the Crimea is remembered above all for the two great sieges of Sevastopol, that is, for Russian heroism; Remy's title puts these two faces together: heroism and ethnic cleansing.", "a32592b6"]},
"tartisma-kirimrus-esir-ticareti":{"oneri": ["In Russian, Ukrainian and Polish memory, the name of the Crimea is associated above all with the slave raids.", "374f0738"], "ad": ["The dark economy of the steppe: slave raids, slave markets, ransom", "d8a7d2f2"], "metin": ["The trade in captives held an important place in the economy of the Crimea. The TDV's article on the Crimea lists captives first among the chief goods sent from the peninsula to the Ottoman lands and to Egypt, followed by furs, hides, fish, beeswax and salt. According to Johannes Remy, most of the captives were Slavs seized in swift, surprise-style raids on the lands of Russia and Poland-Lithuania. The Encyclopedia of Ukraine writes that up to 1556 the Crimean Tatars made more than 80 raids on the regions of Podolia, Volhynia, Galicia and Kiev, and that in the year 1575 alone they carried off more than 35,000 people to be sold in the markets of the Crimea. Captivity also had a ransom side: captured nobles were often returned to their families for money. The TDV's article on the Girays relates that Feth Giray sent home, for a ransom, a Polish nobleman's daughter whom he had captured in a raid, and that because part of the money had not arrived, the girl was made to wait on the way. The question of captives was also a subject of diplomacy: it is recorded that, according to a Polish report, the Tatars who allied with the Ukrainian Cossacks in 1648 agreed to take captive not the Orthodox people but only Poles, and that in 1654 the mirzas who favoured the Cossack alliance argued that this alliance had brought many captives to the Crimea. The historian Brian Glyn Williams says two things together: from its foundation to the end of the 17th century the Crimea was the centre of a lively slave trade that sold tens of thousands of neighbouring Russian, Ukrainian and Polish peasants to the Ottomans, and the fear of the neighbouring peoples was not unfounded; but it was chiefly the nomadic Nogai element on the steppe that carried out the raids. The large settled Tatar population in the south of the peninsula, by contrast, made its living from viticulture, horticulture, crafts and trade, and ran the Khanate's administration, markets and religious institutions.", "2e3113f0"], "kisa": ["In Russian, Ukrainian and Polish memory, the name of the Crimea is associated above all with the slave raids. The sources agree that these were real; they diverge on who carried them out and whether there was nothing more to the Khanate than this.", "d97147d7"], "not": ["Be careful with the figures: the numbers of raids and captives come from contemporary reports and vary from source to source. The figures given here are the own figures of the source named.", "58daaba3"]},
"tartisma-kirimrus-moskova-akinlari":{"oneri": ["The raids were not only for booty: the Crimean khans regarded themselves as the legitimate heirs of the Golden Horde…", "484688f2"], "ad": ["Moscow burns: the Crimean khans' northern campaigns and the struggle over the legacy of the Golden Horde", "346a3565"], "metin": ["The Crimea and Moscow were not always enemies. Hacı Giray, the founder of the Khanate, had established friendship with Moscow against the Golden Horde. The balance changed when Mengli Giray destroyed Saray, the centre of the Golden Horde, in 1502: the common enemy had disappeared, and the two states had become rivals for the lands the Golden Horde left behind. In 1511 the Crimea entered into an alliance with Poland-Lithuania against Moscow. Mehmed I Giray routed a Russian army on the Oka river, advanced to the gates of Moscow and set fire to the surroundings of the city; according to the TDV, the ruler of Moscow thereupon agreed to pay the khan an annual tax (tıyış). The real break came when Tsar Ivan IV took Kazan in 1552 and Astrakhan a few years later (the TDV's articles give differing years for Astrakhan, 1554 and 1556). Devlet Giray never accepted this loss. In 1571 he broke through the Russian line of defence along the Oka, arrived before Moscow and set the city on fire; this success earned him the title 'Taht-algan' (taker of the throne) and was also greeted with joy in Istanbul. But the tsar refused to give back Kazan and Astrakhan. In 1572 the khan set out again, and this time he suffered a heavy defeat at Molodi near Moscow. Historians debate the significance of this defeat: as Ocakli reports, Alan Fisher and Chantal Lemercier-Quelquejay think that after 1572 the khans accepted defeat in the contest for the legacy of the Golden Horde; Ocakli, however, points out that this is not entirely correct, recalling that in 1578 Mehmed II Giray sent envoys to Warsaw and Stockholm proposing a joint campaign. Indeed, in 1592 the Ottoman sultan too formally asked the tsar to return Kazan and Astrakhan. For the Russian side this century was, in the words of the TDV's article on Russia, a period that largely exhausted the country. Moscow gave its answer with defensive lines: from the mid-1630s onwards it built on the southern steppe a new line of forts and earthen ramparts centred on Belgorod.", "c1bcff1f"], "kisa": ["The raids were not only for booty: the Crimean khans regarded themselves as the legitimate heirs of the Golden Horde and wanted Kazan and Astrakhan back.", "c48b0f20"], "not": ["When reading the map: the burning of Moscow is not a territorial change. The raids did not shift the frontier; the real territorial struggle was over Kazan and Astrakhan.", "ac72422a"]},
"tartisma-kirimrus-pominki-harac":{"oneri": ["The same chestful of furs and money was recorded in Bakhchisaray as 'tribute' and in Moscow as a 'gift'.", "b81b8a9d"], "ad": ["Gift or tribute? The treasure Moscow sent the Crimean khan every year", "843daebf"], "metin": ["In the age when the Golden Horde was strong, the rulers of Eastern Europe were vassals of the khan and paid him tax. Because the Crimean khans regarded themselves as the owners of this legacy, they wanted to go on receiving the same payment from Poland, Lithuania and Moscow. According to the TDV's article on the Girays, the Crimean khans, like the khans of the Golden Horde, received from Russia and Poland an annual tax consisting of furs and money. The problem lay in the name of this payment. As Sait Ocakli explains, because Moscow did not wish to continue the old relationship of vassalage, it avoided referring to this money by the word meaning 'tribute' (haraç); instead it called it pominki, meaning 'gift', and regarded it as the price of the khan's promise to prevent his own men from raiding Russian lands. The Polish kings too, regarding themselves as independent rulers, took the same course and called it upominki (gifts). The Crimean side, for its part, although it used different words such as tıyış, ulug hazine (great treasure), vergü and pişkeş (tributary gift), always officially considered these payments a tax. In other words, the same chest was, in two capitals, the proof of two different political claims. The payments were cut off and resumed according to war and peace: according to Ocakli, in the 1640s Warsaw and Moscow hardened their stance against the Tatar raids and stopped the payments, but the Crimean historian Hacı Mehmed Senai writes that in the summer of 1649 Moscow sent the khan a larger treasure than ever. According to Johannes Remy, Russia continued this regular payment until 1700. In the Ottoman-Russian treaty signed in Istanbul that year, according to the TDV's article on Karlowitz, the Russian side's demands concerning the Crimeans' not raiding Russia and concerning the claims to tax were accepted. Today researchers also use the 17th-century tıyış registers surviving from the Khanate's archive (records of the payments coming from Russia) to understand the administrative structure of the Khanate.", "e08fcad1"], "kisa": ["The same chestful of furs and money was recorded in Bakhchisaray as 'tribute' and in Moscow as a 'gift'. The name was the very heart of the struggle over who was subject to whom.", "7a74f034"], "not": ["This debate is connected with the concept of the 'Tatar yoke' in Russian historiography: calling the payment 'tribute' meant showing Moscow as still a vassal of the heir of the Golden Horde, while calling it a 'gift' meant rejecting that old vassalage.", "9d2d359c"]},
"tartisma-kirimrus-ukrayna-kirimtatar-turk":{"oneri": ["For Moscow a threat, for the Cossacks sometimes an enemy and sometimes an ally, for the Ottomans the shield of the north, for the Crimean…", "08ba1f1d"], "ad": ["One khanate, four mirrors: the Ukrainian, Crimean Tatar and Turkish views and those of new research", "7a587ce3"], "metin": ["Ukraine: Ukrainian historiography describes the Tatar raids as a devastation as heavy as Russian historiography does; the Encyclopedia of Ukraine characterises the long rule of the Golden Horde as a catastrophe for the Ukrainian lands. But there is also a complexity in the Ukrainian account: the Ukrainian Cossacks and, after 1648, the Hetman state swung back and forth between war and alliance with the Khanate. In the 19th century the Russian historian Solov'ev portrayed Hetman Khmelnytsky as someone at the khan's command, whereas the Ukrainian Kostomarov regarded him as the khan's partner. Ocakli recalls that the Ottoman and Crimean chronicles too may have shown the khan as the master of the Cossacks and put words of allegiance into the hetman's mouth, whereas the powerful Cossacks would not readily become obedient subjects. In the Soviet period Ukrainian dissidents (such as Grigorenko and Chornovil) defended the Crimean Tatars' right to return to their homeland. Crimean Tatar: the Crimean Tatars regard themselves as the indigenous people of the peninsula and trace their ancestry not only to the Mongols but also to the Circassian, Gothic, Greek, Italian and Armenian communities that had lived in the Crimea earlier. Williams supports this view: the greater part of the Khanate's population was a settled people living in the villages and towns of the south and earning a living from viticulture and trade; the 'plunderer' image fits only the Nogais of the steppe. Turkish: in Turkish historiography, particularly in the works of Halil İnalcık, the Khanate is seen as the Ottomans' northern shield and an actor in the great struggle waged with Moscow over the legacy of the Golden Horde; according to the TDV, Ottoman protection shielded the Khanate from internal strife and from Moscow's expansion. New research: according to Ocakli's finding, although the Crimean Khanate was even in the 17th century a formidable diplomatic and military power vis-à-vis Poland-Lithuania and Moscow, it did not receive the attention it deserved in Ukrainian, Polish, Russian and Western historiography. In recent years, studies based on the Khanate's own correspondence and on the Ottoman archives have begun to examine it not merely as a raiding state but as a state with its own diplomacy.", "a011934b"], "kisa": ["For Moscow a threat, for the Cossacks sometimes an enemy and sometimes an ally, for the Ottomans the shield of the north, for the Crimean Tatars their own state: the history of the Khanate changes according to who writes it.", "8d9f95e2"], "not": ["A question to ask when reading a source: from which capital is the author looking as he describes the Khanate — Moscow, Warsaw, Kiev, Istanbul, or Bakhchisaray?", "c1b46342"]},
"tartisma-korfez-basra-1776-savas-ilani":{"baslik": ["Basra 1776: How did Iran take an Ottoman city without declaring war?", "146dd130"], "metin": ["① A single day conceals a long war. In the chronology the event stands at 16 April 1776, but this is the last day not of a surprise attack but of a long siege. According to Lorimer, who relies on the records of the English East India Company, the Zand forces laid siege to Basra on 7 April 1775. Under the administration of the Ottoman mütesellim (deputy governor) Süleyman Agha, the city held out for a year and surrendered on 16 April 1776. The Encyclopaedia Iranica likewise writes that the city was blockaded and forced to surrender in the year 1190 (1776). In other words, the two states were in fact at war a year before the city fell. ② Who declared war? The articles of the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam diverge on this point. According to the articles on Karim Khan Zand and on the Zands, after Sadeq Khan took Basra, the Ottoman state declared war on Iran in the same year. The article on Abdülhamid I, however, reverses the order: Karim Khan declared war on the Ottoman state over the question of the sanjak of Baban and the Baban family, then occupied Basra and plundered the vicinity of Baghdad. Lorimer also speaks of Karim Khan's declaration of war and adds that his real intentions cannot be known for certain. The two accounts are not mutually exclusive: Iran may have started the campaign, while the Ottomans may have taken the formal decision for war after the city fell. But the sources do not give this sequence day by day. ③ Shortly before the siege the two sides were at peace. When Abdülhamid I came to the throne in 1774, he sent the poet Sünbülzâde Vehbî as envoy to Karim Khan and formally recognised his rule. Relations deteriorated because of a dispute arising from the Baban family (Babanoğulları) on the Ottoman-Iranian frontier (TDV). The sources do not report in detail whether a separate, ceremonial declaration of war was made before the siege. ④ Karim Khan's justifications. The causes listed by the sources are these: the seizure by Ömer Pasha, the governor of Baghdad, of the property of Iranian pilgrims who died at Najaf and Karbala during the plague epidemic of 1773 (Iranica); complaints that Iranian pilgrims had been mistreated (Lorimer); the rivalry for influence over the Kurdish principalities on the frontier, especially Baban and Ardalan (TDV, Iranica); and the wish to avenge defeats suffered in Kurdistan, together with the calculation of controlling Oman and the Gulf trade by seizing Basra (Lorimer). In the same years the English agency at Basra was trying to boycott the Iranian ports and draw all trade to Basra (Lorimer). ⑤ Why could the Ottomans not respond at once? The state had only just emerged from the Ottoman-Russian War of 1768-1774 with the Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca. Baghdad had been devastated by the plague epidemic of 1772, and Iraq was in practice in the hands of the Mamluk pashas (TDV). According to Iranica, the Ottomans long avoided sending troops to Iraq and mobilised to recover Basra only in 1778. During the siege, Ömer Pasha, the governor of Baghdad, came to an agreement with the Imam of Oman, Ahmad b. Said, and Omani ships helped the city by holding the entrance to the Shatt al-Arab (TDV). On the other side, the ships of the Banu Ka'b tribe supported the Iranian army (Lorimer). ⑥ The outcome. According to the TDV, after the war an Ottoman force entered Iran in April-May 1777, and attacks by both sides began along the frontier. In 1778 Karim Khan made an alliance with the Russians. Basra was evacuated after Karim Khan's death on 2 March 1779, when Sadeq Khan returned to Iran to fight for the throne. According to Iranica, the city remained for a short time under the direct rule of the Muntafiq tribe and then came under Ottoman administration again. Süleyman Agha, who had defended the city, returned from Shiraz and, under the name of Büyük (the Great) Süleyman Pasha, became governor of Basra, Baghdad and Shahrizor (TDV; Lorimer).", "8c687526"], "kisa": ["Basra fell not to a night-time surprise attack but to an open siege lasting more than a year. The point on which the sources diverge is which side declared war.", "18d2aba1"], "bag": ["Significance: The three-year occupation of Basra led the Gulf trade to shift to Kuwait and brought to power Büyük Süleyman Pasha, the most powerful of the Mamluk pashas in Iraq. The episode is the most serious conflict on the Ottoman-Iranian frontier after the peace of 1746.", "63ee60bf"]},
"tartisma-korfez-basra-abadan-fav-sahiplik":{"baslik": ["Basra, Abadan and Fao: who held sway on the banks of the Shatt al-Arab, and when?", "b072ad4c"], "metin": ["① 1534-1538 · Basra: Submission. While Suleiman the Lawgiver was in Baghdad, the ruler of Basra, Rashid b. Mughamis, came and declared his submission. In 1538 he sent the keys of the city to the sultan in Edirne. Basra was left to him again on condition that the Friday sermon be read and coins struck in the sultan's name (TDV). ② 26 December 1545 · Basra: Direct rule. When Sheikh Yahya, who succeeded Rashid, turned against the Ottomans, Ayas Pasha, the beylerbey (governor-general) of Baghdad, marched by land, and the sancakbey of Mosul advanced down the Euphrates with 120 ships; Ayas Pasha entered Basra on 26 December 1545. The city became the centre of a beylerbeylik (province), and Iran too recognised this situation with the Treaty of Amasya of 1555. The Kuwaiti coast was also attached to the beylerbeylik of Basra (TDV). ③ 17th century · Basra: Local pashas. Basra remained a province subject to the Ottomans. However, under Afrasiyab Pasha, his son Ali Pasha and his grandson Hüseyin Pasha, it was governed almost independently. The governors of Baghdad brought the city under tight control again several times: Küçük Musa Pasha in 1645 and Kara Mustafa Pasha in 1667 (TDV). ④ 1690-1701 · Basra: Turmoil and Iranian occupation. After a plague epidemic, the city passed first into the hands of the Muntafiq sheikh Mani', then into those of Farajallah, the khan of Huwayza. The Iranians seized Basra in 1696. In 1701 Daltaban Mustafa Pasha, the governor of Baghdad, recovered Qurna and Basra (TDV). ⑤ 1747-1831 · Basra: The Mamluk period. Basra was governed as a mütesellimlik (deputy governorship) dependent on the Mamluk pashas in Baghdad (TDV). ⑥ 1775-1779 · Basra: Zand siege and occupation. Iranian forces besieged the city on 7 April 1775; it surrendered on 16 April 1776 (Lorimer). In the spring of 1779 the Iranians withdrew, and after a short period of Muntafiq rule Ottoman administration returned (Iranica; Lorimer). The sources describe this occupation as limited to the city; they give no separate record for Fao. ⑦ Abadan: Whose island? The TDV article on Abadan writes that the island remained under Ottoman sovereignty for a long time. The Encyclopaedia Iranica, however, says that from the 17th century the island was the territory of the Arab Ka'b tribe. According to the TDV article on Khuzestan, the Arab tribes along the frontier paid taxes sometimes to the Ottomans and sometimes to Iran. According to the same article, when Iran besieged Herat in 1837, the Ottomans seized Muhammara and Abadan. Lorimer, for his part, records for 1837 that Muhammara was taken by the Turks and then evacuated again. In short, Ottoman sovereignty over Abadan was for the most part an indirect and disputed sovereignty exercised through tribal sheikhs. ⑧ May 1847 · Treaty of Erzurum. In the Second Treaty of Erzurum, signed under British and Russian pressure, the Shatt al-Arab remained with the Ottomans. Muhammara, Abadan and the coast of Khuzestan were left to the Qajars (TDV). The convention signed between the Ottomans and Britain in 1913 left the whole of the Shatt al-Arab, apart from Muhammara, under Ottoman sovereignty. But it could not be put into effect because war broke out (TDV). ⑨ 6 November 1914 · Fao. On the morning after the day on which the Entente powers declared war on the Ottomans, British-Indian forces seized Fao, at the mouth of the Shatt al-Arab (TDV; Moberly). In the 19th century Fao was a station of the telegraph line to India (TDV). ⑩ 21-23 November 1914 · Basra. When the Ottoman forces evacuated the city, the British reached Basra on 21 November. The TDV articles on Basra, Kuwait and Iraq give 22 November as the date of occupation. The ceremonial entry and the reading of the proclamation took place on 23 November (Moberly); the TDV article on the First World War also gives 23 November. ⑪ 9 December 1914 · Qurna. Qurna, at the confluence of the Tigris and the Euphrates, surrendered together with the forces of Subhi Bey, the governor of Basra (Moberly). ⑫ 1921 and after. With the establishment of Faisal's kingdom (23 August 1921), Basra and Fao became part of the state of Iraq. Abadan remained in Iran and grew thanks to the refinery that the Anglo-Persian oil company built there in 1909-1913 (TDV).", "c4ae3af3"], "kisa": ["From 1545 to 1914, apart from two brief occupations, Basra and Fao were under Ottoman administration. In Abadan, Ottoman sovereignty was mostly exercised through the tribe and was disputed; the island was left to Iran in 1847.", "ac13314b"], "bag": ["Significance: For four hundred years the two banks of the Shatt al-Arab were not in the hands of the same state. The western bank (Basra, Fao) was the Ottomans' gateway to the Gulf. The eastern bank (Abadan, Muhammara) was a land of the Ka'b tribe, whose loyalty moved back and forth between the Ottomans and Iran. This division, drawn in 1847, became the basis of the Iraqi-Iranian border dispute in the 20th century.", "12723063"]},
"tartisma-korfez-kuveyt-kimin":{"baslik": ["Whose was Kuwait? An Ottoman district, or a sheikhdom under British protection?", "98e17e3e"], "metin": ["① The Ottoman account. According to the TDV, from 1545 the coast of Kuwait lay within the boundaries of the beylerbeylik (province) of Basra. In the 18th century the city became the centre of a kaza (district), and the kaymakams were appointed from among the Sabah sheikhs. Under Midhat Pasha's governorship of Baghdad (1869-1872) the ties grew stronger. The kaymakam, Sheikh Abdullah al-Sabah, met Midhat Pasha at Fao during the Najd expedition of 1871 and joined the campaign with a fleet of eighty ships; his brother Mubarak also took part with the land forces (TDV). In Midhat Pasha's arrangement, Kuwait became a kaza dependent on Basra (TDV). ② The British account. The British official history of the war tells the same events differently. According to Moberly, the sheikh of Kuwait who helped Midhat Pasha's Najd expedition in 1871 was at that time independent. In return he had received land at Fao and an annual allowance; the Ottomans regarded these as proof of their suzerainty over Kuwait. Lorimer's gazetteer, on the other hand, records Kuwait for 1775 as a place dependent on Basra. ③ Mubarak and the secret agreement (1896-1901). Sheikh Muhammad was killed in 1896, and his brother Mubarak became sheikh. The Sublime Porte confirmed his office of kaymakam in 1897. In the face of Russian moves for a coaling station and German moves for the Baghdad railway, a representative sent by Lord Curzon, the Viceroy of India, signed a secret agreement with Mubarak on 23 January 1899. Under it, the sheikh was not to receive the representative of any state without Britain's permission. Mubarak denied this agreement and gave the Ottomans a document declaring his loyalty. On 20 May 1900 he was made a pasha with the rank of mirimiran. However, he did not receive the delegation of the Baghdad Railway Company (TDV). ④ The status quo (1901). On 24 August 1901, in the harbour of Kuwait, a British captain told the captain of an Ottoman ship that Kuwait was under British protection. Thereupon, on 11 September 1901, the two states agreed by an exchange of notes on the status quo: Britain would not occupy Kuwait or take it under its protection, and the Ottomans would not send troops there (TDV). ⑤ The convention of 1913. Hakkı Pasha and Edward Grey signed five conventions in London on 29 July 1913. Under one of these, Kuwait was to be a kaza belonging to the Ottoman state with administrative autonomy, whose kaymakam would be appointed by the sultan. The Ottomans, for their part, would not interfere in the city's internal affairs or send troops. The convention could not come into force because of the war (TDV). ⑥ 1914 and after. When Britain took Basra on 22 November 1914, it declared that Kuwait was under its own protection (TDV). According to Moberly, when the war broke out the sheikh of Kuwait was already openly under British protection. Salim, who was made sheikh after Mubarak's death, announced that he did not recognise the agreements made with the British and tried to mend relations with the Ottomans; Britain, for its part, imposed an embargo on Kuwait (TDV). ⑦ A trace reaching to the present. Kuwait became independent on 19 June 1961. Iraq did not recognise this independence, claiming that Kuwait had once been a district of the vilayet of Basra; it recognised it, however, in October 1963. On 2 August 1990 Iraq occupied Kuwait and declared it its nineteenth province (TDV).", "9227581a"], "kisa": ["Ottoman sources see Kuwait as a district of the vilayet of Basra and its sheikh as a kaymakam. The British official history describes the same sheikh as an independent ruler. Both accounts rest on documents.", "6634f48d"], "bag": ["Significance: The same documents tell two different stories. What is, for the Ottomans, the obedience of a kaymakam is, for Britain, an agreement made with an independent ruler. This distinction lies at the root of Kuwait's border and sovereignty disputes in the 20th century.", "c2bb62fb"]},
"tartisma-korfez-petrol-oncesi-ekonomi":{"baslik": ["Before oil, what did Kuwait, Qatar and Bahrain live on? Were they poor places?", "1f27b03a"], "metin": ["① Pearls: the region's only large source of income. Lorimer's gazetteer of the Gulf, prepared for the British Government of India at the beginning of the 20th century, describes pearl fishing as the premier industry of the Gulf and as the principal, or even the only, source of wealth of those living on the Arab coast. According to the same work, if the pearls were to give out, the trade of Kuwait would be severely damaged and that of Bahrain would fall to a fifth. The value of the pearls exported from the Gulf was estimated at 400,000 pounds sterling in 1866; by 1905-1906 it had risen, on the lowest estimate, to 1,434,399 pounds sterling. About 4,500 boats and over 74,000 people were engaged in the industry (Lorimer). The richest pearl beds were off the Arab coast, especially to the north and east of Bahrain and around the Qatar peninsula (Lorimer); the TDV, too, counts the high-quality pearls of Bahrain's shallow waters among the reasons why the island has been inhabited since ancient times. ② Who held the wealth? The season's expenses of a pearling boat were either met by its owners or borrowed from financiers called musaqqam. The musaqqams sometimes obtained this money from rich Arab and Indian merchants at 10-25 per cent interest. At the end of the season the nakhoda (captain) was obliged to hand over all the pearls to the musaqqam to whom he was in debt, at 15-20 per cent below market value. Of what remained, the boat owner took a fifth, the cost of provisions was deducted, and the rest was divided in shares among the divers and the rope haulers (Lorimer). ③ The divers' debt. The divers were mostly poor Arabs, freed or enslaved Africans, Iranians and Baluchis. Most of them were in debt because of the advances they took from the nakhodas; there were divers whose debt reached 3,000 rupees. A diver who had not paid his debt could not leave his nakhoda; a new nakhoda who hired him had to take over the old debt. These rules were applied in each sheikhdom by a board called the ‘diving court’ (Lorimer). The divers suffered from respiratory diseases, ruptured eardrums and skin diseases; the physician of the American mission hospital in Bahrain had recorded these observations (Lorimer). ④ Fish, boats, dates and trade. According to the TDV, the people of Kuwait lived by pearl fishing, fishing, boatbuilding and, to a limited extent, trade; in Qatar, besides Bedouin pastoralism, there were semi-settled tribes engaged in fishing, pearl diving and trade; Bahrain had rich date groves. Kuwait was also a deep-water port that the trade between Istanbul, Aleppo, Baghdad and Basra on the one hand and India on the other used instead of Basra; it had become the largest port and entrepôt of north-eastern Arabia. In the 1760s there were some 800 boats in its harbour (TDV). After the Utub took Bahrain in 1783, the Arabian trade running by way of Muscat also became linked to Bahrain (TDV). ⑤ Scarce resources. The soil was unsuited to agriculture; in Kuwait there was no perennial water or spring, and drinking water was drawn from slightly brackish wells or brought by ship from Basra (TDV). The population was also small: about 35,000 people lived in Kuwait in 1910, and even in the 1920s the city was, with a population of 40-50,000, in the words of the TDV, ‘a backward region’. In the region trade too, after pearls, rested largely on rain-fed agriculture (Lorimer). ⑥ Collapse and oil. From the 1930s onwards pearl fishing lost its importance because of the economic depression in Europe, its main customer, and competition from Japan (TDV). Oil came immediately after this collapse: in Kuwait the first exploration concession was granted in 1934, oil was found in 1936 and exports began in 1946 (TDV). ⑦ The short answer. According to the sources, this coast was not poor, but its wealth was tied to a single commodity, concentrated in a narrow group and fragile. Pearls produced export wealth; this wealth remained with the merchants and financiers, while the divers who brought it out of the water lived in debt. When the pearl market collapsed, the region had no other source of income.", "846324c0"], "kisa": ["The coast produced the pearl, the Gulf's most valuable export; but this wealth was concentrated in a narrow class of merchants and financiers, while the divers lived bound by debt. Real poverty came in the 1930s, when pearls had collapsed and oil was not yet bringing in money.", "85a563b1"], "bag": ["Significance: The pre-oil economy of the Gulf emirates explains why Kuwait rose as a city of trade and the sea, and why oil concessions were granted so easily in the 1930s.", "2f8f67b6"]},
"tartisma-kucuk-kaynarca-rivayetleri":{"baslik": ["Three stories told about Küçük Kaynarca — and the answer of the sources", "854b770e"], "metin": ["① 'The comfort of an elbow': It is told that the second delegate, İbrahim Münib Efendi, dozed off during the talks leaning on his elbow; that on waking and seeing everyone looking at him, he said, as though he had been following the discussion, 'let us come to the matter of the indemnity'; and that he thereby cost the state 4.5 million rubles in an indemnity the Russians had already given up. According to TDV, this has no basis beyond being an invented tale: the Russians never gave up the indemnity and had demanded 50,000 purses at Bucharest; the grand vizier had authorised the chief plenipotentiary to accept a demand of up to 20,000 purses without prolonging the matter and, if necessary, to go up to 40,000 purses. Since the indemnity was a separate article left to the very end, it was natural that it should be raised last.\n\n② 'Revenge for the Pruth': It is asserted that although the treaty was ready on 17 July, the Russians held it back until 21 July, the day on which the Treaty of the Pruth had been signed (Hammer, Zinkeisen, Jorga, Danişmend). According to TDV, the grand vizier's approval arrived on 21 July and the treaty was signed the same day; moreover, the Treaty of the Pruth too had been signed not on 21 but on 22 July and exchanged on the 23rd.\n\n③ 'In memory of Weissmann': The claim that the peace was made at Küçük Kaynarca in memory of the Russian general Weissmann, who had died there a year earlier, also appears only in Hammer; according to TDV, Rumyantsev's route and the fact that the peace could not have been known for certain in advance make such an intention implausible.\n\nNor is the account true that the treaty was 'dictated in a brief meeting of four to seven hours': talks were held for hours on three separate days, and the understandings previously reached at Bucharest were taken as the basis.", "71d21aab"], "kisa": ["A dozing delegate who brought up the indemnity, a date awaited to avenge the Pruth, a village chosen in memory of a general… TDV finds all three unfounded.", "51583f1f"], "not": ["The date of the treaty is also given differently in the sources: archival copies take 16 July, when the talks began, some primary sources take 17 July, and the Russian side takes 26 July, when the texts were exchanged. The date of signature is 21 July 1774.", "a379beab"], "bag": ["All three stories serve the same function: attributing a heavy defeat to an individual's lapse or to the enemy's symbolic calculation. TDV's objection, by contrast, rests on archival documents and contemporary Ottoman sources (Enveri, Vasıf, Ahmed Resmi).", "df4b9abe"]},
"tartisma-osman-gazi-olum-yili":{"baslik": ["Did Osman Gazi see the conquest of Bursa? — the debate over the year of his death", "ed71678c"], "metin": ["The TDV article on Osman I derives the year of his death not from the narrative of the chronicles but from a comparison of two documents. In the endowment deed (vakfiye) of Asporça Hatun, drawn up at the beginning of September 1323, Osman is alive; in the Mekece endowment deed dated 1324, he now appears to be dead. His death is therefore placed in the year 724 (1324). The same article also relates the Ottoman tradition: in that account, when Osman died Orhan was engaged in the siege of Bursa — and Bursa did not surrender until 6 April 1326. The chronicles write that because of the gout in his foot Osman withdrew, leaving affairs to Orhan, and that after 1305 his name no longer appears in the campaigns. The TDV article on Orhan goes one step further: it says that from the testimony of Ibn Battuta one may conclude that Osman's actual rule as bey ended around 1322. In accordance with his will, Osman was buried in the citadel of Bursa beneath the dome of the old monastery called Gümüşlükubbe (the Silver Dome); when this building collapsed in the earthquake of 1855, the present plain tomb was built by Sultan Abdülaziz in 1863.", "3649e4a7"], "kisa": ["The chronicles have him die while his son was before Bursa; two endowment deeds, however, show that he died two years before the city fell.", "ec818ca5"], "not": ["The detail that news of the conquest was brought to Osman on his deathbed does not appear in the TDV articles on Osman I, Orhan and Bursa; these articles relate only the tradition that \"when he died, Orhan was besieging Bursa\".", "a5d09f80"], "bag": ["The two accounts are not measuring the same thing. The endowment deeds provide a documentary date and support 1324; linking the death to the siege of Bursa comes from the chronicle tradition. For a bey who had fallen ill and long since left affairs to his son, to say \"he saw Bursa\" and to say \"he died without seeing it\" are each separate claims. The two entries in the chronology carry this distinction side by side: the entry on his death gives 1324, while the entry on the conquest of Bursa gives the tradition.", "f43b5e36"]},
"tartisma-otuzbir-mart-1909":{"baslik": ["The 31 March Incident (1909): who was against the constitutional regime?", "dbac8f47"], "metin": ["The uprising that broke out in Istanbul on 31 March 1325 in the Rumi calendar, 13 April 1909 in the Gregorian, took place only nine months after the proclamation of the constitutional regime. The answer to the question \"who was against it and why\" is not a single group; it is several clusters that were disturbed at the same time for very different reasons.\n\n■ THE FIRST CLUSTER: THE PURGED OFFICERS\nIn the army there was an old conflict between the ranker officers (alaylı, trained not in a military school but in the barracks) and the school-trained officers (mektepli). When a purge of the rankers was undertaken after the constitutional restoration, a group who had spent their years in the army suddenly found themselves without a profession. The complaint of this cluster concerned not the constitution but their own future.\n\n■ THE SECOND CLUSTER: THOSE WHO LOST THEIR PLACE\nThe cadres of the era of Abdulhamid II had lost their privileges. In a change of regime, the group that organizes fastest is often the group that loses the most.\n\n■ THE THIRD CLUSTER: THE POLITICAL OPPOSITION\nThe interference of the Committee of Union and Progress in the affairs of government and its repressive attitude provoked a reaction even among groups that supported the constitutional regime. The circle of the Ahrar (Liberal) Party and conservative groups were the political expression of this reaction. That is, some of those who rose up were not against the constitutional regime but against the way those who had brought it were using power; confusing these two is the shortest way to misread the event.\n\n■ THE FOURTH CLUSTER: THE RELIGIOUS DEMAND\nDerviş Vahdeti and the circle of the İttihad-ı Muhammedi (Muhammadan Union) Society joined the event with a demand for sharia.\n\n■ THE FUSE\nOn 7 April 1909 the murder of the journalist Hasan Fehmi by an unidentified assailant heightened tension. On the night of 12-13 April, soldiers of the 4th Rifle (Avcı) Battalion rose up with a demand for sharia. Some three to four thousand rebels carrying white, green and red flags gathered in Hagia Sophia Square and occupied the Chamber of Deputies. Among their demands were the appointment of Kamil Pasha as grand vizier and the abolition of the Committee of Union and Progress.\n\n■ ITS SUPPRESSION\nThe Action Army began to arrive from 19 April onward and on 24 April took complete control of the city. On 27 April 1909 Abdulhamid II was deposed and Mehmed Reşad succeeded him. Many rebels, including Derviş Vahdeti, were executed.\n\n■ WHERE THE DEBATE LIES\nThe sultan's role in the incident is one of the most debated questions of this era, and here the sources differ sharply. The claim that the person who lost his throne as a result of the uprising had himself organized that same uprising has been read differently by its supporters and its opponents down to the present day. No judgment has been passed in this card: it is certain that he was deposed after the suppression of the uprising, but his share in bringing about the uprising is not certain.", "d28f5395"], "kisa": ["Who was against the constitutional regime? The answer is more complicated than saying \"the reactionaries.\"", "1d5bb0c5"]},
"tartisma-p75b-cerkes-surgunu-1864":{"baslik": ["The Circassian deportation and migration to Anatolia: the concepts of 'migration', 'deportation' and 'genocide'", "81117598"], "metin": ["① The facts (TDV 'Circassians'). The Circassians, who with the help of the allied fleet had seized part of the Taman peninsula during the Crimean War and who went on fighting even after Shamil's surrender (1859), met at Sochi on 13 June 1861 and asked the Ottoman Empire, Britain and France for help. In 1861-1864 the Russians occupied the lands of the Abzakhs, Shapsugs and Ubykhs; the people were informed that unless within a month they settled in the places to be shown to them along the Kuban or emigrated to Ottoman territory, they would be deported to the interior of Russia as prisoners of war. The deportation of the Circassians, which the Russians had been planning since 1857 and which was officially decided in 1861 with the establishment of the Caucasus Commission and the tsar's approval, is according to TDV 'in the view of many researchers, the first act of ethnic cleansing in modern history'. The number of those deported to and migrating to Ottoman territory between 1858 and 1878 is given as 1,200,000 to 1,500,000. The TDV article 'Abazas' writes that almost all the Abazas living between Anapa and Gagra were deported in 1862-1865 and that the Russians forcibly deported some 45-50,000 Abazas, and adds that no clear statistical data exist on those who migrated to Ottoman territory.\n\n② Settlement in Anatolia (TDV 'Circassians'; Özkiraz–Çetin 2015). On 16 January 1860 the Ottoman Empire established the Refugee Commission (Muhacirin Komisyonu); attached to this commission, which lasted until 19 March 1875, were the Anatolian, Rumelian and Syrian settlement commissions. The Circassians were settled in Istanbul, Rumelia, Anatolia and Syria; those forced to leave Rumelia after the Treaty of Berlin (1878) were sent to Anatolia, Syria and Iraq; migration from the Caucasus continued until the 1910s. According to Özkiraz–Çetin, the cities in Anatolia where Circassian settlement was densest were Tokat, Balıkesir, Eskişehir, Samsun, Kayseri, Sivas and Bursa; until their final places of settlement were determined the migrants struggled with hunger and epidemic diseases, and they both experienced great problems and caused problems with the local people; an aid society in London sent hardtack to Trebizond, and the state and the people, including Ottoman princes, gave assistance.\n\n③ The debate over concepts. A question that comes to mind: was this 'migration', 'deportation' or 'genocide'? The sources use different words: TDV uses 'deportation' and 'ethnic cleansing' (attributing the latter to many researchers); Özkiraz–Çetin use the heading 'deportation and genocide' (the English abstract of the article says 'deportation and massacres'); Bolat (an author who states that he is a member of the board of the Istanbul Circassian Association) argues in his article that the term 'migration' is not enough to describe what happened and assesses the concepts of 'deportation' and 'genocide'; Walter Richmond's monograph 'The Circassian Genocide' (Rutgers UP, 2013) uses the term in its title. This shows that the concept occurs in academic writing; no legal-history assessment of whether the concept can be applied in its legal sense, and no official position of Russia, was found in the sources read. On this card 'genocide' is mentioned not as a verdict but as 'a term used by some academic studies'.\n\n④ Interpretation (an assessment arising from reading the sources side by side, not a definitive verdict). The word chosen for the same facts (forced departure, deaths, settlement) changes with the position of the narrator: Ottoman state documents and TDV use the language of 'muhacir (refugees)/settlement', while authors close to Circassian associations use the language of 'deportation and genocide'. The reader can see that both languages describe the same facts.", "6e340639"], "kisa": ["TDV calls the Circassian deportation, decided in 1861 and reaching its peak in 1864, 'in the view of many researchers, the first act of ethnic cleansing in modern history'; the term 'genocide', for its part, is used in academic writing but is disputed, and Russia's official position is not in the sources read.", "e1b1ca36"], "bag": ["Significance: this deportation, which concerns the roots of hundreds of thousands of people in present-day Turkey, is one of the largest population movements of the Ottoman Empire's last century and the result of the Caucasus question between Russia and the Ottomans.", "5e444be4"]},
"tartisma-p75b-donanma-yakilmaya-musait-mi":{"baslik": ["Sinop, Çeşme, Navarino, Lepanto: is there a common cause that burned the Ottoman fleet four times?", "08b9d151"], "metin": ["① The question. A question that comes to mind: Sinop (1853), Çeşme (1770), Navarino (1827) and Lepanto (İnebahtı) (1571) — four times the Ottoman fleet was burned or destroyed by an enemy fleet; was there a common weakness? The condition set by the specification: any verdict must be sourced. So first the cause of each event as given in the sources was written out separately, then what they had in common was sought, and what was found was divided into 'stated in the source' and 'this page's interpretation'.\n\n② Çeşme (5-7 July 1770). TDV Çeşme Incident (M. Münir Aktepe): in the battle in open water, when the ship of Cezayirli Hasan Bey and the galleon of Admiral Spiridov began to burn, the ships that feared the fire would spread moved away from the battle area; then the fleet, despite the opposition of Cezayirli Hasan Bey, was taken by the Kapudan Pasha (Grand Admiral) Hüsameddin Pasha and Cafer Bey into 'a place with no possibility of manoeuvre', inside Çeşme Harbour. The Russians closed the mouth of the harbour, sent in fire boats and set alight some thirty ships anchored very close to one another; about 5000 soldiers died. Cause: a command decision (taking refuge in a narrow harbour) and, according to the TDV article 'Bahriye' (Navy), a navy that had not seen war for fifty years between 1717 and 1770 and had 'not been brought up to the desired level' at a time when seamanship in Europe was advancing along scientific lines.\n\n③ Navarino (20 October 1827). TDV Navarino (İdris Bostan): the Ottoman-Egyptian fleet was in Navarino harbour; the British, French and Russian allied fleets entered the harbour and mounted a 'surprise attack'; TDV stresses that there was no state of war between the states and gives the loss as 52 ships and 6000 sailors (37 ships belonged to the central Ottoman fleet, the rest to the Egyptian fleet). TDV 'Bahriye': it was 'burned in Navarino Harbour without a declaration of war'; this disaster destroyed the strength and knowledge that the Turkish navy had accumulated since Selim III, and it was the last battle of sailing ships. Cause: political — an intervention made although there was no state of war — and the presence of the fleet in harbour.\n\n④ Sinop (30 November 1853). Özcan: the squadron, sheltering from a storm, was anchored in a harbour protected by five batteries; there was no lookout ship outside the harbour, and the commander had not considered a surprise attack likely; the Russians had heavy ships of the line with shell guns, while the Ottomans faced them with light vessels, frigates and steamers. The Naval Council had already decided that the squadron should be withdrawn to Istanbul, but the decision could not be carried out. Cause: an imbalance of forces, unpreparedness and the storm; Özcan also advances a thesis that the British ambassador had the squadron made up as a light one (the author's thesis, see `savas-p75b-sinop-1853`).\n\n⑤ Lepanto (7 October 1571). TDV Battle of Lepanto (İdris Bostan): the fleet was tired from a six-month campaign and short of oarsmen and fighting men; at the council of war Uluç Ali Pasha proposed waiting in the gulf and Pertev Pasha agreed, while the Kapudan Pasha Müezzinzade Ali Pasha, citing the order he had received, wanted to fight. TDV: the main difference between the two fleets was that the Ottoman fleet was tired and the allied fleet fresh. The article 'Bahriye' writes that the ships that dealt the Turks a heavy blow in this battle were high-sided, thirty-six-gun 'gali's (galleasses), and that the battle is regarded as the end of the age of the oar and the beginning of the age of sail. The loss is given as 200-250 ships in the TDV article `inebahti` and as 190 ships (sunk or captured) in the article `inebahti-deniz-savasi` — there is a difference between the two TDV articles. Cause: a decision to give pitched Battle; not an attack on a harbour.\n\n⑥ Common elements stated in the sources. The TDV article 'Bahriye' does not give a 'cause of the four disasters' in a single sentence, but it offers two recurring explanations: (a) backwardness in technology and organisation — the defeat of the oared galley by high-sided ships (Lepanto), the failure to keep up with the scientific progress of seamanship in Europe (Çeşme), Navarino being 'the last battle of sailing ships', and at Sinop wooden ships against Russian shell guns (Özcan); (b) administration: the entrusting of sea power, 'after Barbarossa, Turgut Reis and the seamen they trained', to kapudan pashas with no knowledge of seamanship ('Bahriye', for the seventeenth century). But in the same article TDV also writes that the fleet was rebuilt after Lepanto, that Turkish seamanship reached its highest level in the time of Kılıç Ali Pasha ('Bahriye'), and that after Çeşme Cezayirli Gazi Hasan Pasha opened the Naval School: that is, there was a recovery after every disaster, and the narrative of 'a single continuing decay' does not match the source's own narrative.\n\n⑦ Interpretation (an assessment arising from reading the sources side by side, not a definitive verdict). When the four events are set side by side, a distinction appears: at Çeşme, Navarino and Sinop the fleet was struck while anchored in a harbour (at Çeşme it had gone in by its own decision); at Lepanto, however, the two fleets met in the open. So the question of being 'ripe for burning' can be asked of three of them only as 'a wooden fleet at anchor'; nor is this a situation peculiar to the Ottomans: at Aboukir (1 August 1798), of 13 French ships of the line anchored in the bay, only two managed to escape, with two frigates (card `deniz-ebukir-1798`: TDV Ebûkīr, Royal Museums Greenwich). The Ottoman fleet also has victories it won at sea (`savas-preveze-1538`, `savas-cerbe-1560`, `dunya-donanmanin-buyuk-zaferleri`).\n\n⑧ Verdict: none of the sources read gives a single cause for the four events; no common cause is shown. The causes of the four events are separate (a command decision, intervention without a declaration of war, unpreparedness and imbalance of forces, fatigue and the order received); the only shared aspects are backwardness in technology/organisation and the defencelessness of a fleet at anchor, and neither is stated in the sources for all four events.", "9342868f"], "kisa": ["No source was found that ties the four events to a single cause in answer to the question history enthusiasts often ask, 'why was this fleet so easy to burn?' The sources list different causes for each event; in three the fleet was struck while anchored in harbour, and the fourth was an open pitched battle.", "dcf87c11"], "bag": ["Significance: a sourced test of the frequently made generalisation about Ottoman naval history that 'the fleet was always getting burned' — the result is not a single cause but four separate stories.", "bd06982b"]},
"tartisma-p75b-husrev-donanma-rusya-vehmi":{"baslik": ["Would Koca Hüsrev Pasha have wanted to give the fleet to the Russians? Two sides of a suspicion", "cc6ef9d8"], "metin": ["① The source of the question. The chronology entry writes that Ahmed Fevzi Pasha acted 'on the suspicion that Koca Hüsrev Pasha would give the fleet to Russia'. According to Dönmez, this suspicion appears as follows in Avidick's report: Hüsrev and Halil Pasha have poisoned the sultan and intend to hand the fleet over to Russia. The rumour was being spread by Osman Bey, of Fevzi Pasha's close circle, who also told it to Admiral Lalande in this form. A question that comes to mind: how well founded was this suspicion?\n\n② Facts that strengthen the possibility. (a) When the Egyptian army advanced towards Istanbul, help was requested from the Russians (March 1833); the TDV article on Hüsrev Pasha records that on the day the sultan visited the Russian fleet anchored at Büyükdere, he spent the night at Hüsrev Pasha's waterside mansion at Emirgân and showed him great favour — the presence of the Russian fleet in Istanbul coincides with the period when Hüsrev Pasha held influence. (b) He took part in the negotiations of the Treaty of Hünkâr İskelesi, and the first meeting was held at his waterside mansion (TDV). (c) In 1829, after the Peace of Adrianople, the Russian ambassador Orlov wished to send him, in the name of the tsar, a jewelled dagger and a fur, and the sultan prevented him from accepting them (TDV Hüsrev Pasha) — that is, Russian interest had been visible earlier too. (d) Dönmez writes that Hüsrev, Âkif and Fevzi Pasha were all three regarded by Britain as pro-Russian because they had taken part in Hünkâr İskelesi.\n\n③ Facts that weaken the possibility. (a) The TDV writes that Hüsrev Pasha did not favour a conflict with Russia because the new army was not yet strong enough, and that he advocated a policy of peace until the Russians attacked — this is caution rather than pro-Russian leaning. (b) According to information that reached the British embassy in April 1839, cited in one of Dönmez's footnotes, the strongest Russian partisan at the Sublime Porte was Fevzi Pasha himself ('The Kapudan Pasha is the head of the systematic Russian party'); that is, in British eyes at the time, the person making the accusation was more pro-Russian than the target of the accusation. (c) The consuls of the European states at Alexandria told Mehmed Ali that Fevzi Pasha's claims that 'the sultan has been poisoned and Hüsrev has come to an agreement with Russia' had not yet been established as certain. (d) According to Dönmez's summary, Admiral Lalande had accepted these claims without investigating their truth; the Ottoman Minister of Foreign Affairs, Reşid Pasha, and the French ambassador also saw that what Avidick related did not agree with what the admiral related. (e) Palmerston ordered Stopford, the British commander in the Mediterranean, to explain to the Ottoman officers and sailors that Fevzi Pasha had misled them with the words 'if you go to Istanbul, the fleet will fall into Russian hands', and to warn them that if they did not return, it was precisely then that the sultan would fall into Russian hands (Dönmez) — that is, the British government saw the Russian danger not in the fleet's returning but in its not returning. (f) Hüsrev Pasha later did all he could to get the fleet back (a letter to Mustafa Pasha; the plan with two fermans carried by Muhsin Efendi, Dönmez footnote 31); that is, he wanted the fleet to return not to Russia but to the Sublime Porte.\n\n④ What was not found. In none of the sources consulted is there a document or testimony that Hüsrev Pasha made any attempt to transfer the fleet to Russia. Nor was any source read that confirms the claim that the sultan was poisoned. On the other hand, no Russian archival source was read either on whether Russia wanted such a transfer in July 1839.\n\n⑤ Interpretation (this is an assessment drawn from reading the sources side by side, not a definitive verdict). It is possible to look at the function of the rumour: in his letter reported by Dönmez, Mehmed Ali had developed the argument that Fevzi Pasha and the commanders 'were not traitors but had fled from the wickedness of Hüsrev Pasha'; the rumour served both Fevzi Pasha's presentation of his own act as a move of rescue and Mehmed Ali's justification for keeping hold of the fleet. This is a conjecture; it is not written directly in the source.\n\n⑥ No verdict has been given. The information at hand shows that the possibility is 'neither entirely groundless nor documented': Hüsrev Pasha's Russian connections were real, but the link that draws from these connections the conclusion of 'giving the fleet to Russia' can be established in the sources only through what was said by Fevzi Pasha's circle.", "2d311fd7"], "kisa": ["The justification for Fevzi Pasha's taking the fleet to Egypt was the rumour that Hüsrev Pasha had poisoned the sultan and would hand the state and the fleet over to Russia. Sources that support and weaken this possibility are set side by side; no verdict is given.", "d99e3704"], "bag": ["Significance: the justification for the Ottoman fleet's passing into the hands of a rebel governor was a rumour; the degree of truth in that rumour lies at the centre of the question whether the event was 'betrayal' or 'fear and factional conflict'.", "8519a02b"]},
"tartisma-p75b-kirim-ittifak-kultur-borc":{"baslik": ["What did the Crimean War leave to the Ottomans? The Western alliance, city life and foreign debt", "741c1847"], "metin": ["① The alliance. According to the TDV article on Abdülmecid, in this war Britain, France and Piedmont took the side of the Ottoman State; in return, the allies, who helped with their armies, obtained from Abdülmecid a reform edict confirming the Tanzimat Edict (the Reform Edict of 1856, 18 February 1856). The Treaty of Paris (30 March 1856) placed the integrity and independence of the Ottoman State under the joint guarantee of the signatory powers, and neutralised and demilitarised the Black Sea (TDV Tanzimat). The same articles also describe a second side of the alliance: the TDV article on the Treaty of Paris writes that the Sublime Porte approached the allies with caution and reacted against the rights of the non-Muslim population being placed under the joint guarantee of Europe; TDV Tanzimat adds that the reference to the edict in Article 9 of the treaty put the state under an obligation and prepared the ground for inviting foreign intervention. The question that comes to mind is this: was this alliance support or tutelage? The TDV article on the Eastern Question writes that the war's ending in Russia's defeat shows how important the support of France, Britain and Austria was; the TDV article on Hüsrev Pasha, however, says that as early as 1839, with the joint note of the five powers, the Ottoman State 'in a sense came under the tutelage of Europe'.\n\n② The city and daily life. The TDV article on the Tanzimat lists the concrete traces the war left in Istanbul: the concentration of population caused by the allied armies exposed the city's infrastructure problems, and on 25 July 1855 the municipality (şehremaneti) was established; the telegraph, invented in 1837, entered Ottoman lands during the war (the Istanbul-Edirne-Shumen (Şumnu) line was opened in 1855, the British built the Varna-Crimea and Varna-Istanbul lines, and on 29 March 1855 a telegraph directorate was established); the Meclis-i Âlî-i Tanzîmat (High Council of Reforms) was established on 26 September 1854, and the second book of the French Commercial Code was put into force under the name of the Bankruptcy Law. The TDV article on Abdülmecid writes that the sultan began on the eve of the Crimean War to accept the custom of meeting foreign ambassadors directly, and TDV Tanzimat that after the war he attended the balls given at the embassies. The Selimiye Barracks was given to British soldiers and turned into a hospital (see `kimdir-p75b-florence-nightingale`). Claims of 'cultural change' concerning dress, entertainment, life in Beyoğlu and the like were not found in this round linked directly to the Crimean War in the TDV articles consulted; for these a separate source on social history is needed.\n\n③ Foreign debt. TDV Abdülmecid: to meet the heavy expenses of the Crimean War, money was borrowed from abroad for the first time (24 August 1854); this was followed by a second loan in 1855, a third in 1858 and a fourth in 1860. TDV Tanzimat: the Crimean War imposed a great cost on the treasury, upset the financial balance to a degree that could never again be set right, and dragged the finances into the quagmire of foreign debt; foreign borrowing became a means of finance to which later Ottoman statesmen would resort very frequently. The question whether there was any such thing as foreign debt in the classical period is the subject of a separate card.\n\n④ Why a debate? The same war is told in two languages in the sources: the collapse of Russia's aim of establishing sole dominance over the Ottomans (Eastern Question) and the cost's dragging the finances into a quagmire of debt (Tanzimat). Which weighs more heavily is a value judgement; both have been written in the card.", "0d1f7281"], "kisa": ["The Ottoman State 'won' the war, but the TDV describes the same war both as Russia's defeat and as a burden that damaged the finances beyond repair. The alliance with the Western powers brought, along with security, grounds for intervention.", "09dc4a57"], "bag": ["Significance: the alliance that made the Ottoman State an official member of the European states system in 1856 was at the same time the beginning of the chain of foreign debt, from 1854 onwards, and of reform commitments.", "27ed22e6"]},
"tartisma-topkapi-sarayi":{"baslik": ["Topkapı Palace: little-known points and unresolved debates", "193b2052"], "metin": ["① FOR CENTURIES ITS NAME WAS NOT 'TOPKAPI'. For a long time after it was built (according to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, probably until the eighteenth century), the palace was called Sarây-ı Cedîd-i Âmire, that is, the 'New Palace', to distinguish it from the old palace in Beyazıt. The name 'Palace of the Cannon Gate' (Top Kapısı Sarayı) passed to it later. The name comes from a seaside pavilion that was called the 'Cannon Gate' (Toplu Kapı) because cannon had been placed on both sides of it.\n\n② THE STARTING DATE IS UNCERTAIN. Contemporary sources disagree with one another about when the palace was begun and finished. The firmest evidence we have is the date 883 (1478) on the Bâb-ı Hümâyun (Imperial Gate). Working backward from this date, the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam accepts that construction probably began in 1465.\n\n③ WHEN WERE THE TOWERS OF THE BÂBÜSSELÂM (GATE OF SALUTATION) BUILT? The two towers of the second gate divide researchers. According to one view, the shape of the towers was inspired by buildings that Suleiman the Lawgiver saw on a European campaign; the towers must therefore have been added when the gate was renovated. Gülru Necipoğlu, author of a comprehensive study of the palace, holds that the towers were built in the Conqueror's time together with the walls. The debate has not been settled.\n\n④ THE SULTAN COULD WATCH THE COUNCIL UNSEEN. In the wall of the hall where the Imperial Council (divan) met is a latticed window, behind which rises the Tower of Justice. The tower's door on the Harem side allowed the sultan to come here without being seen by anyone and to watch the council whenever he wished. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam links this arrangement to the practice of leaving the conduct of council meetings to the grand vizier.\n\n⑤ GAZELLES IN THE PARADE SQUARE (ALAY MEYDANI). Sixteenth-century miniatures and the accounts of members of ambassadors' retinues show that animals such as gazelles and roe deer roamed the second courtyard.\n\n⑥ THE PALACE'S POPULATION. In a record of 883 (1478) from the Conqueror's reign, the number of palace staff is about 726. Together with the Harem and those in the sultan's personal service, who are not included in this record, the palace population is thought to have exceeded 1500. On an ordinary day the kitchens could turn out meals for between 1500 and 3000 people; on days of council meetings and ambassadorial receptions the figure rose much higher.\n\n⑦ THE 'IRANIAN MASTERS' OF THE TILED KIOSK: A MISREADING. It was long written that the kiosk's tiles were the work of Iranian or Azeri masters. According to Semavi Eyice, this claim arose from misreading the word 'alâ' in the inscription as 'Alî' and the phrase 'sâye-i yezdânî' as 'şâkirdân-ı Îrânî' (Iranian apprentices), and it is entirely baseless. In Eyice's view, the claim that Atik Sinan, an architect of the Conqueror's reign, worked on the kiosk is likewise an unsupported assumption until a clear document is found. Nor does the view that the portico on the front façade was a loggia built for watching jereed games rest on any sound source.\n\n⑧ AN EXECUTION IN THE 'GLASS PALACE'. The Tiled Kiosk was formerly also known as the 'Sırça Saray' (Glass Palace); Tursun Bey likens it to a place made of glass. As reported by Eyice, Naima writes that Abaza Pasha, one of Murad IV's viziers, was imprisoned in the Sırça Saray and executed there by the sultan's order on the night of 23-24 August 1634. It is also known that toward the end of the same century aghas attached to the palace lived in the kiosk.\n\n⑨ WHY WAS THE PALACE ABANDONED? The first sultan to think of making permanent use of the waterside buildings at Dolmabahçe was Mahmud II. According to the article on Dolmabahçe Palace in the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, his wish to leave Topkapı was not due solely to the fact that his father, Selim III, had been killed in this palace. The real factor was the city's growth beyond the walls toward Pera and Beşiktaş; this growth also coincided with the process of Westernisation. The new palace that Abdülmecid began in 1842 was opened on 7 June 1856, and the seat of government moved to the shore of the Bosphorus.", "0d847f99"], "kisa": ["For centuries the palace was not called Topkapı, the date it was begun is uncertain, and it is debated to whose time the towers on its gate belong. As for the claim that Iranian masters made the Tiled Kiosk, it arose from a misreading.", "b99a26f1"], "bag": ["The entries for 1478 in the chronology mark the completion of the palace and the move into it, while the 1856 entry marks the departure from it. Between these two end points, this card distinguishes which information about the palace rests on documents (inscriptions, registers, chronicles) and which on researchers' interpretation. In this way the reader will not take the name 'Topkapı' or the story of the 'Iranian masters' for established fact.", "5e44b57b"]},
"tartisma-universite-kurulus-yili-1453-mi-1863-mu":{"baslik": ["The university's founding year: 1453 or 1863?", "f95e7761"], "metin": ["■ What the claim says\nIt is argued that higher education in Istanbul has continued as an unbroken chain since the conquest, and that the foundation can therefore be traced back to 1453. The basis is the madrasas founded immediately after the conquest and their continuity in the life of higher education in Istanbul.\n\n■ THE OTHER SIDE'S CRITERION\nThe opposing view looks at the institution's own records: the decision to establish a Darülfünun was taken in 1846, the first lecture was given on 13 January 1863, and the lasting foundation came about on 31 August 1900. These dates belong to the history not of a chain of madrasas but of a new institution on the model of the European university; indeed, the attempts at a Darülfünun were not added on to the madrasa but established as a separate structure alongside it.\n\n■ THE REAL AXIS OF THE DEBATE\nThe two sides are answering different questions.\n• The criterion of continuity: “Since when has there been higher education in this city?” — the answer goes back to the conquest.\n• The criterion of institutional identity: “When was today's university founded, with its legal personality and its system of instruction?” — the answer comes to the 19th century.\nBetween the madrasa and the Darülfünun there is not continuity but distinction with respect to curriculum, language of instruction, the system of diplomas and employment after graduation; on the other hand, both drew on the same city and, in part, on the same pool of teachers.\n\n■ WHAT COULD AND COULD NOT BE ESTABLISHED\nThe four attempts to found the Darülfünun and their dates are clearly stated in the source. By contrast, the official rationale itself for counting 1453 as the founding year was not found in the encyclopaedia articles surveyed; the document on which the claim rests has not been weighed here. What the reader can hold as certain is this: the first Darülfünun lecture was in 1863 and the lasting foundation in 1900.", "9e69569a"], "kisa": ["Can a university's founding year be 1453? The debate is in fact not a debate about history but about “what is to be called a university”.", "d6cba3aa"]},
"tartisma-voyvodaliklar-ilhak-karsilastirma":{"oneri": ["The Bulgarian, Serbian and Bosnian states were also, for a time, tribute-paying vassal states.", "666ca395"], "ad": ["Bulgaria, Serbia and Bosnia were annexed; why were Wallachia, Moldavia and Transylvania not?", "69d10b92"], "metin": ["A common beginning — According to the article Haraçgüzâr (tribute-payer) in the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, before final annexation Byzantium, the Serbian and Greek despotates, the Bulgarian Kingdom and the Albanian lords were also tribute-paying entities. That is, most of the Balkan states started out as vassals. The difference emerged in what happened when the vassal relationship broke down.\n\nBULGARIA — According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, when the Bulgarian king Shishman secretly communicated with the Hungarian king Sigismund in 1392 and took a stand against the Ottomans, it was decided to abolish the kingdom entirely. Tarnovo was taken in 1393 and the Bulgarian Kingdom came to an end.\n\nSERBIA — According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, with the capture of Smederevo in 1459 the Serbian despotate was abolished and Serbia was incorporated into the Ottoman lands. The country was organized as the sanjak of Smederevo. According to the same article, in the first land surveys the majority of the sipahis in this sanjak were Christians; the old native ruling class kept its privileges for some time longer.\n\nBOSNIA — According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, when the last king of Bosnia, relying on Western support, refused to pay tribute, this 'meant that he had ceased to be a vassal ruler', and the Conqueror completed the conquest of Bosnia in 1463.\n\nHUNGARY — Part of Hungary, too, was at first a vassal kingdom. According to the article on Transylvania in the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, János Szapolyai became king under Ottoman protection. After his death the Ottomans made Buda the seat of a beylerbeylik (governorate-general) and attached it directly to the centre, while giving Transylvania to his son. Thus in 1541 Transylvania became a tribute-paying voivodeship. Two parts of the same country had entered two different statuses.\n\nWALLACHIA and Moldavia — According to the article on Moldavia in the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, the Conqueror did not interfere in the internal affairs of this principality; the article puts it this way: 'He did not turn Moldavia, like Bulgaria and Hungary, into a province governed by an Ottoman pasha.' Even when Stephen the Great (Stefan cel Mare) withheld the tax in 1475 and defeated an Ottoman army, the response was not annexation. In 1484 Bayezid II took Kiliia (Kili) and Akkerman, regarded as the 'keys and gates' of Moldavia, and the region was thereby bound to the Ottomans. The same course was followed in later centuries: the vassal country was left in place, but key points on the coast and the frontier were taken under direct administration. Bender and the Budjak were taken in this way in 1538, and Khotyn after 1711. The second response to rebellion was to change the ruling class (the Phanariot princes).\n\nWHY? — The TDV articles do not explain this difference in a single sentence. The indications that can be read are these. First, the native ruling class and the church remained in place: the church of Moldavia was attached to the patriarchate of Istanbul, and the three nations of Transylvania kept their own churches. Second, these countries were like a wall between the Ottomans on one side and Hungary, Poland and Russia on the other: according to a saying attributed to Stephen, Kiliia and Akkerman were the wall of all Moldavia, and Moldavia was the wall of Hungary and Poland. Third, these countries paid tribute regularly; according to the TDV article Haraçgüzâr, when grain and supplies were requested for the provisioning of Istanbul or for campaigns, their price was deducted from this tribute. In other words, the centre could obtain both revenue and provisions without setting up a direct administration. This is the interpretation of this card; the sources give no verdict on the relative weight of the causes.", "dfbfa268"], "kisa": ["The Bulgarian, Serbian and Bosnian states were also, for a time, tribute-paying vassal states. But when they rebelled they were annexed. The voivodeships beyond the Danube, by contrast, remained vassals even when they rebelled; instead of turning them into provinces, the Ottomans took their key fortresses and replaced their rulers.", "0bc13c8c"], "not": ["Albania and Greece (the Morea, the Greek despotates) followed the same path as Bulgaria and Serbia: a tribute-paying beginning and then annexation (as enumerated in the TDV article Haraçgüzâr). They are not treated separately in this card. Details of the terms and of the three voivodeships' own periods are in the status cards 'Wallachia', 'Moldavia' and 'Transylvania'.", "175a43e6"]},
"tartisma-voyvodaliklar-ittifak-osmanli-topragi":{"oneri": ["Wallachia, Moldavia and Transylvania managed their own internal affairs, but in foreign affairs they were bound to Istanbul.", "3c591d82"], "ad": ["Does a voivodeship that allies itself with a foreign state count as 'Ottoman territory'?", "6df4640c"], "metin": ["Its place in Islamic law — According to the article Haraçgüzâr (tribute-payer) in the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, Christian principalities and states that accepted Ottoman sovereignty and paid a fixed tax every year held a special status called 'dar al-ahd' (abode of the covenant). Apart from the tax, there was no interference in their internal administration. When attacked from outside they received protection, and their people had the same rights as the dhimmi subjects in the Ottoman lands. According to the same article, the vassal principalities that regularly received protection were Wallachia, Moldavia, Transylvania and Dubrovnik. Istanbul also intervened in disputes among these countries: in a decree of 1564 concerning a quarrel between Transylvania and Wallachia it was stated that 'the people and men of both sides are my tribute-paying subjects'.\n\nFOREIGN affairs — According to the article on Transylvania in the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, for about 150 years Transylvania enjoyed broad autonomy in its internal affairs, while in foreign affairs it acted in dependence on Istanbul. The ahidnames granted to the princes of Transylvania, the kings of Hungary or the ruler of Kartli (TDV, article Ahidnâme) were likewise documents of this tie. In other words, these countries had no right to make war and peace on their own.\n\nBUT what about foreign alliances? Two different situations can be seen in the sources. One is cooperation approved by Istanbul: according to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, the Transylvanian prince Bethlen Gábor 'remained friends with the Turks' and, while fighting Austria, established contact with the Protestant countries of Europe. This relationship, conducted against an enemy of the Ottomans, did not damage his allegiance. The other is an alliance regarded as rebellion: Michael the Brave (Mihai Viteazul), voivode of Wallachia (1593-1601), rose in revolt over taxes and received help from the prince of Transylvania; the Ottomans, for their part, established friendly relations with Transylvania and Poland and left him isolated. When the Transylvanian prince George II Rákóczi set his sights on the Polish throne, he fell out with Istanbul and died in a war he had entered upon. Dimitrie Cantemir, voivode of Moldavia, made an alliance with Tsar Peter in 1711; after the Pruth he fled to Russia.\n\nTHE OTTOMAN response was not annexation but a tightening of control — According to the article on Moldavia in the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, after Cantemir and the Wallachian voivode Brâncoveanu, in the reign of Ahmed III, Greek princes who would remain under the supervision of the Sublime Porte were appointed to these two voivodeships in place of native princes. The era of the Phanariot princes lasted until 1821, and these voivodes became 'mere officials of the sultan'. According to the TDV article on Khotyn, after 1711 Khotyn too was taken from Moldavia and placed under direct Ottoman administration, first as a nahiye (district) and then as a sanjak. This is the point of direct Ottoman rule in the middle of Moldavia on the map.\n\nWas it 'OTTOMAN territory'? The Ottomans' own answer had two sides. According to the article on Moldavia in the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, during the Karlowitz negotiations of 1699 the Ottoman plenipotentiaries argued that Moldavia could not be handed over to Poland, saying that it was 'a free country, subject to the Ottoman State not by the sword but of its own consent'. That is, Moldavia was not regarded as Ottoman property like a province, yet the Ottomans considered it so closely bound to themselves that it could not be ceded to another state.", "34546fd2"], "kisa": ["Wallachia, Moldavia and Transylvania managed their own internal affairs, but in foreign affairs they were bound to Istanbul. Foreign alliances made against the Ottomans were regarded not as a right but as rebellion. The Ottomans, for their part, considered these countries both 'free' and under their own protection.", "22606f0e"], "not": ["When reading the map: the atlas colours these three countries in the 'vassal' shade, distinct from the red of direct Ottoman rule. This is a choice midway between two views. What foreign sources call a 'separate state' and Ottoman sources a 'tribute-paying subject' are two faces of the same structure. For details of the terms, see the 'Language of allegiance' cards; for the two historiographies, see the card 'Internal rebellion or war of independence?'.", "6cbc7719"]},
"tartisma-yildiz-suikasti-1905":{"baslik": ["The Yıldız assassination attempt (1905) and Tevfik Fikret's poem", "70aec251"], "metin": ["On 21 July 1905, the bomb attack targeting Abdulhamid II as he came out of the Friday procession (selamlık) failed. Because of a short delay, the sultan was not beside his carriage at the moment of the explosion. What is debated is not so much the event itself as the mark it left.\n\n■ THE POEM\nTevfik Fikret wrote his poem \"Bir Lahza-i Teahhur\" (A Moment's Delay) on the assassination attempt; the poem was published in 1906 and passed from hand to hand in the years before the proclamation of the Second Constitutional Era. The title describes exactly that short delay: the momentary lateness that caused the assassination attempt to fail.\n\n■ THIS IS WHERE THE DEBATE BEGINS\nAssessments of the poem differ sharply from source to source. One reading sees Fikret as a poet standing against despotism: in \"Sis\" (Fog), which he wrote in 1902, he heaped curses on the Istanbul of the era of Abdulhamid II, and \"Bir Lahza-i Teahhur\" is a continuation of this stance. Another reading describes the same poem as applauding those who organized the assassination attempt, and levels a grave accusation. Both readings arise from the same text, and the difference between them depends less on the text itself than on the answer given to the question of who organized the attempt and why.\n\n■ FIKRET'S OWN LINE\nFikret's political target was not a single sultan. \"Doksan Beşe Doğru\" (Toward Ninety-Five) and \"Han-ı Yağma\" (The Feast of Plunder), which he wrote in 1912, are harsh satires written against the Unionists who overthrew despotism. That is, the same poet confronted with equal severity first the sultanate and then those who overthrew the sultanate; to count him as the man of a single side is to misread both.\n\nWE STOP HERE: No judgment has been passed in this card on the person and organization that arranged the assassination attempt, its connection with Armenian political movements, or the motives behind the incident: the body of the encyclopedia entry consulted did not cover these subjects, and a gap has been left rather than writing an unsupported attribution.", "813c871c"], "kisa": ["A bomb went off a minute and a half late. A poet lamented this delay, and that poem is still debated today.", "dfdac8c6"]},
"teknik-1908-secimleri-ve-meclis":{"baslik": ["The elections of 1908: who could vote and who could not?", "a98b9057"], "metin": ["On 17 December 1908 the Chamber of Deputies convened again after an interval of thirty years. To understand who made up the parliament, one must first know the procedure that elected it.\n\n■ TWO-STAGE ELECTION\nThe election was not direct. The people first chose the primary electors, these designated the secondary electors, and it was the secondary electors who chose the deputy. This procedure had two faces: on the one hand, it was the only practical method that could work in a country with a low literacy rate spread over a vast geography; on the other, because it placed a filter between the voter and the deputy, it made it easier for an organized society to determine the result.\n\n■ THE MEASURE OF REPRESENTATION\nDeputies were allotted on the basis of the sanjak; sanjaks with a male population between 25,000 and 75,000 elected one deputy each.\n\n■ WHO COULD ELECT AND WHO COULD BE ELECTED\nThe minimum age was 25 to be an elector and 30 to be a deputy. The system was based on male voters, which means that WOMEN were completely excluded from the right to vote and to stand for election. An identity card was required, and double voting was prevented.\n\n■ WHAT THIS MEANT\nThe right to vote was not universal, but neither did it carry a religious distinction: non-Muslim Ottoman subjects were part of the electoral process and were represented in the parliament. In other words, the election of 1908 was not an election in which \"everyone voted\" but an election by \"male subjects meeting certain conditions.\" Among those left outside were women entirely, and in part those who did not meet the property and age conditions.\n\n■ THE RESULT\nThe Unionists won the elections by an overwhelming majority.\n\n■ WHAT THE PARLIAMENT COULD DO\nIt could propose laws, examine the budget and supervise the government; it also had the authority to bring down the government by a motion of interpellation, and this authority was actually used in 1909: the government of Kamil Pasha the Cypriot was brought down by a vote of no confidence.\n\n■ THE AMENDMENT OF 1909: the real turning point\nThe amendment of the Kanun-i Esasi in 1909 considerably changed the balance between legislature and executive in favor of the parliament and established a structure similar to the European monarchies. It had two concrete results: the members of the government became responsible to the parliament, and the sultan's authority to dissolve the parliament was limited. The amended Article 53 gave the chambers the authority to draft laws; Articles 10 and 118 were arranged so as to include sharia provisions.\n\nIn other words, the constitution of 1876 had been born as a grant from the sultan; in 1909 it turned into a text that the parliament amended with its own hand. The difference between the two is the moment when constitutional government passed from words to an institution.", "22645fdc"], "kisa": ["Who voted in 1908, and, more importantly, who could not?", "3d55d35d"]},
"teknik-arazi-kanunnamesi-1858":{"baslik": ["The Land Code (1858) — five kinds of land", "b0255e0d"], "metin": ["■ Five kinds of land\nThe code dated 6 June 1858 divides Ottoman land into five classes: mülk (land in the full private ownership of an individual), mîrî (state land, whose bare ownership remains with the state while possession is granted to a person), waqf (land whose revenue has been assigned to a pious foundation), metruk (places left for common use, such as village pastures and roads) and mevat (uncultivated, ownerless dead land). This classification was not an invention of the code; the code compiles principles that had been applied for centuries into a single text — the code's own statement of purpose is to bring together the principles of land law in the Tanzimat period.\n\n■ THE REAL CHANGE: MÎRÎ LAND MOVED CLOSER TO PRIVATE PROPERTY\nThe code widened the right of possession over mîrî land in two directions. The categories of heirs to mîrî land were raised from three to eight, while the circumstances in which it could be transferred by title deed (tapu) were reduced from nine to three. Read together, the result is clear: in terms of transferability, mîrî land moved markedly closer to privately owned land. The state continued to retain bare ownership, but the right of the person holding the land was now both broader and more clearly defined.\n\n■ WHO PREPARED IT\nThe text was prepared by a commission chaired by Ahmed Cevdet Pasha. A few years later the Mecelle (the Ottoman civil code) would emerge from the same tradition of commissions.\n\n■ WHY IT MATTERS\nThe question of whose the land is also answers the questions of from whom taxes come and from where soldiers come. In the last half-century of the empire, the registration of the right of possession laid the foundation both of the land registry system and of provincial finance.", "9eade3b2"], "kisa": ["To the question of whom the land belonged to, the Ottomans gave five different answers — in 1858 these answers were brought together in a single text for the first time.", "3715fce4"]},
"teknik-bogaz-ulasimi-sirket-i-hayriyye-oncesi":{"baslik": ["Transport on the Bosphorus and the Şirket-i Hayriyye", "5d5533f1"], "metin": ["■ On the Bosphorus before the steamer\nThe TDV article on the Bosphorus describes it thus: formerly, transport on the Bosphorus was provided by peremes (light rowing boats) pulled by six oars and by market boats whose revenue was endowed to a charitable work; the people went by market boat to excursion spots such as Yuşa Hill, Kanlıca, Çubuklu and Beykoz. The sultans went out on pleasure trips on the Bosphorus in ornate imperial caiques; on these occasions the bostancıbaşı (chief of the palace gardeners' corps) steered the boat and gave the sultan information about the waterside mansions, gardens and pavilions on the shore. Thus the TDV's sentence shows that before the steamer, oared craft (pereme, market boat) were used on the Bosphorus; details such as routes, fares and the number of sailings were `not found` in the articles consulted.\n\n■ 1851: THE FIRST STEAMER AND THE COMPANY\nAfter the proclamation of the Tanzimat and the Crimean War, as interest in the Bosphorus and traffic on it increased, one of the steamers of the Tersâne-i Âmire (Imperial Arsenal) was assigned to government officials living on the Bosphorus; but these sailings, which ran only morning and evening, did not meet the need. Thereupon, on the proposal of Keçecizâde Fuad Pasha, then undersecretary of the grand vizierate, and Ahmed Cevdet Pasha, the Şirket-i Hayriyye was established (the TDV article on the Bosphorus writes “1851”). The company's openly stated aim was to provide an example of the “anonim” (joint-stock) companies that brought great wealth and employment in Europe, and to make it easier for those living on the Bosphorus to travel to and from the city.\n\n■ THE STRUCTURE OF THE COMPANY\nBy the ferman issued after the draft proposal had been approved by Abdülmecid, the concession and monopoly to operate steamers between Istanbul and the landing stages on the Anatolian and Rumelian shores of the Bosphorus was granted to the company for 25 years; the Golden Horn (Haliç) was not included in the concession. The capital was fixed at 45,000 liras and divided into 1500 shares of 30 liras each, and later another 500 shares were issued. As of 7 December 1850, Abdülmecid, his mother Bezmiâlem Valide Sultan, the ministers, officials and investors had taken 830 shares; the court chronicler Lutfi Efendi writes that Mustafa Reşid Pasha encouraged officials to buy shares, that many took certificates out of regard for him, and even that officials went into debt to sarrafs (moneylenders) and bankers for the shares. Because the public was not accustomed to joint-stock companies, a problem arose in determining the form of management; on 3 January 1852 the operation was farmed out for six years, and a steamer brought from England, with paddle wheels on all four sides, began service in the spring of 1852. The first board of directors was formed on 27 June 1854. The first known statutes of the company are dated 31 December 1872. A note on dates: the TDV bases the approval of the draft proposal on issue 436 of Takvîm-i Vekāyi (10 Muharram 1267); since the same article gives the correspondence 10 Rabi al-Awwal 1268 = 3 January 1852, counting back, 10 Muharram 1267 falls in November 1850 (this site's calculation). The TDV article on the Bosphorus, for its part, writes that the founding was in “1851”; the year of founding may therefore be cited as 1850 or 1851.\n\n■ FROM WITHIN THE WALLS TO BEYOĞLU: THE GOLDEN HORN\nThe company's concession did not cover the Golden Horn; one crossed between the two sides by way of the Golden Horn. According to the TDV article on the Golden Horn, the first bridge linking the two sides was the Hayrâtiye Bridge, completed in 1836; the temporary bridge on barrels that the Conqueror built in 1453 had not lasted long, and there had been thought of inviting Leonardo da Vinci and Michelangelo to build a bridge, but nothing came of it. This means that before the bridges the Golden Horn was crossed by boat (this site's interpretation: the TDV does not write explicitly of this crossing as boatmen's work, but it does describe how the later bridges affected the boatmen; see card: Galata Bridge). The details of transport between 1836 and 1845 (how many boats, which landing stages, what fare) were `not found` in the articles consulted.", "54811d5a"], "kisa": ["When there were no steamers between the two shores of the Bosphorus, there were six-oared peremes and market boats; the Şirket-i Hayriyye, for its part, was an enterprise by way of a lesson from the state in answer to the question “what is a joint-stock company?”", "fe32c77a"]},
"teknik-bulgar-eksarhligi-1870-kilise-ve-kimlik":{"baslik": ["The Bulgarian Exarchate (1870) — church and nation", "8f4881a3"], "metin": ["■ What happened\nOn 11 March 1870 an independent Bulgarian church was established. This was an arrangement that removed the Bulgarians, in religious terms, from the administration of the Greek Orthodox Patriarchate of the Phanar.\n\n■ THERE IS A DIFFERENCE IN THE DATE — BOTH ARE GIVEN\nTwo dates are recorded for the same event: 11 March 1870 in one article and 28 February 1870 in another. The eleven-day difference between them is consistent with the difference between the Rumi and Gregorian calendars of the period; however, since the articles do not say so explicitly, both dates have been left here as they stand, and neither has been derived from the other.\n\n■ WHY A CHURCH QUESTION IS A QUESTION OF IDENTITY\nIn the Ottoman order non-Muslim subjects were organised as communities, and the boundary of a community was drawn by confession and church. Since the religious administration of the Orthodox subjects was concentrated in the Patriarchate of the Phanar, a Bulgarian peasant was in the official order an Orthodox Christian attached to the Greek patriarchate. A separate church means separate schools and a separate church language; and separate schools and language are in the next generation the institutional ground of a separate national identity. For this reason 1870 is regarded in Balkan history less as a religious event than as a turning point of identity.\n\n■ BACKGROUND\nThe source counts the patriarchate's attitude towards the Bulgarians among the causes of the split: it states that this exploitation played a part in the formation of rebel committees among the Bulgarians, who had had no complaint about Turkish rule until the 19th century. In other words, the first axis of the split was not Ottoman-Bulgarian but Greek-Bulgarian.\n\n■ WHAT COULD NOT BE ESTABLISHED\nNo explicit sentence about the patriarchate's excommunication of the Bulgarian church in 1872 was found in the article surveyed; this card does not describe that step.", "b37fb428"], "kisa": ["Why was the separation of a church treated as an affair of state? Because in the Ottoman order a community was the boundary that the church drew.", "8251ee6e"]},
"teknik-bulgaristan-sinirinin-cizilisi":{"baslik": ["How was the border of Bulgaria drawn? (1878-1909)", "1d72de7b"], "metin": ["With the protocol signed in Istanbul on 19 April 1909, the Ottoman State formally recognized the independence of Bulgaria, which had been proclaimed unilaterally six months earlier. But how that border took shape is a story that began thirty-one years before this and has three separate stages.\n\n■ THE FIRST LINE: 1878, THE NEGOTIATING TABLE\nThe Treaty of San Stefano, signed after the war of 1877-1878, had envisaged a Greater Bulgaria that also took in much of Macedonia. The Treaty of Berlin reduced it considerably: an autonomous principality was established between the Danube and the Balkans, including Sofia, Nikopol, Sistova, Rusçuk, Silistra, Varna, Shumen (Şumnu), Lovech and Tarnovo.\nThe remaining part did not return entirely to the Ottomans: from the sanjaks of Plovdiv, Sliven, Stara Zagora, Tatar Pazardzhik, Burgas and Haskovo, a province of Eastern Rumelia with a separate status was formed. In other words, Berlin created not a single Bulgaria but a Bulgaria split in two.\n\n■ THE SECOND LINE: 1885, THE FAIT ACCOMPLI\nThis division lasted seven years. In 1885 the Principality of Bulgaria added the province of Eastern Rumelia to its territory as well. The separation drawn at the negotiating table in Berlin was erased on the ground by a fait accompli.\n\n■ THE THIRD LINE: 1908-1909, RECOGNITION\nOn 5 October 1908 Bulgaria declared its independence and left the status of a principality. With the Istanbul protocol of 19 April 1909, the Ottomans recognized this. The negotiations covered not only independence but also the questions of the Rumelian railway and compensation: the state gave up the lines on the territory it had lost in return for compensation of 42 million francs.\n\n■ \"WERE THE BULGARIANS ABLE TO JOIN EVERY PLACE WHERE THEY WERE NUMEROUS?\"\nNo. The Greater Bulgaria envisaged by San Stefano also included Macedonia; Berlin took this back, and Macedonia remained under Ottoman administration. For thirty-five years thereafter the Bulgarian movement set its sights on Macedonia: this is the background of the struggle of the guerrilla bands in the region and of the Ilinden uprising of 1903. In other words, it was not ethnic concentration that determined the border; the border was determined by the balance among the great powers.\n\n■ THOSE LEFT BEHIND\nWhile the border changed, the population did not stay in place. It is recorded that during and after the war of 1877-1878 between 500,000 and 600,000 Turks were killed or forced to migrate. The figure for a single sanjak shows the scale: in the sanjak of Plovdiv, the Turkish population, which was 300,000 in 1875, had fallen to 15,000 in 1878. Nor did the migration happen only once: according to official records, 72,524 Muslim Turks emigrated between 1893 and 1902; at the end of the Balkan War in 1913, 115,883 people applied officially for resettlement.\n\nDrawing a border is a matter of the map; the displacement of the people on both sides of that border takes decades.", "d68a1cc7"], "kisa": ["The border of Bulgaria was drawn three times: in a treaty, in a coup, in a protocol.", "d77dedff"]},
"teknik-cezayir-dayilik":{"baslik": ["The rule of the deys in Algiers — from the pasha appointed by Istanbul to the dey chosen by the military corps", "90ccd8df"], "metin": ["■ The beginning of Ottoman sovereignty (from 1516)\nThe people of Algiers asked for help against the Spanish occupation; the brothers Oruç Reis and Hızır (Barbarossa) Reis took Algiers in 1516. Hayreddin Barbarossa obtained the support of the Ottoman sultan and, by having the Friday sermon read in the city in his name, formally attached the region to Ottoman administration.\n\n■ THE ‘WESTERN REGENCIES’ (GARP OCAKLARI) — A SEMI-AUTONOMOUS MILITARY SYSTEM\nAlgiers was governed within the semi-autonomous military and administrative structure called the Western Regencies; the system passed through four stages: the period of the beylerbeys (1518-1587), the period of the pashas (1587-1659), the period of the aghas (1659-1671) and the period of the deys (1671-1830).\n\n■ RELATIONS WITH ISTANBUL — NOT TIGHT BUT LOOSE\nThe bond was far looser than in a typical provincial administration: the system of the deys produced serious challenges to central authority; the deys repeatedly rejected the governors sent by Istanbul and conducted their own foreign relations independently. In the source's words, the Ottoman government applied to the Algerian corps a FREER administration rather than strict centralisation. The deys could independently declare war and make peace, signed their own treaties and did not accept the governors sent by Istanbul; when Istanbul attempted to use force in 1729-1731, the Ottomans' internal crises provided the conditions that protected the Algerian corsairs from this pressure.\n\n■ MILITARY STRUCTURE\nThe forces consisted of a combination of soldiers recruited from Anatolia and local cavalry (the ‘Makhzan’) raised from the Berber tribes; in the 1630s their total reached about 20,000 men.\n\n■ NOT TAX, BUT INCOME FROM CORSAIRING\nThe main source of revenue was not a tribute paid to Istanbul but corsair activity — a financial model that was the exact reverse of the classical relationship of a tributary state (see Wallachia/Moldavia/Transylvania/the Crimea).\n\n■ THE TITLE OF DEY — A SEPARATE TRAP IN THE TDV\nThe TDV article `dayi` does NOT describe this administrative title but the term in Islamic jurisprudence for a ‘relative on the mother's side (maternal uncle, dayı)’ — although the link is live, the article is irrelevant to the subject. All the information on the rule of the deys in this card was taken from the `cezayir` article itself.\n\n■ TUNISIA, LIBYA, EGYPT, SUDAN, SOMALIA, ETHIOPIA — NOT RESEARCHED\nThe same analysis could NOT yet be carried out for these regions — `bulunamadı` (not found); it should be left to a later round.", "702cc074"], "kisa": ["In the end a governor appointed by Istanbul could no longer even take up his post — in Algiers, real power had been gathered by the deys whom the Algerians' own corps chose.", "a496a500"]},
"teknik-darulaceze-ve-hayir-kurumlari":{"baslik": ["The Darülaceze (1896) and charitable institutions", "78b42ee8"], "metin": ["The Darülaceze (House for the Destitute), opened on 2 February 1896 on the heights of Okmeydanı, is the most visible Ottoman experiment in the field of social policy, and its starting point is a question of the city more than a feeling of charity.\n\n■ WHERE THE IDEA CAME FROM\nBy a decree dated 30 March 1890, Abdulhamid II asked for an institution to be founded to protect homeless children and disabled people who begged in the streets. That is, the birth of the institution begins with begging being seen as a matter of security and public order; the remedy was described not as a ban but as shelter.\n\n■ CONSTRUCTION AND MONEY\nThe design and planning were carried out between 1890 and 1892, and Halil Rifat Pasha laid the foundation on 10 November 1892. The construction was directed by Vasilaki Efendi, a master builder of the Imperial Arsenal. The budget, initially foreseen at 100,000 liras, was reduced to 70,000 liras. The money was collected from these sources: the sultan's gift of 17,000 liras, donations, lottery tickets, theater revenue and collection boxes in places of worship.\n\n■ WHOM IT WAS OPEN TO\nHomeless children, the elderly, the infirm and the disabled. The founding principle was stated clearly: no distinction of religion or nationality was to be made. This principle was also reflected in the architecture: a mosque, a church and a synagogue were built together in the courtyard. Three houses of worship under one roof are the shortest expression of what the institution meant to say.\n\n■ WHAT IT CONTAINED\nAt the opening, 150 women were the first to be admitted. The institution was not merely a shelter: a hospital with a capacity of 200 beds, an orphanage and a milk nursery for children aged 0-7 were established. There was also education: between 1896 and 1907, 48 people received diplomas and 6 became hafiz (memorizers of the Quran); in 1907, 130 male and 22 female students were registered at the institution.\n\n■ WHY THIS ERA\nThe Darülaceze is not an event on its own but part of a wave of institution-building. The same era must be read together with the schools where orphans and poor children were educated, the new hospitals and the health institutions established around the modern medical school. The common point is this: in this era an attempt was made to turn work that had previously been done through endowments, piecemeal and locally, into institutions with a central budget, regulations and staff.", "f1f647f5"], "kisa": ["It is easy to ban begging; it is hard to find a place for the one who begs. How did a state try to do this?", "9ea23605"]},
"teknik-darulfunun-dort-deneme":{"baslik": ["The Darülfünun — a university founded four times", "17db7eec"], "metin": ["■ From decision to first lecture: seventeen years\nThe decision to establish a Darülfünun (university) was taken in 1846. The first lecture could not be given until 13 January 1863: the chemist Derviş Pasha, under the supervision of İbrahim Edhem Pasha, lectured on physics and chemistry. The seventeen years in between passed amid a lack of a building, of teachers and of prepared students.\n\n■ FOUR ATTEMPTS\n• 1863 — series of lectures open to the public. The lectures were brought to an end following the reactions provoked by Jamal al-Din al-Afghani's remarks on prophethood.\n• 20 February 1870 — the Darülfünun-ı Osmanî was officially opened. It failed to take root because enough qualified teachers and sufficiently prepared students could not be found.\n• 1874-75 — the Darülfünun-ı Sultanî began teaching; because of financial instability its activity died out around 1880-81.\n• 31 August 1900 — the Darülfünun-ı Şahane was opened. This is the attempt that achieved continuity.\n\n■ THE COMMON CAUSE OF FAILURE\nThree of the four attempts died out for the same three reasons: no teachers, no prepared students, no money. This shows that founding a university is not a matter of a building and a decree, and that one level of education rests on the level before it — indeed, a lasting Darülfünun was able to take root only after the network of idadi (secondary) and rüşdiye (upper primary) schools had reached a certain density.", "a8646a2d"], "kisa": ["Deciding to found a university was not enough: the same institution was founded four times within thirty years and closed three times.", "6a376003"]},
"teknik-divandan-nezaretlere-maliye-nezareti":{"baslik": ["From council to ministries — the founding of the Ministry of Finance", "4ed02611"], "metin": ["■ The centre had shifted to the Sublime Porte\nFrom the 17th century onwards, actual authority had passed from the Dîvân-ı Hümâyun (Imperial Council) to the grand vizier's headquarters (the Sublime Porte), and the Reisülküttab and the scribes of the Divan had left the palace and settled there (see card: the Ottoman administrative structure and its transformation). The TDV defines Bâbıâli (the Sublime Porte) as an expression used from the late 18th century for the Pasha's Gate and the office of the grand vizierate, and, after the ministries were established, in the sense of the Ottoman government. This pre-Tanzimat structure was turned into ministries (nezâret) at the hands of Mahmud II.\n\n■ 11 MARCH 1836: THE FIRST MINISTRIES\nBy Mahmud II's imperial rescript (hatt-ı hümâyun) dated 23 Dhu al-Qa'dah 1251 (11 March 1836), the office of reisülküttab was converted into the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, and the stewardship of the grand vizierate into the Ministry of the Interior (Umûr-ı Mülkiyye, Civil Affairs). On 9 October 1837 the name of Umûr-ı Mülkiyye was changed to Ministry of the Interior (Dahiliye) (Âkif Pasha became minister). The joint meeting place of the ministries was the Meclis-i Vükelâ (Council of Ministers): this council, which appears in documents under many names such as Encümen-i Mahsûs and Meclis-i Meşveret, and whose date of founding is not known (it probably formed through a gradual transition from the Meclis-i Meşveret, or Consultative Assembly), met under the presidency of the chief minister (başvekil) or grand vizier, ensured coordination among the ministries, and submitted the drafts it prepared to the sultan for approval by memorandum (tezkire). In 1838 the expression “başvekil” (prime minister) was used for a short time in place of “grand vizier”.\n\n■ FROM THE DEFTERDARLIK TO THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE — THE TDV GIVES TWO ACCOUNTS\n• The article on the Cerîde Nezâreti (Superintendency of Registers): “on 28 February 1838 the use of the term defterdarlık (office of the treasurer) was forbidden and the Ministry of Finance was established”; the bureaus of the Bâb-ı Defterdârî (treasurer's department) were reorganised, and the Cerîde and Mevkūfat bureaus were merged.\n• The article on the defterdar (based on Cezar): when the Hazîne-i Âmire (Imperial Treasury) and the Mint Treasury were merged at the end of 1837, the office of chief defterdar was abolished; in February 1838 the two treasuries were separated, the Imperial Treasury was merged with the Mansûre Treasury, and the defterdarlık was turned into a Ministry of Finance; as the treasuries were separated again the following year, a defterdar was once more placed at the head of the Imperial Treasury. When the two treasuries were merged in 1841, this time definitively, the institution of the defterdarlık gave way to the Ministry of Finance.\nThe two accounts do not contradict each other; they differ in scale: 28 February 1838 is the first step, but it was reversed a year later; the permanent Ministry of Finance dates from 1841. The TDV does not reconcile this in a single sentence; which date is to count as the “founding” is the reader's choice.\n\n■ WHAT CHANGED\nIn the classical order, financial affairs were conducted by the defterdars of Rumelia and Anatolia (in later centuries also by additional defterdarships), and the defterdars sat in the Dîvân-ı Hümâyun. In the new order there was a single minister and a Council of Ministers that met at the Sublime Porte. A side effect of this change can also be seen in the records: when the bureaus of the defterdarlık were merged, the Cerîde Nezâreti's task of keeping population and tax records was also reorganised (see card: the first population census).\n\n■ HOW THE HIGH BUREAUCRACY TOOK SHAPE\nThe TDV article on the Ministry of Foreign Affairs counts this post, whose origins go back to the office of reisülküttab, among the “high-ranking scribal offices”, and links the attempts from Selim III onwards to open embassies and consulates in Europe to the view, after Küçük Kaynarca, that arrangements on European models were necessary. In other words, what happened between 1836 and 1841 was the redistribution of the classical posts (reisülküttab, steward of the grand vizierate, defterdar) under new ministerial names while preserving their functions; the TDV does not sum this up in a single sentence saying that “a new class of bureaucrats was born” — that reading is this site's interpretation.", "98dbaa6d"], "kisa": ["The defterdarlık became the Ministry of Finance on 28 February 1838, but it returned a year later — the permanent transition dates from 1841.", "411d9fdc"]},
"teknik-eflak-bogdan-erdel-haklari":{"baslik": ["The rights and obligations of Wallachia, Moldavia and Transylvania", "bf61432b"], "metin": ["In addition to the general list, the concrete division of powers in the three Balkan and Carpathian voivodeships:\n\n■ INTERNAL AFFAIRS\nIn all three, the native voivode or prince kept internal administration in his own hands: in Wallachia the local boyars and an independent Orthodox metropolitanate, in Moldavia a similar local administration, and in Transylvania the diet of the ‘three nations’ (Hungarians, Székelys and Saxons) maintained its own internal order. The Ottomans did not interfere in the day-to-day administration of any of them.\n\n■ TAX (TRIBUTE)\nAll three were liable for a fixed or rising annual tribute: in Wallachia and Moldavia the amount multiplied over time (in Moldavia from 2,000 to 12,000 gold pieces), and in Transylvania it rose from 10,000 to 40,000 gold pieces. This payment was TRIBUTE (haraç), not a direct tax — the amount was paid in a lump sum not to the central treasury but from the voivode's own treasury.\n\n■ SUPPLYING TROOPS\nThe TDV articles that were read do not EXPLICITLY mention, for any of the three, a regular, compulsory obligation to supply soldiers to the Ottoman army — this is their basic difference from the Crimean Khanate, whose regular participation in campaigns was a standard part of Ottoman mobilisation. The princes of Transylvania joined Ottoman campaigns when political circumstances compelled them, but this article gives no figure for a ‘compulsory contingent’ — `ölçülemedi` (could not be measured).\n\n■ FOREIGN AFFAIRS AND THE APPOINTMENT OF THE PRINCE\nIn all three, foreign policy was subject to Ottoman approval. The appointment of the prince or voivode was not a purely local matter: in Transylvania the sultan could intervene directly and appoint the prince (the example of Gábor Bethlen, 1613); in Wallachia and Moldavia, after 1716/1710, the native dynasties were shut out altogether and replaced by Phanariot Greek rulers appointed directly by Istanbul — this is a common tendency showing that over time all three CAME CLOSER to the centre rather than moving away from it.\n\n■ GARRISON\nIn the classical period none of the three had a permanent Ottoman garrison (the article on Wallachia states this explicitly); this contrasts with the Crimea, where garrisons were placed directly in the coastal fortresses (Caffa and others).", "76119cc9"], "kisa": ["These three voivodeships were neither fully independent nor an Ottoman sancak — the line in between lay in taxation, in the army and in whose approval a prince needed to take the throne.", "e9d284ec"]},
"teknik-eyaletten-vilayete-1864":{"baslik": ["From eyalet to vilayet — the Provincial Regulation of 1864", "4d7f729e"], "metin": ["■ The old order: the eyalet\nIn the classical order the largest provincial unit was the eyalet (province) governed by a beylerbeyi (governor-general). The eyalet was divided into sanjaks, the sanjaks were administered by sanjak-beys, and military force was largely provided through the timar system. In the nine provinces where no timars were distributed (the salyaneli, or annual-remittance, provinces), taxes were collected directly for the treasury. The beylerbeyi was both military and administrative chief; authority was concentrated in a single hand.\n\n■ THE TANZIMAT'S FIRST BLOW: FINANCE WAS SEPARATED\nWith the proclamation of the Tanzimat in 1839, financial affairs in the provinces were taken away from the governor and entrusted to independent tax collectors (muhassıls). In other words, the governor lost the money first.\n\n■ THE NEW ORDER: THE VILAYET\nIn 1864 vilayets (provinces) were formed in place of the eyalet system. The new order had two defining features: the establishment of administrative councils that shared the governor's responsibility, and the reduction of the boundaries of provincial units. The two together divided the provincial power that had been concentrated in a single hand both horizontally (through the council) and geographically (through the smaller unit).\n\n■ WHAT WAS REMOVED, WHAT CAME — SUMMARY\n• Removed: one-man administration centred on the beylerbeyi, wide eyalet boundaries, the combination of military and administrative authority in the same office.\n• Introduced: a smaller administrative unit, a council that took part in decision-making alongside the governor, and the conduct of financial affairs through a separate branch.\n\n■ WHAT COULD NOT BE ESTABLISHED\nHow the new order worked in practice — how many decisions the councils actually produced — has not been established in the article on which this card is based; the card describes only the change in the legal framework.", "557f6eed"], "kisa": ["The eyalet went, the vilayet came — and some of the powers on the governor's desk were distributed to a council.", "8dd708d9"]},
"teknik-galata-koprusu-ne-zaman-acildi":{"baslik": ["When was the Galata Bridge opened? In 1844 or 1845?", "a3a358d2"], "metin": ["■ The obvious question: when was it opened\nTwo TDV articles give different years: the article on Bezmiâlem Valide Sultan writes, under the heading “Galata Bridge (Cisr-i Cedîd or Valide Bridge)”, that the bridge was commissioned in 1844 as a floating bridge on wooden pontoons and served for eighteen years; the article on the Golden Horn says that “in 1845 the first Karaköy Bridge, again on pontoons and of wood, built at the Tersâne-i Âmire, was set up between Karaköy and Eminönü” (the same bridge). There is no sentence reconciling the two. This site's interpretation: “commissioned” may refer to the order given in 1844 and “was set up” to its entry into service in 1845, but the TDV does not write this. No record giving the day of opening is in the two articles consulted: `not found`. The TDV's own heading (galata-koprusu) is not a separate article but a cross-reference (“see”) to the article on the Golden Horn.\n\n■ THE SEQUENCE OF BRIDGES (TDV Golden Horn, Semavi Eyice)\n• Hayrâtiye Bridge (Cisr-i Atîk, the Old Bridge), 1836: the FIRST bridge over the Golden Horn linking the two sides; built at the Arsenal in the reign of Mahmud II, between Azapkapı and Unkapanı, 600 cubits (arşın) long; at the opening ceremony the sultan crossed it on horseback, and no toll was charged.\n• 1845 (TDV Bezmiâlem: 1844): the first wooden pontoon bridge between Karaköy and Eminönü; it is estimated to have been about 500 m long; it served for eighteen years. Its founder was Bezmiâlem Valide Sultan (TDV).\n• 1863: a second Karaköy Bridge, again of wood; it served until 1875.\n• 1863: the “Jewish Bridge”, which the banker (sarraf) Mıgırdiç Cezayirliyan had built on wooden piles between Ayvansaray and Pîrîpaşa and which charged those crossing it — the boatmen who carried passengers by boat burned it ten days after its opening because their business was suffering.\n• 1870-1875: agreements for a new iron bridge with the French Forges et Chantiers de la Méditerranée (1870) and with the representative of the Englishman George Wells (1872); delayed because of the Franco-German war. According to the TDV, the French firm's bridge was set up towards 1875 as the Azapkapı-Unkapanı Bridge (480 m, 26 pontoons, contract price 135,000 gold liras; dismantled in 1912 and taken off Eyüp); the English firm's iron bridge, 480 m long with 24 pontoons, stood as the third Karaköy Bridge between Karaköy and Eminönü until 1912 (the price, agreed at 95,000 gold liras, rose to 105,000 gold liras because of the change of location).\n• 27 April 1912: the iron bridge of the German firm M.A.N. (237,000 gold liras) was ceremonially opened to service; beneath it were shops and cafés, and there were special tokens for collecting a toll from those crossing. The old bridge was towed between Unkapanı and Azapkapı and used for another 28 years; on the night of 12 February 1936 it broke loose in a snowstorm and broke up.\n\n■ WHO LOST WHEN THE BRIDGE CAME\nThe TDV writes that even after the bridge came, the boat services between Eminönü and Karaköy, used by “those who did not wish to cross the bridge on foot”, continued until recent times, and that on the Golden Horn the bridges, steamers and factories destroyed its character as a place for outings and summer resort. The boatmen's burning of the Jewish Bridge is a concrete expression of the rivalry between the boatmen's trade and the bridge.", "e0cf3eb0"], "kisa": ["To the question “When was the Galata Bridge opened?” two TDV articles give two years — 1844 and 1845 — while neither writes the day of opening.", "b1ac0fc1"]},
"teknik-galatasaray-mekteb-i-sultanisi-1868":{"baslik": ["The Galatasaray Imperial School (Mekteb-i Sultanî) (1868)", "118316a1"], "metin": ["■ Opening\nThe Mekteb-i Sultanî (Imperial School) began teaching with a ceremony on 1 September 1868; it was housed in the Galata Palace (Galata Sarayı) building in Beyoğlu. Instruction was to be largely in French, and some subjects were to be taught in Turkish at the insistence of the Ottoman side.\n\n■ FRANCE'S ROLE\nFrance's part in the founding of the school was decisive: the French Minister of Education Victor Duruy had even prepared the curriculum of the lycée to be established and sent it to Istanbul.\n\n■ THE FIRST YEAR: 409 PUPILS\nWhen teaching began, of a total of 409 pupils 172 were Muslims and 237 non-Muslims. The breakdown of the non-Muslim portion is given community by community: 58 Gregorian Armenians, 31 Catholic Armenians, 26 Catholic Latins, 41 Greeks, 47 Jews, 34 Bulgarians. By March 1870 the enrolment had risen to 640: 273 Muslims, 367 non-Muslims.\n\n■ OBJECTIONS — THEY DID NOT COME FROM WHERE EXPECTED\n• The Greeks objected to the removal of the Greek language from the curriculum.\n• The Papacy forbade Catholic pupils to enter the school; the ban was later lifted through French intervention.\n• The Jewish community hesitated to send its children to a school under Christian administration.\n• By contrast, the point to which the source draws particular attention is this: no opposition came from the Ottoman ulama to Muslim children being sent to the school.\n\n■ WHY IT MATTERS\nThe school became one of the main channels training officials who knew a Western language for the state service — especially for the Foreign Ministry. In Ottoman modernisation the school was an instrument as decisive as law: the cadre of a generation later was formed on these benches.", "bc683777"], "kisa": ["The harshest objection to a state lycée teaching in French came not from the ulama but from the Pope and the Greek community.", "0d5e8a9c"]},
"teknik-gazetecilik-dogusu-ilk-osmanli-gazeteleri":{"baslik": ["How was journalism born? The first Ottoman newspapers", "d00fee3e"], "metin": ["■ How journalism was born\nTDV says only this: in Europe the newspaper and journalism have a long past that can be traced from the beginning of the 17th century; in the Ottoman lands the first newspapers were published at the end of the 18th century by foreigners, in their own languages, in centres such as Istanbul and İzmir. The names and places of the first printed newspapers in Europe and the reasons for their birth are `not found` in the articles read; this card makes no guesses on that subject.\n\n■ WHO CARRIED THE NEWS IN THE CLASSICAL PERIOD\nNo single institution held the place of a regular newspaper; different instruments did different jobs (the classification is the page's interpretation):\n• Town criers (dellals): the news criers working under the command of the chief crier (dellalbaşı) went round the markets shouting out the news they had been given to the people; they received a daily wage. Announcing to the people events such as the death and accession of a sultan fell to the criers, who were official agents of the state (TDV: dellal).\n• Couriers (ulaks): the postal system that carried orders and news from one menzil (post station) to the next; the sultan, viziers and qadis could issue courier warrants (TDV: ulak). This was the state's own internal communication, not a publication open to the public.\n• Official court chronicler (vakanüvis): the official historian of the state. The institutionalisation of the post as a state service dates to the grand vizierate of Amcazade Hüseyin Pasha at the beginning of the 18th century; in 1716 Naima was granted a salary of 120 akçe a day; the court chroniclers handed their notes on to one another so that the record would be uninterrupted (TDV: vakanuvis). The product was not an issue distributed to the people but the archival history of the state.\n• Coffee house: from the second half of the 16th century coffee houses were seen as places where gossip and rumours, called “state talk” (devlet sohbeti), spread, which the political authority watched carefully and sometimes banned (TDV: kahvehane). The name “kıraathane” (reading house) for a place for reading dates to after the Tanzimat; the first example is the Sarafim Kıraathanesi, opened on Divanyolu in 1857.\n\n■ THE FIRST NEWSPAPERS\n• Curnalü'l-Irâk: a Turkish-Arabic newspaper alleged to have been published in November 1816 by the Mamluk Davud Pasha, governor of Baghdad; TDV hedges this as “alleged”.\n• Vekayi-i Mısriyye: Mehmed Ali Pasha's newspaper, half in Turkish and half in Arabic, whose first issue appeared in Cairo on 2 December 1828.\n• Takvim-i Vekayi: 1 November 1831, Istanbul, the official gazette (see the separate card).\n• Ceride-i Havadis: 31 July 1840-26 September 1864, 1212 issues in all. Its owner was the Englishman William Churchill, who had settled in İzmir in 1815; in 1836 Churchill, while hunting in Moda, wounded a child and was arrested, the ambassador Ponsonby, relying on the rights under the capitulations, delivered a note, and when the affair grew, the Ottoman government apologised and, as compensation, he was granted the concession to publish a newspaper. The newspaper appeared with the support of the printing press and staff of the Takvimhane (Gazette Office); Sultan Abdülmecid granted it an allowance; its sales did not exceed 150. TDV shows this newspaper as the publication in which the word “gazete” (newspaper) was first used in the Ottoman press; it introduced innovations such as the first correspondent (Alexandria, 1840), the first supplement, death notices and biography (1844), serialised fiction (1846), readers' letters and war correspondence (the Crimea, 1854).\n• Tercüman-ı Ahval: a newspaper that Agah Efendi began publishing on 22 October 1860 and of which 792 issues appeared between 1860 and 1866. TDV says that it is regarded as “the beginning of the Turkish press” because it was the first to be published by a Muslim Turkish citizen without aid from the state. The introduction written by the chief writer Şinasi began the tradition of the signed leading article; the aim was a plain Turkish that the people would understand.\n• Tasvir-i Efkâr: 27 June 1862, Şinasi, “the fourth Turkish newspaper”; it bore the words “This is an Ottoman newspaper concerning news and learning” and appeared twice a week.\n\n■ WHO WERE THE FIRST JOURNALISTS\nThe first director of Takvim-i Vekayi, placed at its head in 1832 with the title of director of the Takvim-i Vekayihane-i Amire (Imperial Gazette Office) and the Tabhane-i Mamure (Imperial Printing House), was Esad Efendi; TDV writes, in separate articles, that the gazette's Introduction was written by Sahaflar Şeyhizade Esad Efendi and that an Esad Efendi of the same name was the official chronicler appointed by Mahmud II — it is probable that the two articles mean the same person, but TDV does not explicitly join them (the page's interpretation). News from the Sublime Porte was gathered by Sarım Efendi, and news from the War Ministry (Serasker's office) by Said Bey. Alexandre Blacque worked on the French edition. The owner of Ceride-i Havadis was William Churchill, and among its writers in the early years are counted Münif (later Minister of Education), Ahmed Tevfik, Emin Firdevsi and Karslızade Cemaleddin Mehmed. The owner of Tercüman-ı Ahval was Agah Efendi, and its chief writer Şinasi. In Ceride-i Havadis the articles were unsigned; the writers can be learned from indirect sources.", "511992cf"], "kisa": ["The first newspapers were published by a governor of Egypt, an Englishman and the state itself; the title of “the beginning of the Turkish press”, however, is given to Tercüman-ı Ahval, which appeared without aid from the state.", "0bf59460"]},
"teknik-habes-eyaleti":{"baslik": ["Was the Province of Habesh Abyssinia?", "8ea5b446"], "metin": ["In the Ottoman provincial organization there is a unit called the \"Province of Habesh,\" and its name is misleading: it does not include the Abyssinian kingdom, that is, the highland country we call Ethiopia today. What it includes is the African coast of the Red Sea and the ports of that coast.\n\n■ FOUNDATION\nThe province was officially established on 5 July 1555; the first beylerbeyi (governor-general) was Özdemir Pasha. Its first center was Sawakin, then Massawa came to the fore; in 1559 Debarwa in the interior was established as a military base.\n\n■ WHICH PLACES WERE ATTACHED TO IT\nMassawa, Arkiko, Sawakin, Zeila, Beylul, Aydhab, Berbera and the Dahlak Islands. It was divided into sanjaks such as Akik and Arkiko; in 1573 Ibrim, from Upper Egypt, also joined the province. On paper, the province's reach extended from the Egyptian border to Mombasa in East Africa; but this reach was a coastal strip and a string of ports, not a land country.\n\n■ SO\nThe expression \"Province of Habesh\" derives from the name of the great geography facing that coast; the places the administration actually held were the points that could be supplied from the sea. This also explains the nature of the Ottoman presence in the Red Sea: the aim was not to conquer the highlands but to prevent the Portuguese, and later others, from entering the Red Sea, and to secure the pilgrimage route and the Hejaz. Holding ports suffices for this; holding the interior is unnecessary and expensive.\n\n■ ITS END\nIn the nineteenth century the province was merged with the administration of Jeddah. In 1865 the ports were transferred to the Khedive of Egypt, Ismail Pasha; from the 1880s onward the coast passed into the hands of the Italians and other European powers. The passage of Zeila and Berbera to Britain in 1884 is the last link of a coastal administration of three hundred and thirty years.", "cacaf579"], "kisa": ["Was the Province of Habesh Abyssinia? The name of a province does not have to cover a country.", "8caabfeb"]},
"teknik-hicaz-demiryolu":{"baslik": ["The Hejaz Railway: its construction and destruction", "caa8cbee"], "metin": ["On 1 September 1908 the Hejaz Railway reached Medina. From Damascus to Medina it is 1464 kilometers. Both the construction and the destruction of this line are like a summary of the last thirty years of the Ottomans.\n\n■ THREE AIMS\nThe line carried military, political and religious aims together. Its most concrete promise concerned the pilgrimage: it would reduce a journey of forty to fifty days to four or five days, increase the number of pilgrims and considerably cut costs. Two more things are added to this: being able to get soldiers and news to a distant province, and displaying the caliph's position in the Islamic world with a work visible to the eye.\n\n■ WHERE THE MONEY CAME FROM: here is the real difference\nWhile the other lines of the era were built with foreign concessions and the kilometer guarantee, the Hejaz line was financed through a system of donations. Muslims, statesmen and ordinary people alike, from India to Egypt and from Russia to Morocco, contributed. About one third of the fund's revenue came from donations, and two thirds from sources such as stamps, fees and mining concessions. This choice is no coincidence: a line built by concession would have brought a foreign company into the holy lands.\n\n■ CONSTRUCTION\nOn 2 May 1900 Abdulhamid II ordered the construction, and on 1 September 1900 the official inauguration of the works was held. The line reached Amman in 1903 and Ma'an in 1904; in 1905 the Haifa branch was completed and went into operation. Medina was reached in 1908, and on 1 September 1908 the ceremony opening the line to traffic was arranged by the sultan himself. The line was laid as a narrow-gauge track with a gauge of 1.05 meters; 43 engineers worked on it, 17 of them Turks, 12 Germans and the rest from other countries.\n\n■ WAR AND REVOLT\nIn the First World War the line played a critical role in military transport, and precisely for this reason it became a target. In 1916 Sharif Husayn revolted in cooperation with the British; the line in the desert became the chief target of the revolt. In this geography a railway is the army itself: if you tear up the rails, the army corps is stranded in the desert. The fate of the line thus became one with the fate on the front, and when Medina surrendered on 10 January 1919, Ottoman rule came to an end.\n\n■ WHAT REMAINED\nThe southern section of the line could never again be fully operated. Sleepers left under the sand, overturned locomotives and half-finished stations are the form in which the last great engineering work of an empire has come down to the present. The Damascus-Amman section continued to be used; the section going to Medina, however, came to a halt.", "53446e8a"], "kisa": ["The line that reduced the forty-day pilgrimage road to four days was torn up piece by piece in the desert eight years later.", "a4d96180"]},
"teknik-ilk-nufus-sayimi-1830-31":{"baslik": ["The first census (1830-31) — why only men?", "44901e26"], "metin": ["■ Had there been no counting before?\nKeeping records was not new; what was new was a census that wrote down the whole population according to a single system. From the 15th century onwards the Ottoman state carried out “tahrir” (land and population surveys) in order to establish the social and economic elements of the regions it conquered and thereby to obtain soldiers and taxes; the classical tahrir registers recorded for each sancak (district) the taxable population reckoned by households, its distribution by quarter, village and community, and the revenues. This method was abandoned towards the end of the 16th century; in its place separate registers were kept for the jizya, timar roll-calls and the avarız (extraordinary levies) (Başaran's article reports this on the basis of Öztürk). The census of 1830-31, in the words of one academic study, was the first census to include the Muslim and non-Muslim male population TOGETHER.\n\n■ WHY IT WAS NEEDED\nAccording to TDV, the system, which had been forced to change from the end of the 18th century, needed data for the organisation of taxation, military service and administration. The direct trigger was the abolition of the Janissary Corps in 1826: it was necessary to know the manpower available for the new army (Asâkir-i Mansûre-i Muhammediyye (Victorious Soldiers of Muhammad)) and the economic level of society. The calculation of taxes was the second reason. According to Başaran, a census had been attempted earlier, in 1828-29, but could not be carried out throughout the whole country because of the war with Russia; the operations were started again in 1830-31, after the war.\n\n■ THE SITUATION IN EUROPE\nTDV writes that censuses in the present-day sense began to be taken from the middle of the 18th century and gives the dates: Sweden 1748, Denmark 1769, Spain 1787, the United States of America 1790, England and France 1801. That is, the Ottoman census came about thirty years after the English and French examples and more than eighty years after the Swedish one (the page's calculation). The method of the European censuses, and whether the Ottomans drew inspiration from them, is `not found` in the articles read — TDV gives only the years.\n\n■ WHO AND WHERE WERE COUNTED\nOnly men were written down; on the basis of religion, rough ethnic characteristics and occupational status were determined (TDV). The census could be carried out in Anatolia and in part of Rumelia: the summary (icmal) compiled in 1831 covers the provinces (eyalets) of Anatolia, Karaman, Çıldır, the Islands of the White Sea (Cezair-i Bahr-i Sefid), Rumelia and Silistra. For places that could not be counted, separate attempts were made later; for example, in the Erzurum and Van regions the census could be completed only in 1836.\n\n■ THE FIGURES\nAccording to TDV's table, in the census of 1830 the total male population in Anatolia and Rumelia was 3,753,642 (Anatolia 2,383,876, Rumelia 1,369,766). Religious and ethnic distribution: Muslim 2,501,425 · Christian reaya (tax-paying subjects) 1,178,171 · Roma (Kıptî) 36,675 · Jewish 17,012 · Armenian 20,309. In the Rumelia column, non-Muslim reaya men (811,546) outnumber Muslim men (513,448). For the regions where the census was taken, the total population according to the 1831 summary appears to be about 7.5 million. Note: the row and column totals in the table fail to agree with one another by a few hundred persons; the card reports the table's own “Total” row. It is also striking that 7.5 million is close to twice 3.75 million men, but TDV does not write how it made this calculation — this is an observation, the page's interpretation.\n\n■ HOW THE FIGURES WERE OBTAINED\nAn academic study examining the registers of Ödemiş, relying on the finding of Enver Ziya Karal (1943), relates the following: so as not to alarm the people, the state recorded those fit for military service with labels such as “matlûb-ı âliye muvâfık” (conforming to what is required) or “marked with the sign of the letter mim”; the Muslim population was generally divided into two, “muvâfık” (suitable) and “gayri muvâfık” (unsuitable), and within the unsuitable part subgroups such as old man, child, water carrier and amel-mande (incapable of work) were sometimes also distinguished. The registers contain information on age, occupation and disability; the census was not taken all at once but over a long period of time, and records of births, deaths and migration were updated in red ink. According to TDV's article on the Ceride Nezareti (Ministry of Registers), the statistical information that census officials sent from the places they visited was collected in Istanbul; in order to preserve these registers, to recalculate taxes according to the records and to follow changes in population, the Ceride Nezareti was established in 1831 (Said Efendi, the chief secretary (mektupçu) of the Bab-ı Defteri (finance department), became its director (nazır) with a monthly salary of 7500 kuruş).\n\n■ AFTERWARDS\nIt was decided that the whole male population should be written down and that the registers of changes (vukuat) should be sent to Istanbul every three months; although population directors were appointed to the sancaks, population officials to the kazas (sub-districts) and registrars to the nahiyes (townships), the desired result could not be obtained. The provincial organisation was later attached first to the administrations of Tahrir-i Emlak (property survey) and shortly afterwards to those of Ahz-ı Asker (military recruitment), and lost its real function. TDV says that the Ottoman state possessed population information that was “for the first time general and reliable” only with the Population Registry Administration (Sicill-i Nüfus İdaresi), established in 1881.", "19265f40"], "kisa": ["When the Janissary Corps was abolished, the state had no list of the people with whom to build its new army — the first regular census was taken primarily for military service and taxation, counting only men.", "b9a5d5c3"]},
"teknik-iltizam-sistemi":{"baslik": ["The tax-farming (iltizam) system — a state that put taxes up for sale", "c315c39d"], "metin": ["■ How iltizam worked\nIn classical tax farming, the right to collect the taxes of a district was sold by auction; the term was short, generally covering a period (tahvil) of one to three years for the revenue unit (mukataa). The tax farmer (mültezim, the contractor) undertook to pay the state a sum in advance or in instalments, and in return collected and kept the taxes of that district.\n\n■ WHY IT TURNED INTO A CRISIS\nThe prolonged wars of the late 17th century drained the treasury, while territorial losses reduced revenue. Tax farmers with short-term contracts taxed the reaya heavily in order to recover their investment quickly, which in turn eroded productive capacity — a vicious circle had arisen in which taxation and production choked each other.\n\n■ MALİKÂNE (1695) — A PROPOSED SOLUTION\nIn place of short terms sold at auction, from 1695 the state introduced the ‘malikâne’ method: the mukataa was now granted for life (on condition of the holder's survival), the annual tax was FIXED, and a large down payment called the ‘muaccele’ was paid to the state in advance. Since the tax farmer would now expand production and keep for himself the surplus above his fixed obligation, he acquired an interest in improving the land and extending credit to the reaya — in theory, the interests of the state, the tax farmer and the producer were drawn in the same direction.\n\n■ COMPARISON WITH OTHER STATES — NOT MEASURED\nIt is general historical knowledge that in this period France (the ferme générale) and other European states also applied similar methods of tax farming, but the TDV source read for this card (the article on malikâne) does NOT ITSELF make this comparison — it gives neither comparative figures nor a direct reference. Under the rule in §4, this comparison has NOT been written here; it requires a separate survey of academic sources, `bulunamadı` (not found).", "0b3d7303"], "kisa": ["The state did not collect the tax itself but put it up for sale — and when this model of sale produced its own crisis a century later, the state began to sell the same business ‘for life’.", "a3e5c408"]},
"teknik-istanbul-depremleri":{"baslik": ["The earthquakes of Istanbul: from 1509 to 1894", "cb87ec30"], "metin": ["The history of Istanbul is punctuated by tremors as much as by conquests and changes of reign. Among the great Istanbul and Marmara earthquakes, the dates 1509, 1719, 1766 and 1894 are mentioned in particular.\n\n■ 1509: \"THE LITTLE DOOMSDAY\"\nThe earthquake that struck the city on 14 September 1509 was called by exactly this name among the people of its time. Heavy damage occurred to the walls, the mosques and many buildings, and there was great loss of life. On the order of Bayezid II, a large-scale campaign of repair and construction was launched in the city.\n\n■ 1766: THE COLLAPSE OF THE FATIH MOSQUE\nIn the great earthquake of May 1766, the main dome of the Fatih Mosque collapsed completely, and its walls were damaged beyond repair. The reconstruction, begun in 1767 on the order of Mustafa III under the direction of the architects Sarım İbrahim Efendi and İzzet Mehmed Bey, was completed in 1771 with a new design that differed from the old plan. In other words, the Fatih Mosque seen today is the product of an earthquake.\n\n■ 1894: THE EPICENTER IN THE GULF\nThe earthquake that occurred off the Gulf of İzmit on 10 July 1894 hit Istanbul hard; many mosques and historic buildings, including the walls, were damaged. The figures on the earthquake's loss of life are muddled across the sources, and no number is given here.\n\n■ HOW WAS THE MEANING OF AN EARTHQUAKE READ\nThe Ottoman administration treated great tremors together with a moral warning: after an earthquake the people were warned away from sin, and sometimes wine and entertainment were banned. Among the people, an earthquake was considered a sign of the Day of Judgment and was thought of together with the descriptions of the Last Day in the Quran. For this reason, earthquake records are not merely lists of destruction but also documents of the worldview of an era.\n\n■ HOW DO WE KNOW\nThe record of these events was kept in chronicles, official decrees and repair registers. In which year and by which architect a building was rebuilt often tells the severity of an earthquake more precisely than written descriptions do.", "e23db764"], "kisa": ["How many times in four hundred years is a city destroyed and rebuilt?", "611d6550"]},
"teknik-kaime-kagit-para":{"baslik": ["Kaime — the first Ottoman paper money", "c8db2211"], "metin": ["■ What is the kaime\nKāime literally means “that which stands, that which takes the place of something”; it received this name because it circulated as a representative of coin. In Ottoman finance it was a developed form of the esham system applied earlier, and it differed from esham in being used without a berat (warrant) (for esham, see card: the chain of tax-farming (iltizam), malikâne and esham).\n\n■ HOW IT WAS ISSUED IN 1840\nIn the first years of the Tanzimat, while ways of finding revenue for the treasury were being discussed, the proposal came from Ârif Hikmet Bey, who later became Shaykh al-Islam, and it was accepted. Because the need for money was urgent, the kaimes were put into circulation written by hand, without waiting for printing plates to be made and for them to be printed. The first kaimes, totalling 160,000 liras (32,000 purses), were issued in the time of the Minister of Finance Sâib Pasha, “probably in June 1840” (the TDV is not certain about the month either). The term of circulation was eight years and the interest 12.5% a year; the interest was paid in two instalments. The interest was intended to make the kaime more attractive than the circulating esham. The kaime had no backing; only for the interest to be paid on it was security provided from the revenue of the Istanbul customs.\n\n■ HOW IT WORKED\nBecause the people were not accustomed to such a practice, the kaimes were not well received; being handwritten, they immediately attracted the attention of counterfeiters, and it was not easy to tell a forgery apart. After a while their circulation in the provinces was banned. Over the two years from the beginning of 1841, the handwritten kaimes were replaced with printed ones; to prevent imitation they were reprinted in different forms in 1844 and 1847. The interest was reduced in 1843 from 12.5% to 10%, and later to 6%. In 1851, 100,000 liras of interest-bearing kaime were withdrawn and replaced with INTEREST-FREE kaime of 20 piastres — the TDV considers this important in that the kaime truly became a medium of exchange. In March 1854, for the Crimean War, army kaimes of 10 and 20 piastres, valid only in the war zone, were prepared (856,250 liras, withdrawn in 1857); in 1856 the interest-bearing kaime in circulation reached 1,720,000 liras. The TDV also writes that on many occasions the state issued kaime in secret from the public.\n\n■ THE END OF THE FIRST ISSUE: 1861-1862\nAttempts to abolish it (the compulsory “iâne-i umûmiyye” general contribution of 1848, the sterling loan of 1858) failed because the money collected was spent on other things. On 13 December 1861 the price of the kaime in Istanbul fell extraordinarily and it ceased to be accepted in the market; tradesmen and merchants closed their shops in panic, and in the mosques the public was urged that the shops be opened. Through the work of Fuad Pasha, who was appointed grand vizier with sole authority over financial matters, it was decided to abolish the kaime with a loan obtained from Britain: 40% of the value would be paid in cash and the remainder in eshâm-ı cedîde (new esham) bearing 6% interest; at the beginning of September 1862 it was officially announced that it had been withdrawn from circulation.\n\n■ THE SECOND ISSUE: 1876-1878\nIt was resorted to again to finance the disturbances in the Balkans and the Ottoman-Russian War of 1877-1878. In 1863 the authority and monopoly for issuing kaime had been transferred to the Imperial Ottoman Bank (Bank-ı Osmânî-yi Şâhâne). This time series and serial numbers were added; it was put into circulation first at the front, and on 28 August 1876 in Istanbul. The total amount of kaime rose to 16 million liras; the commission that began work on 1 April 1878 burned the collected kaimes in public squares. That the state's revenue in 1879 was almost as much as the kaime (18.5 million liras) is, according to the TDV, a measure of its desperation.\n\n■ PAPER MONEY IN THE WORLD: FOUR EXAMPLES\n• China (Song dynasty): paper money began at the end of the 10th century in Sichuan with the “jiaozi”, which private financial firms issued in exchange for coin, silk and other valuables; when the firms went bankrupt, the state took over printing in 1023. In the 13th century, when the threat of invasion increased military spending, the Song's excessive printing led to serious loss of value (Richard von Glahn, Hoover Institution).\n• Sweden: Europe's first banknotes were issued in 1661 by Stockholms Banco, founded in 1657. The reasons were that the heavy copper plate money (a 10-daler piece weighed about 20 kg) could not be carried, and that copper had lost value against silver; Johan Palmstruch requested permission to issue “kreditivsedlar” (credit notes). The notes were for round sums, made out to the bearer and payable on demand (Museum of the National Bank of Belgium).\n• England: the Bank of England was founded in 1694 as a private bank to act as banker to the state and to finance the war against France; before 1725 the banknotes were handwritten, and in 1725 partly printed notes were issued for sums of 20 pounds sterling and above (Bank of England).\n• France: the assignat appeared in December 1789 with a first issue of 400 million livres backed by the confiscated lands of the Church; each new issue increased the loss of value, in 1793 it became almost unacceptable, and in February 1796 the printing plates were destroyed in front of the public (New York Fed, Liberty Street Economics).\nIn the Chinese and French examples, the sources state that excessive printing led to loss of value; in the case of the Ottoman kaime too, the TDV describes the falls of 1861 and 1877. The inference linking these three cases — that printing which is unbacked and secretly increased destroys confidence — is this site's interpretation; none of the sources consulted makes this comparison for the Ottomans.", "0611c162"], "kisa": ["The first Ottoman banknote was written by hand, paid interest, was disliked by the people and became the first target of counterfeiters — while in the world at large the story of paper money goes back a thousand years.", "c3e72906"]},
"teknik-kanun-i-esasi-1876-sistem":{"baslik": ["The Kanun-i Esasi (1876): what did the first constitution provide for?", "2d1c3914"], "metin": ["■ How it was drafted\nThe text was prepared stage by stage in a special commission chaired by Midhat Pasha, a body made up of ulema, soldiers and bureaucrats; Western constitutions were drawn upon. It was proclaimed on 23 December 1876. It contains 119 articles under twelve headings.\n\n■ WHAT THE SYSTEM PROVIDED FOR\n• The sultanate and the caliphate belong to the eldest male member of the Ottoman dynasty.\n• The sultan is granted broad powers, among them diplomacy and the supreme command of the army.\n• For legislation, a two-chamber General Assembly (Meclis-i Umumi) is established: the elected Chamber of Deputies and the Chamber of Notables, whose members are appointed by the sultan.\nIn other words, the system is not a parliamentary regime but a monarchy with an assembly: there is a parliament, but the government is responsible not to the parliament but to the sultan.\n\n■ ARTICLE 113\nThe most debated provision of the constitution is Article 113, which gave the sultan the authority to send into exile persons deemed dangerous to the security of the state. Figures such as Namık Kemal and Ziya Pasha sharply criticized this article even at the time. The dispersal, through the power of exile, of the very circle that had wanted constitutional government shows how this article worked.\n\n■ THE LIFESPAN OF THE PARLIAMENT\nThe parliament began work in March 1877 and was dissolved in 1878; the constitution remained suspended for thirty years. It came back into force with the proclamation of the Second Constitutional Era in 1908. The real transformation came with the amendments of 1909: the sultan's powers were markedly curtailed and the government was made responsible to the parliament. That is, the monarchy with an assembly founded in 1876 only came close to a parliamentary system in 1909.\n\n■ WHILE READING\nIt is misleading to read the 1876 text as meaning “there is a constitution, so power is limited.” The measure of limitation lies not in the existence of the text but in who controls the executive; looked at by this measure, 1876 and 1909 are two distinct regimes.", "8fa51fa1"], "kisa": ["The first Ottoman constitution established a parliament, but the same text also wrote in the sultan's power to exile whomever he wished.", "cfced1bc"]},
"teknik-klasik-burokrasi-nisanci-kalem-defterhane":{"baslik": ["Nişancı, chancery and Defterhane — the classical bureaucracy", "4fe59c09"], "metin": ["■ The three pillars of the classical structure\nThe main outlines of the ranks of the classical state, from the grand vizier to the kazasker (military judge) and from the defterdar (treasurer) to the Shaykh al-Islam, are described in a separate card (see card: the ranks of the Ottoman state). This card adds the piece missing there: the office of the nişancı (chancellor) and the “kalem” (chancery bureau) system that worked alongside it. According to the TDV article on the nişancı, at the beginning of the 15th century the office of the defterdar emerged as a separate institution to conduct financial business; in the reign of the Conqueror (1451-1481) the third pillar of the bureaucracy, the Defterhâne-i Âmire (Imperial Registry), was established. The nişancı, for his part, was the member of the Dîvân-ı Hümâyun (Imperial Council) who supervised bureaucratic business and drew the sultan's tughra (monogram).\n\n■ WHAT THE NİŞANCI DID\nHis principal duty was to draw the sultan's tughra on documents such as fermans (decrees), nâmes (royal letters), ahidnames and berats (warrants of appointment). Until the beginning of the 18th century, because they were officials who knew the laws of the state and proposed new ones when necessary, they were also called “müftî-i kānun” (mufti of the law); nişancıs (Karamânî Mehmed Pasha, Celâlzâde Mustafa Çelebi) played a major part in preparing the Law Code of the Conqueror and in the codification movement of the reign of Suleiman the Lawgiver. Corrections in the tahrir registers (survey registers) could be made only by the nişancı; since these registers counted as conclusive evidence in disputes over timars and land, this power was important. When nişancıs and defterdars were chosen for office, they were not given the berat that was issued to other statesmen; they were appointed solely by the ruler's oral command.\n\n■ WHO BECAME NİŞANCI\nUntil the beginning of the 16th century, men of the ilmiye (learned religious) class with a strong command of chancery composition were chosen; later, as specialisation developed within the kalemiye (scribal service), professional bureaucrats began to be appointed. In some cases the office of nişancı and the rank of vizier of the dome were combined in a single person. From the 18th century onwards, rising from the office of nişancı to high positions such as the grand vizierate came to an end; nişancıs joined the officials of hâcegân (senior bureaucrat) rank who were subject to the system of annual appointment (the appointments made in the month of Shawwal), and on paper held a place among the state's six high offices called the “menâsıb-ı sitte” (the three defterdars, the nişancı, the reisülküttab and the superintendent of the registry).\n\n■ THE NİŞANCI'S PLACE AS THE CENTRE SHIFTED\nFrom the middle of the 17th century, when the Paşakapısı (Sublime Porte) became the new centre of administration in place of the Dîvân-ı Hümâyun, the Reisülküttab, the steward of the grand vizierate (sadâret kethüdâsı) and the beylikçi (head of the Divan chancery) became the most important bureaucrats; according to the TDV, the office of reisülküttab was regarded as head of the bureaucracy in place of the nişancı, but in official precedence it was kept below the nişancı. The superintendent of the registry (defter emini), who headed the Defterhâne-i Âmire, also became in time the nişancı's equal and took over part of his work.\n\n■ 1836 AND AFTER\nThe TDV writes that after the abolition of the office of nişancı in 1836, the task of drawing the tughra was given to the superintendent of the registry; however, it is “not clearly evident” whether the office of nişancı was abolished entirely in 1836 or only the tughra work was taken from it. Corrections in the tahrir registers are still seen to have been made by nişancıs, with the signature “tevkī‘”, until 1908; this indicates that the post existed until the last days of the empire. In other words, the institution was not wiped out in a single day; on the road to the Tanzimat its functions were distributed among the new ministries and the Sublime Porte. For the details, see the separate card: from the sultan's council to the ministries (1836-1841).", "7c94bc7f"], "kisa": ["Drawing the tughra, the sultan's signature, was a government post — the nişancı was the “chancery” pillar of the classical state, and in 1836 he gave up his place to a ministry.", "53d53c63"]},
"teknik-klasik-donemde-dis-borc-var-miydi":{"baslik": ["Was there foreign debt in the classical period? The first loan, 1854", "1841efc4"], "metin": ["■ The answer to the question\nThe TDV article on the Ottoman Public Debt Administration (Düyûn-ı Umûmiyye) answers the question directly: despite the frequent shortages of money and budget deficits, Ottoman statesmen strenuously avoided borrowing money from abroad; the few attempts made in this direction before the Tanzimat also failed. Foreign debt, then, is a matter of modern times, of the post-Tanzimat era. For the “classical period” (roughly the 15th-17th centuries), a record of any loan taken from a foreign state or bank was `not found` in the articles consulted; this does not mean ‘there never was any’, it is only the finding of this reading.\n\n■ CLOSING THE DEFICIT FROM WITHIN: THREE INSTRUMENTS\nWhen the state needed cash, it turned to its own subjects:\n• Esham (1775 to the 1860s): in this method, which the TDV calls an “internal borrowing system”, a part of the revenues of mukātaa (revenue farm units), called the “faiz” (surplus), was divided into slices called “sehim” (shares) and sold to private persons, in return for an advance payment (muaccele), as an income for life. Every subject, man or woman, Muslim or non-Muslim, member of the military class or reaya (tax-paying subjects), could buy shares (for details see card: the chain of tax-farming (iltizam), malikâne and esham).\n• Sarrafs: bankers who lent money to statesmen, who from the 1760s, especially in war years, financed the treasury with short-term loans, and who stood as guarantors in tax-farming and malikâne. Because the credits they extended to the state in the last quarter of the 18th century were not repaid, some sarrafs went bankrupt (TDV: sarraflik).\n• Kaime (1840): unbacked, interest-bearing domestic paper money (see card: kaime).\n\n■ THE FIRST ATTEMPT: 1850-51\nWhen, in the fiscal year 1850-51, the treasury reached a point where it could not even pay salaries, Mustafa Reşid Pasha signed the first foreign loan agreement. When Fethi Ahmed Pasha and Damad Mehmed Ali Pasha, brothers-in-law of Sultan Abdülmecid, explained to the sultan the dangers that foreign borrowing would bring, the agreement was not ratified; the treasury paid 2,200,000 liras in compensation and cancelled the contract.\n\n■ 24 AUGUST 1854: THE FIRST CONTRACT\nThe Crimean War (1853-1856), which began with the support of Britain and France, who feared that Russia would seize control of Mediterranean trade, shook the finances still further; the Ottoman administrators accepted the offers of credit from Britain and France and signed the first loan agreement on 24 August 1854 — in the TDV's words, this was the beginning of the era of foreign borrowing. As the loan did not cover war expenses, a second agreement was signed on 27 June 1855; the Egyptian tribute and the customs revenues of Syria and İzmir were pledged as security for these two loans. According to the TDV's table, the 1854 loan had a principal of 3,300,000 liras, of which 2,640,000 liras were received, at 6% interest; the 1855 loan had a principal of 5,500,000 liras, of which 5,644,375 liras were received, at 4% interest (the 1855 bonds were sold above their face value; the TDV notes this as the sole exception).\n\n■ WHAT HAPPENED NEXT\nFrom 1854 to 1874 fifteen foreign loan agreements were signed; although the total principal was 238,773,272 liras, because the bonds were sold at low prices and because of commissions, the amount actually received was 127,120,220 liras. Only 7.8% of these loans was spent on the Rumelian railway; the greater part went to closing the budget deficit, to instalments on domestic and foreign debts and to withdrawing the depreciating paper and copper money. The article on the sarrafs adds: the banker families in Istanbul (Baltazzi, Zarifi, Camondo and others) lent to the state, as short-term, high-interest advances, money they had obtained from European financial circles at low interest and on long terms. By the decree of 6 October 1875 it was announced that half of each foreign debt instalment would be paid and new bonds issued for the other half; in April 1876 payments stopped. The Ottoman Public Debt Administration, representing the creditors, was established by the Decree of Muharram of 1881. The TDV writes that foreign borrowing, which began in 1854, lasted exactly a hundred years when its continuation under the Republic's agreement of 1928 is also taken into account.", "d9be6a9a"], "kisa": ["Ottoman administrators strenuously avoided borrowing from abroad; even the first agreement, signed in 1850-51, was not ratified — the first loan contract was made on 24 August 1854.", "6b5fbd75"]},
"teknik-meclis-i-vala-yi-ahkam-i-adliyye":{"baslik": ["Meclis-i Vâlâ — a council that both made laws and sat in judgement", "8f665c40"], "metin": ["■ Founding\nThe Meclis-i Vâlâ (Supreme Council of Judicial Ordinances) was founded on 24 March 1838; one of the former seraskers (commanders-in-chief), Koca Hüsrev Pasha, was made its president, and it had five members. After a ceremony held in the presence of the sultan and the Shaykh al-Islam, it began work on 31 March 1838; its place of work was the Gülhane Pavilion. Its task was to prepare the laws and regulations needed to carry out the reforms known as “tanzîmât-ı hayriyye, tanzîmât-ı mülkiyye” (beneficial reorganisation, civil reorganisation), to give final form to the reports (mazbata) drawn up by the Dâr-ı Şûrâ-yı Bâbıâlî (Consultative Council of the Sublime Porte) and the Dâr-ı Şûrâ-yı Askerî (Military Council), and to check whether the laws it issued were being applied. Its internal rules provided that members should express their views freely and that decisions should be taken by majority vote.\n\n■ WHAT THE STRUCTURE WAS BEFORE\nIn the classical order, decisions were taken in the Dîvân-ı Hümâyun (Imperial Council) and later in the grand vizier's council at the Sublime Porte (see card: the Ottoman administrative structure and its transformation); legislation and adjudication were not separate institutions but the business of the same council. The TDV writes that apart from the Consultative Assembly (Meşveret Meclisi), the practice of an “institutionalised assembly” was not widespread, and that for this reason the council's work faltered in its early years: members also continued in their former posts, and because of differences in rank, lower-ranking members could not voice opinions in the presence of their superiors; the ranks were equalised, the members were given distinct decorations, and their place in official precedence was fixed.\n\n■ AFTER GÜLHANE: THE CENTRE OF THE TANZIMAT\nOwing to confusion of jurisdiction with the Dâr-ı Şûrâ-yı Bâbıâlî, that council was abolished on 12 August 1839, and some of its members were transferred to the Meclis-i Vâlâ. After the Gülhane Imperial Rescript, the task of identifying and deliberating on reforms was given to the Meclis-i Vâlâ and the number of its members was increased; on 8 March 1840 it moved to a new building constructed at the Sublime Porte. The TDV also stresses the council's task of trying high-ranking statesmen who acted contrary to the Tanzimat: Hüsrev Pasha, the former minister of the interior Âkif Pasha, Nâfiz Pasha, Tâhir Pasha and Hasib Pasha were tried and punished in the council; opponents were silenced with the charge of “conduct contrary to the Tanzîmât-ı Hayriyye”. The TDV's summary: the council was the “true protector” of the Tanzimat and, together with the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, the planner and overseer of the reforms.\n\n■ WHAT IS ITS MODERN EQUIVALENT\nIt is difficult to match it to a single institution, because the Meclis-i Vâlâ did three jobs at once: preparing laws and regulations, supervising their application and trying senior bureaucrats. In time these jobs were separated: on 26 September 1854 the Meclis-i Âlî-i Tanzîmat (High Council of Reforms) was established and took over legislative authority; the Meclis-i Vâlâ dealt with matters such as the dismissal and appointment of minor officials, inheritance, disciplinary cases and land disputes. On 15 July 1861 the two councils were merged under the name Meclis-i Ahkâm-ı Adliyye (president: Keçecizâde Fuad Pasha). In March 1868 (two TDV articles give the dates 5 and 6 March) this council was divided into two: the Şûrâ-yı Devlet (Council of State; president: Midhat Pasha) and the Dîvân-ı Ahkâm-ı Adliyye (Court of Judicial Ordinances; president: Ahmed Cevdet Pasha). The TDV writes that with this arrangement legislation and the judiciary were separated from each other, and that the Şûrâ-yı Devlet laid the foundation of today's Council of State (Danıştay) and the Dîvân-ı Ahkâm-ı Adliyye that of the Court of Cassation (Yargıtay).\n\n■ HOW ITS MEMBERS WERE CHOSEN\nAccording to the TDV, membership was conferred as an “office” (mansıp), and at one period, because members were appointed through favouritism, their number rose to forty and the reports being prepared could not be signed because of the disorder; the number was subsequently reduced to fourteen (1858). So it was not an elected assembly in today's sense but a board formed by appointment (this site's interpretation: an inference from the TDV's statements).", "e41d7192"], "kisa": ["The council that prepared the laws of the Tanzimat was at the same time the court that tried pashas who acted against it — thirty years later it was split in two, and its two parts became the foundations of the Council of State and the Court of Cassation.", "b2f45158"]},
"teknik-muhendishane-ardil-okullar":{"baslik": ["The later life of the two engineering schools: Istanbul Technical University, the War College and the Naval Academy", "d02e4311"], "metin": ["■ Mühendishâne-i Berrî-i Hümâyun (Imperial School of Military Engineering, 1795) — the common root of Istanbul Technical University (İTÜ) and the War College (Harbiye)\nTwenty years after the naval engineering school, it was founded at Hasköy in 1210 (1795) to train land engineers; in TDV's own words, it was 'one of the most important institutions of the Nizam-i Cedid'. The first instructors were entirely Turkish — headed by Abdurrahman Efendi, with NO foreign teachers; in 1801 an English engineer who had converted to Islam (known by the names Selim/Bailey) joined the staff. The curriculum covered fortification, map and land surveying (cadastre), artillery and advanced mathematics extending to differential and integral calculus, as well as French and Arabic lessons.\n\n■ THE ZIGZAG PATH OF THE LAND SCHOOL\nIn 1806 it moved to Eyüp and was separated from the military unit to which it had been attached; in 1808 it returned to its old building at Hasköy. It went through a period of neglect between 1826 and 1839 and was revived by the reforms of Mahmud II. In 1883 the Hendese-i Mülkiyye Mektebi (civil engineering school) was opened under it, and in 1908 a separate civil engineering school was established. Between 1928 and 1944 it passed through various educational reforms.\n\n■ TWO SEPARATE OUTCOMES, ONE ROOT\nTDV describes this institution as 'the first nucleus of Istanbul Technical University' — İTÜ was officially founded on this legacy in 1944. The same article also states that the Mühendishâne-i Berrî formed 'the first nucleus of the War College as well' — that is, a SINGLE institution of 1795 branched into two separate lines, one civilian (İTÜ) and one military (Harbiye/Military Academy of the Land Forces).\n\n■ MÜHENDİSHÂNE-İ BAHRÎ-İ HÜMÂYUN (1775) — THE ROOT OF THE NAVAL ACADEMY\nThe naval engineering school founded in 1775 (see the separate card) took the name Mühendishâne-i Tersâne-i Âmire (Engineering School of the Imperial Arsenal) in 1195 (1781); in 1210 (1795) it separated from the land school and concentrated solely on naval education; in 1830 it moved to Heybeliada, and in 1846 this move became permanent. TDV regards this institution as the foundation of today's Naval Academy.\n\n■ A COMMON PATTERN\nBoth engineering schools were born of the same crisis (the Russian war of 1768-1774, Çeşme in 1770), were part of the same reform package (the Nizam-i Cedid, see the separate card), and both lasted beyond the 19th century while changing names and buildings many times — not without interruption, but they reached the 20th century WITHOUT BREAKING OFF.", "5fe5f2db"], "kisa": ["Of the two engineering schools, one became the first nucleus of a present-day university, the other of a present-day military academy — and both were born of the same wind of the Nizam-i Cedid.", "103a7008"]},
"teknik-muhendishane-i-bahri-i-humayun":{"baslik": ["The Imperial Naval Engineering School (Mühendishâne-i Bahrî-i Hümâyun, 1775): the first modern school, born of Çeşme", "ecd18934"], "metin": ["■ Why it was founded\nThe technical shortcomings in the Ottoman-Russian War of 1768-1774, and in particular the near annihilation of the fleet in the raid at Çeşme in 1770, clearly revealed the need for trained naval officers. The Mühendishâne-i Bahrî-i Hümâyun was founded in response to this need in the reign of Abdülhamid I, during the third grand vizierate of Grand Vizier Derviş Mehmed Pasha.\n\n■ TWO DIFFERENT DATES — THE SOURCE CONTRADICTS ITSELF\nThe TDV article gives the foundation with two different dates and does not reconcile them: according to French archival documents the opening was on 29 April 1775; according to the Ottoman record, however, it was in Jumada al-Awwal 1189 (approximately July 1775) — a difference of about two months. This card reports both and takes no side.\n\n■ THE FIRST STAFF — AND A BARON\nThe first director was Baron de Tott, a Frenchman of Hungarian origin who had earlier become known in Ottoman service for his artillery reforms. The first students were chosen from among former captains and the children of high-ranking officials; the first teacher of mathematics was Gilles Jean-Marie Brazzer de Kermovan, and the first head teacher was Seyyid Hasan Bey of Algiers (he left within a year to become a captain because of the Russian war and was executed in 1787).\n\n■ CURRICULUM: FROM MATHEMATICS TO NAVAL ENGINEERING\nAt first the instruction was mainly mathematics; at the request of Cezayirli (the Algerian) Gazi Hasan Pasha it was shifted towards naval engineering, geometry and geography. In 1190 (1776) a regulation was drawn up and ten students were enrolled.\n\n■ FRENCH ENGINEERS\nIn 1198 (1784), during the grand vizierate of Halil Hamid Pasha, the school was reorganised, and French engineers such as Lafitte-Clavé and Monnier joined the staff. Jacques Balthazar Brun established and directed the shipbuilding department between 1793 and 1798; Benoît took charge of the shipbuilding classes and Parale of the practical classes. The Ottoman teachers listed are Seyyid Osman, Gelenbevi İsmail, Palabıyık Mehmed, Ali Bahar, Mehmed Salih, Kasabbaşızade İbrahim and Mehmed Ruhuddin Efendi.\n\n■ 1195 → THE NAME CHANGED, 1210 → SPLIT IN TWO\nIn 1195 (1781) the school took the name Mühendishâne-i Tersâne-i Âmire (Engineering School of the Imperial Arsenal). In 1210 (1795) the training in land engineering was separated and a distinct institution was founded under the name Mühendishâne-i Berrî-i Hümâyun (Imperial School of Military Engineering) — from that date naval and land engineering were two separate schools. In 1212 (1797) the courses were organised into four groups: cartography and geography, navigation, shipbuilding, and fortification.\n\n■ DECLINE AND RELOCATION\nWith the end of the Nizam-i Cedid in 1807, instruction was neglected for a time. In 1830 the school moved to Heybeliada, and in 1846 this move became permanent.\n\n■ HISTORICAL SIGNIFICANCE\nThe TDV describes the institution as ‘the first school founded in the modern sense’ in the history of Turkish education; it forms the foundation of today's Naval Academy.", "f2cc78b1"], "kisa": ["From the ashes of a fleet burnt at Çeşme a school was born — its first director was not an Ottoman but a French baron of Hungarian origin.", "23a330cd"]},
"teknik-narh-defterleri":{"baslik": ["Narh registers — who set the prices?", "4ff47a60"], "metin": ["■ What it was for\nNarh was a state practice that set price ceilings on basic goods and services; its purpose was to ensure that the people could shop at reasonable prices (especially for foodstuffs).\n\n■ WHO SET THEM\nThe authority that fixed the price was the kadi (judge) of Istanbul, but not on his own: the opinion of the relevant tradesmen (the guild) was sought. For items such as meat, dairy products and bread, the agha of the Janissaries or other military officials and the guild stewards (kethüda) also took part in the process; compliance in the market was monitored by the muhtesib (market inspector).\n\n■ THE REGISTERS THEMSELVES\nOnly two narh registers have survived to the present day: those dated 1600 and 1640. The 1640 register is particularly comprehensive — it is almost a complete market handbook, containing not only food and clothing but also service prices and standards of hygiene.\n\n■ SEASONAL AND EXTRAORDINARY CHANGE\nPrices were reset according to the season (as with dairy products in summer and winter), after the harvest, and in extraordinary circumstances such as war, blockade, famine or natural disaster; changes in the standard of the coinage (see the separate card: debasement) also triggered a general revision of prices.\n\n■ ITS END\nThe kadis' authority in this field lasted until the middle of the 19th century. A Price Office established in 1851 proved ineffective and in 1854 gave way to a Narh Council attached to the Şehremaneti (city prefecture). By 1856 price records had ceased to appear in the court registers, and narh was largely abolished, with everything other than vegetables, fruit and bread left to the free market.", "4d02c9db"], "kisa": ["A guild master and the kadi would sit at the same table and decide the price of bread — and the price of milk in winter was different from its price in summer.", "1716ee6a"]},
"teknik-nizam-i-cedid":{"baslik": ["Nizam-i Cedid (New Order, 1792-1807): its causes, content, opposition and end", "01777ff9"], "metin": ["■ Cause — the unanimous vote at Shumen (Şumnu)\nThe heavy defeats in the Ottoman-Russian/Austrian war of 1787-1792 and the Treaty of Sistova (with Austria) plainly exposed the institutional weakness of the army and the leading men of state. The upper ranks of the army and the administration met at Shumen on 11 August 1791 and decided UNANIMOUSLY that the war could not be continued — this was the direct turning point that pushed Selim III towards a comprehensive reform programme.\n\n■ CONTENT — REORGANIZING EVERYTHING, CIVIL AND MILITARY\nThe Nizam-i Cedid (1792-1807) comprised the creation of a new army trained in European discipline; the opening of a separate treasury, the İrâd-ı Cedîd (New Revenue), to meet the costs of this army; the founding of the Mühendishâne-i Berrî-i Hümâyun (1795) and the reform of the Mühendishâne-i Bahrî-i Hümâyun (1775); the opening of a printing press attached to the engineering school (1797); the construction of new barracks in Istanbul and Anatolia; and the reorganization of the grain supervisorate (grain administration) so as to prevent abuse. At the head of the reform cadre were bureaucrats such as Grand Vizier Koca Yusuf Pasha and Mustafa Reşid; on the side of the ulema, figures such as Tatarcık Abdullah Efendi supported the process.\n\n■ OPPOSITION — 'BID'AH' AND THE TAX BURDEN\nThe Janissaries, the traditional military groups and conservative circles saw the reform as bid'ah (innovation contrary to religion) and 'Europeanization'; in the provinces, meanwhile, the heavy taxes that financed the new army provoked popular resentment.\n\n■ OUTCOME — THE KABAKÇI REVOLT\nThe revolt that broke out on 25-29 May 1807 under the leadership of Kabakçı Mustafa, with the support of Shaykh al-Islam Topal Ataullah, abolished the Nizam-i Cedid, deposed Selim III and ended in the execution of the leaders of the reform.\n\n■ SIGNIFICANCE AND IMPACT — A DEFEAT, BUT A LASTING EFFECT\nAlthough it collapsed institutionally, the Nizam-i Cedid prepared the ground for the later Tanzimat reforms and for Mahmud II's own reorganization, especially the Auspicious Incident (Vaka-i Hayriye) of 1826 (the abolition of the Janissary corps) — Mahmud II completed the unfinished programme of Selim III, this time crushing the same resistance by military force.\n\n■ AN INTERESTING DETAIL — NOT FROM THE EXISTING TAXES BUT FROM A NEW TREASURY\nInstead of laying an additional burden on the existing tekâlif system (see the separate card: the Ottoman tax system), the reform was financed by establishing a SEPARATE treasury (İrâd-ı Cedîd) — in other words, Selim III tried to finance the new army within a self-sufficient financial structure without touching the old tax order.", "71b4bc18"], "kisa": ["An army admitted it was beaten not on the battlefield but by unanimously asking for a ceasefire — and out of that meeting a whole age of reform was born.", "ec8d2178"]},
"teknik-osmanli-demiryollari":{"baslik": ["Ottoman railways and the kilometer guarantee", "23b2cbb4"], "metin": ["The key to Ottoman railway history is not technical but financial. The state could not build lines itself; it granted a concession to a foreign company and in return took on the company's losses. The name of this method was the kilometer guarantee, and the whole story follows from it.\n\n■ HOW THE KILOMETER GUARANTEE WORKED\nThe company builds and operates the line; for every kilometer of the line, the state pledges it a certain annual revenue. If the line earns, the company earns; if it loses, the state pays the difference. That is, all of the risk lies with the state and all of the profit with the company. This was the only way for a country without a line to come to own one; but it also had a flaw: the length in kilometers came to matter more than the development of the places the line passed through.\n\n■ THE RUMELIA LINE: large scale, large bill\nOn 17 April 1869 the Rumelia Railways Agreement was signed with Baron Maurice de Hirsch: a network of about two thousand kilometers starting from Istanbul and running by way of Bosnia to Salonica and the Sava River. What the state sought was military: to be able to get troops to the rebellions breaking out in the Balkans, and to integrate with Europe. The sum committed was 28 million francs a year for ninety-nine years; it is recorded that at the end of the reckoning the state came under a burden of about 2,772,000,000 francs, while Hirsch earned around 350 million francs.\nThe first section opened on 4 January 1871: Yedikule-Küçükçekmece, fifteen kilometers. On 17 June 1873 Istanbul-Sarımbey-Belova went into operation; in the first half of 1875 the Skopje-Mitrovica and Tırnova-Yanbolu lines were completed. The European connection was established on 12 August 1888: the Orient Express left Sirkeci for Vienna for the first time.\n\n■ THE ANATOLIAN LINE: the same method, different capital\nOn 24 September 1888 the concession for the Haydarpaşa-Ankara line was granted to Alfred von Kaulla, and the contract was signed on 4 October 1888. The state gave a guarantee of 15,000 francs a year for every kilometer. On 4 March 1889 the Anatolian Railway Company was founded.\n\n■ COLLECTING THE BILL\nThe price of this method was not only money. After 1878 a significant part of the Rumelia line was no longer on Ottoman territory; in 1909 the state left the lines in Eastern Rumelia to the Bulgarians in return for compensation of 42 million francs. That is, on the territory it had lost, the state paid a second time for the line it had itself had built.", "f43538b1"], "kisa": ["The state had no money, and a railway was expensive: how was the line built, and who paid for it?", "6c4f3aa0"]},
"teknik-osmanli-devlet-kademeleri":{"baslik": ["The ranks of the Ottoman state — from the vizierate to the office of Shaykh al-Islam", "b00bd70e"], "metin": ["The Ottoman central organisation ran along two branches — the seyfiye/mülkiye (the military and civil service: government by sword and pen) and the ilmiye (the learned establishment: government by knowledge of religious law) — and each branch had its own ladder of ranks.\n\n■ VIZIER AND GRAND VIZIER\nIn the sources the vizier was referred to by a tangle of titles such as vizier, pasha, sahib, asaf, vekil and lala; the most senior vizier in the Imperial Council (Divan-ı Hümayun) bore the title of grand vizier. The first viziers (from Hacı Pasha, ~1348, onwards) came from the ulema, most of them from the judiciary — between 1385 and 1453 the office was almost entirely in the hands of the Çandarlı family. The arrest and execution of Grand Vizier Çandarlı Halil in 1453 on the order of the Conqueror changed this system radically: the office passed from administrators of ulema origin to commanders of kul (devshirme) origin. By 1656 the institution had weakened; the appointment of Köprülü Mehmed Pasha re-established its authority in a way that was to last about fifty years.\n\n■ KAZASKER — THE HIGHEST IN THE LEARNED ESTABLISHMENT\nThe office of kazasker (chief military judge) was founded in the first years of Murad I (around 762/1361 or 763/1362), probably borrowed from the Anatolian Seljuk model; the first kazasker was Çandarlı Kara Halil, who moved to this post from the judgeship of Bursa. In the last years of the Conqueror (on the proposal of Karamani Mehmed Pasha) the office was divided in two, the kazaskerates of Rumelia and of Anatolia; under Selim I an Arab and Persian kazaskerate was also opened for a short time, but it was later merged with that of Anatolia. At first the post dealt with military legal cases and with accompanying the army on campaign; over time its main work became running the system of appointing and dismissing kadis and professors.\n\n■ DEFTERDAR — THE DEPUTY FOR THE TREASURY\nThe office of defterdar (chief treasurer), given its form by the Conqueror's code of law (kanunname), was regarded as the sultan's deputy in financial matters: no money left the treasury without permission, financial regulations passed through its hands, and it was under its supervision to see that tax collectors did not oppress the reaya. In the time of the Conqueror it was divided in two, the defterdarships of Rumelia and of Anatolia; in the 16th and 17th centuries an Arab and Persian defterdarship and second and third defterdarships were added. After Selim III the institution was reorganised, and in 1841 it was turned into the Ministry of Finance.\n\n■ SHAYKH AL-ISLAM — THE HOLDER OF THE FATWA\nThe appointment of Molla Fenari as mufti of Bursa in 1425 is regarded as the beginning of the Ottoman office of mufti, or Shaykh al-Islam. The essence of the post was to issue fatwas; he also supervised the appointments of all kadis and professors, watched over the great endowments and took part in ceremonies such as the girding with the sword. The office, at first regarded as subordinate to the grand vizier, turned in the 16th and 17th centuries into a partner of equal weight with him in the consultative councils.\n\n■ KETHÜDA — EACH LEVEL'S OWN DEPUTY\nThis title, derived from the Persian kedhuda (master of a house or village), was not a single office but a MODEL: the kethüda of the grand vizierate (the grand vizier's chief assistant) was the most important; there were also dozens of subtypes such as the provincial kethüda, the kapı kethüda (the representative in Istanbul of provincial officials), the kethüda of the kul or Janissaries and the guild kethüda. The guild kethüdas represented the guild before the government, settled disputes among masters and watched over the fairness of prices; in the 16th and 17th centuries they emerged from the shadow of the guild elders and became the actual leaders. The institution declined with centralisation after 1826.\n\n■ SERDAR-I EKREM\nThis is the title given to the vizier who commanded the army with full powers when the sultan did not go on campaign in person — most often it was the grand vizier himself, but it could also be given to another vizier; his authority extended to taking decisions of war and peace in the sultan's name.", "ae18a78c"], "kisa": ["From the vizierate to the office of Shaykh al-Islam: how many rungs did an official have to climb, and what was each rung called?", "f91d2efe"]},
"teknik-osmanli-egitim-sistemi":{"baslik": ["The Ottoman education system — the madrasa and its ranks", "7ba9b48b"], "metin": ["■ Three criteria, one hierarchy\nOttoman madrasas were ranked along three axes: by the principal book taught (the Tecrîd, Miftâh and Telvîh madrasas…), by the daily stipend of the professor (müderris) (between twenty and sixty akçe) and by institutional status. The Sahn-ı Semân (Eight Courtyards) and Tetimme madrasas, founded in 1470, formed the summit; by the 18th century the ranks had grown very complicated, and a long ladder had arisen stretching from the ibtidâ-i hâric grade to the dârülhadis (school of hadith).\n\n■ CURRICULUM\nThe basic subjects were Arabic morphology and syntax, logic, kalam (theology), rhetoric, fiqh (jurisprudence), hadith and Quranic exegesis; the ‘Dars-i Nizami’ represented the standard curriculum. The higher-level madrasas also taught medicine, mathematics and astronomy. Although the textbooks were in Arabic (works by scholars such as al-Taftazani and al-Jurjani), the actual language of instruction was Turkish.\n\n■ CAREER PATH\nA student who completed the madrasa and received his diploma (icazet) could take up a post as müderris, kadi or mufti — the ranks of education and of the judiciary were intertwined (see the separate card: kadi). The institutional ranking could at times also vary according to the teacher's seniority.\n\n■ SUMMIT AND DECLINE\nThe Süleymaniye complex (1550-1557) was the high point of the system. From the 18th century onwards the madrasas ‘became cumbersome and lost their flexibility’, and technical education shifted to separate military schools such as the Mühendishâne (engineering school). The reform attempts of the 19th century did not last; with the Law on the Unification of Education (Tevhîd-i Tedrîsat) of 1924 the madrasas were transferred to the Ministry of Education (Maarif Nezâreti) and closed shortly afterwards.", "6f5b61df"], "kisa": ["How many akçe a day the teacher a student studied under was paid mattered as much as which book he read — and this ladder would lead him up either to a judgeship or to a professorship.", "2ffee1f0"]},
"teknik-osmanli-fas-iliskileri":{"baslik": ["Ottoman-Moroccan relations", "00c6a0f1"], "metin": ["■ A single brief occupation — the city of Fez, 1554\nThe Ottomans never took Morocco (Fez) permanently. The only concrete military presence was when forces under the commander Salih Pasha took the city of Fez in 1554 and held it for just nine months.\n\n■ THE BATTLE OF WADI AL-MAKHAZIN (1578) — SUPPORT, NOT ANNEXATION\nWhile the Saadian ruler Mawlay Muhammad was trying to unite Morocco, Ottoman expansion from Algiers was seen as a threat. Although the Battle of Wadi al-Makhazin (Ksar el-Kebir) (1578), in which the Ottoman-backed Sultan Abd al-Malik defeated King Sebastian of Portugal, has gone down in history, this victory did NOT ESTABLISH lasting Ottoman sovereignty — Morocco remained an independent Saadian state.\n\n■ THE REAL FORM OF THE RELATIONSHIP: ENVOYS AND GIFTS\nThe bond between the two states consisted essentially of the coming and going of envoys and the exchange of gifts — no regular relationship of tributary status or vassalage was established. Although Saadian rulers such as Sultan Ahmad al-Mansur continued to correspond with Istanbul, there were tensions over questions of ceremonial recognition.\n\n■ THE ALAWI (FILALI) PERIOD — THE RELATIONSHIP WEAKENS\nIn the period of the Alawi (Filali) dynasty, which came to power after 1631, Ottoman influence declined greatly. When the French occupied Algeria in 1830, the fact that the people of Tlemcen sought protection from Morocco symbolises, in the words of the TDV, the end of the ‘organic bond’ the Ottoman state still had with the region.\n\n■ TRIBUTE-PAYING / PROTECTORATE / ALLIANCE — NONE WAS FORMALLY ESTABLISHED\nAccording to the TDV article that was read, Morocco at no time entered the status of a tribute-paying dependency like Wallachia, Moldavia, Transylvania or the Crimea, and no formal protectorate agreement was concluded either — from beginning to end the relationship remained at the level of diplomatic and religious courtesy between two independent Muslim states; nor does the text of the article mention an open state of war (Wadi al-Makhazin was not an Ottoman-Moroccan war but Morocco's own struggle for the throne).", "5a460de7"], "kisa": ["The Ottomans held a Moroccan city for nine months and then never returned — the remaining three centuries passed in the exchange of envoys and gifts.", "544b52e2"]},
"teknik-osmanli-hukuk-sistemi":{"baslik": ["The Ottoman legal system — the kadi, the sharia and kanun", "f1c5dbac"], "metin": ["■ Two sources, one enforcer\nOttoman law had two layers: the religious law (sharia), regarded as unchangeable, and the kanun/kanunname (sultanic or customary law) that the sultan issued as need arose. The official who applied both at the same time was the kadi — in the source's own words, ‘a man of sharia law but at the same time one of the civil officials’.\n\n■ THE APPOINTMENT AND PROMOTION OF THE KADI\nThe kadi was appointed by a sultanic diploma (berat) and entered in the kazasker's daybooks (rûznâmçe). Candidates were educated in the madrasa, went through a period of waiting and examination called mülâzemet, started at the lowest rank of the hierarchy and rose by seniority (see the separate card: madrasa).\n\n■ JURISDICTION — NOT ONLY LAWSUITS\nThe kadi's duties went far beyond the courtroom: during campaigns he was responsible for the army's supply and logistics, as well as for the upkeep of fortresses and for public order; acting like a notary, he entered contracts, wills and transfers of property in the court registers; he supervised the market and the narh (see the separate card: narh registers), oversaw pious endowments, managed the property of orphans and mediated between the people and the centre.\n\n■ THE FATWA AND THE SHAYKH AL-ISLAM\nThe second pillar of the law was the fatwa mechanism: the Shaykh al-Islam (or local muftis) answered questions according to Hanafi jurisprudence, and these fatwas carried weight in the courts and in administrative decisions — at times even on intensely political matters such as the deposition of a sultan (see the separate card: ranks of the state).", "8a4db372"], "kisa": ["A kadi was at one and the same time the man of both the sharia and the sultan's law — and his court also covered the market, the orphan's property and the upkeep of fortresses.", "d91f1436"]},
"teknik-osmanli-idari-yapi-degisimi":{"baslik": ["How did the Ottoman administrative structure change? From the Divan to the Sublime Porte", "2043b8f4"], "metin": ["■ THE IMPERIAL COUNCIL (DIVAN-I HÜMAYUN) — the single table of the state\nFrom the middle of the 15th century to the beginning of the 17th, the highest decision-making organ of Ottoman administration was the Imperial Council: the grand vizier, three to seven viziers of the dome (kubbealtı viziers), the kazaskers, the nişancı (chancellor) and the defterdars sat at the same meeting — the Shaykh al-Islam had no official seat in the council. The Divan was both an executive body and the highest court; anyone, whatever his status, could apply to it directly with a petition, and the officials were obliged to hear every complaint — this openness astonished Western observers. Before decisions became final they were ‘submitted’ to the sultan, that is, presented to him in person; the meetings were conducted in the second courtyard of the palace with strict ceremony.\n\n■ THE QUIET RELOCATION OF THE CENTRE\nFrom the 17th century onwards actual authority began to shift to the ‘afternoon council’ (ikindi divanı) held in the grand vizier's own residence. In the 18th century the Imperial Council turned largely into a ceremonial shell; real administration was in the hands of the Bab-ı Asafi (later called the Bab-ı Âli, the Sublime Porte), the seat of the grand vizier. Under Mahmud II this structure was abolished entirely and European-style ministries (nezarets) were established.\n\n■ THE PROVINCES: EYALET AND SANCAK\nOutside the centre, administration was built on the province (eyalet or beylerbeylik) and its subdivision, the sancak; each level had its own kadi, defterdar and military and administrative chief — it worked like a scaled-down copy of the ministerial model at the centre. In the classical period the province-and-sancak system was tied to the order of the timariot sipahis; from the 17th century onwards, as the tax-farming and malikâne methods linked to the central treasury came to the fore, this tie weakened (see the separate card: the tax-farming system).", "25eaada3"], "kisa": ["For three centuries a state met under the same roof, and then that roof quietly emptied — the machinery of decision-making moved from one courtyard to another within the same palace.", "f5970603"]},
"teknik-osmanli-ilk-fabrikalar-atolyeler":{"baslik": ["The first Ottoman factories — from state manufactories to the Feshane", "5a5b7a67"], "metin": ["■ Before the factory: the state's manufactories\nIn the Ottoman state, large-scale state production facilities existed long before the 19th century: TDV writes that the powder works (baruthanes) should be seen as “the factories of the age where gunpowder was made”; the naval arsenal, the cannon foundry (tophane) and the mint were similar state places of manufacture (see TDV: baruthane, tophane, tersane, darphane). As for paper, TDV gives the following picture: in 1741 İbrahim Müteferrika attempted to set up a paper workshop in Yalova; a factory aiming, with capital from the Mint, at production on a par with English and Dutch paper was opened at Beykoz in 1805 and was to supply paper to government offices, the Üsküdar Printing House and the army. A European observer in the middle of the 19th century wrote that Turkish factories such as Beykoz did not receive sufficient encouragement and that it came cheaper to bring paper from Europe (TDV quotes this from Charles White).\n\n■ THE REIGN OF MAHMUD II: THE FIRST FACTORIES IN THE MODERN SENSE\n• Feshane (Fez Factory) (1833): after the abolition of the Janissary Corps, the headgear of the new army became the fez; the demand for fezzes was met by imports first from Tunis and Egypt and then from Europe, in quantities exceeding 500,000 a year. This was found unsustainable from the point of view of the balance of foreign trade, and a move was made to domestic production: the director of fez manufacture, Katibzade Mustafa Efendi, brought 23 master fez makers from Tunis and 15 journeymen from Bursa; with 1.5 million kuruş allocated from the Mukataat Treasury (the treasury of revenue farms), production began at Kadırga in 1833, and a fulling mill (dinkhane) was set up in İzmit for washing. In 1839 it was moved to Defterdar Landing in Eyüp; in 1842 looms for woollen cloth (broadcloth) were added, and in 1843 a steam engine arrived. While the cost of a fez was 34 kuruş in the 1840s, by 1865 it had fallen below 9 kuruş; the number of workers rose from around 250 in the 1860s to more than 450 at the end of the 19th century. For the detailed story see the separate card: the founding of the Feshane.\n• İslimye textile factory (1834): TDV writes that the textile factory founded in 1834 in İslimye, which today bears the name Sliven in Bulgaria, made the city stand out as the first industrial city of the Balkans; it also mentions a state-owned textile factory and the famous manufacture of aba (coarse woollen cloth).\n• İzmit Broadcloth Factory (1845): opened in the reign of Abdülmecid for the army's uniform cloth, it served for a long time (TDV: izmit).\n\n■ THE OTHERS\nThe TDV article on the Feshane mentions the Feshane, together with the İslimye and İzmit broadcloth factories, the Bakırköy Veliefendi Printed Cotton Factory, the Hereke Fabric Factory and the Bursa Silk Factory, which were established around the same dates with state support, as the nucleus of the domestic textile industry. The founding years of the last three are `not found` in the articles read; for Hereke and Bakırköy, the slugs for separate articles in TDV turned out to be dead (HTTP 302).\n\n■ COMMON FEATURES\nThe common thread that emerges from TDV's account of the Feshane: the reason for its founding was the needs of the army and the balance of foreign trade; because high-quality raw material (e.g. fine wool) was imported, the first years closed at a loss; when from 1864 the state began to buy goods from the factory at cost price, profit depended only on market sales; from 1877 the whole output of the Feshane was reserved for military needs. Whether these factories gave rise to a civilian industrial bourgeoisie is not discussed in TDV — the card does not enter into this question.", "9a81741b"], "kisa": ["The word “factory” was new, the state manufactory was old — what was different at the beginning of the 19th century was the idea of producing at home what the army needed instead of buying it from abroad.", "a6213a72"]},
"teknik-osmanli-tabi-statu-listesi":{"baslik": ["Lands dependent on the Ottomans and their statuses", "36e6d07d"], "metin": ["In the classical period the five regions below were not under direct sancak or provincial (eyalet) administration but were tied to the Ottomans by a form of bond peculiar to each — all five according to the source, and each of the five separately:\n\n■ WALLACHIA (from 1373) — A TRIBUTARY VOIVODESHIP\nIn 1373 Voivode Vlaicu recognised Ottoman suzerainty; he retained his independence in internal affairs. After the defeat at Rovine in 1394, Mircea made the tributary status definitive in 1417. The Treaty of Szeged brought a compromise providing both that Wallachia would pay tribute to Istanbul and that its voivodes would be regarded as nominally subject to the Hungarian kings. The native voivode continued to run internal administration by means of an independent Orthodox metropolitanate and the landowning boyars; in return he paid an annual tribute, was subject to the Ottomans in foreign policy and (at first) there was no permanent Ottoman garrison on his territory. This order lasted until 1716 — at that date Phanariot Greek rulers began to be appointed in place of native voivodes (until 1821).\n\n■ MOLDAVIA (from 1455) — THE NORTHERN BRANCH OF THE SAME MODEL\nVoivode Petru Aron II preserved his autonomy by paying an annual tribute of 2000 gold pieces from 1455; the Conqueror did not interfere in internal affairs and did not make the region a sancak under direct rule. The tribute increased over time (12,000 ducats a year under the successors of Stephen the Great). From the 1710s Moldavia too passed under Phanariot rule.\n\n■ TRANSYLVANIA (from 1541) — AN ELECTIVE BUT CONFIRMED PRINCIPALITY\nWhen his father died in 1540, Transylvania was turned into a tributary voivodeship in 1541, and János Zsigmond became prince. The tribute was at first 10,000 gold pieces and rose to 40,000 in the 17th century. It had broad autonomy in internal affairs and was dependent on Istanbul in foreign affairs; the sultan had the last word in the election of the prince — the appointment of Gábor Bethlen as ‘independent prince’ in 1613 is an example of this.\n\n■ THE CRIMEAN KHANATE (from 1475) — THE ONLY VASSAL WITH A MILITARY OBLIGATION\nBy Mengli Giray's agreement of 1475, the khan accepted Ottoman protection; while broad autonomy in internal affairs was preserved, the coastal fortresses (Caffa, Sudak, Mangup) passed under direct Ottoman administration. Unlike the other three principalities, the Crimean khans were obliged to send troops regularly to Ottoman campaigns (on the Muscovite, Polish, Habsburg and Safavid fronts). In struggles for the throne the sultan's approval was decisive — whereas in Wallachia and Moldavia the influence of the local boyars was more decisive, in the Crimea Ottoman support was indispensable.\n\n■ THE HIJAZ — THE SHARIFATE, A CLASS OF ITS OWN\nIn the Hijaz, government was divided between the Sharif of Mecca and the Ottoman governor; the sharif had privileged powers such as conducting relations with the Bedouin Arabs and securing the pilgrimage routes. The central government never drew a clear line between the duties of the sharif and those of the governor, and this produced constant conflicts of authority. Until 1840 the region was in practice administered through the government of Egypt; afterwards direct Ottoman control grew stronger. The system ended when Sharif Hussein, appointed in 1908, resisted centralisation and rose in revolt with British support in 1916.\n\n■ THE COMMON PATTERN\nAll four (excluding the Crimea) share the same framework: a native or elected ruler + a fixed or rising tribute + dependence in foreign policy + (at first) the absence of a garrison. The axes on which they diverge are military obligation (the Crimea) and, over time, the degree of centralisation (Wallachia and Moldavia moved closer to the centre in the 18th century through Phanariot rule, the Hijaz in the 19th century through the direct weight of the governorship).", "5442e84a"], "kisa": ["The Ottoman lands were not painted in a single colour — from Wallachia to the Crimea, each had its own contract, its own tax and its own way of choosing a ruler.", "dc66ad4b"]},
"teknik-osmanli-tarihciligi-kaynaklari":{"baslik": ["Ottoman historiography and its sources", "db102780"], "metin": ["The written memory of Ottoman history rests on a wide range, from the vakanüvis (official chroniclers) formally appointed by the palace to independent writers. The three names read in this further reading represent three different ages and three different methods — each also has a card of its own:\n\n■ NEŞRÎ (d. 1520) — THE FIRST CRITICAL EYE\nHe is regarded as the first figure who compiled early Ottoman history, systematically weighed earlier sources such as Aşıkpaşazade, and tried to explain events through the link between cause and effect.\n\n■ NAÎMÂ (d. 1716) — THE FIRST OFFICIAL CHRONICLER\nHe is the figure who in 1702 bore the title of the empire's first official historian (vakanüvis); his work records the years 1592-1660 in a vivid narrative.\n\n■ AHMED CEVDET PASHA (1823-1895) — THE BRIDGE TO MODERN METHOD\nHe is the last great chronicler and jurist, who combined the classical narrative tradition with modern source criticism and brought European history into the picture as well.\n\n■ A NAME THAT OUGHT TO BE ASKED ABOUT BUT WAS NOT FOUND\nThe TDV slug tried for Vecihi Hasan Efendi (a 17th-century chronicler) (`vecihi`) proved to be dead; no alternative slug was searched for — `bulunamadı` (not found), and no separate card was written.", "6d90720e"], "kisa": ["The chronology of this atlas does not come out of nowhere — behind it lies a chain of chroniclers whose names are known, whose methods are debated and who at times refute one another.", "2c51c408"]},
"teknik-osmanli-vergi-sistemi":{"baslik": ["The Ottoman tax system — sharia taxes and customary taxes", "6c2b1a5f"], "metin": ["■ Two categories: sharia and customary\nOttoman taxes (tekâlîf) were grouped into two main sets: tekâlîf-i şer'iyye (grounded in Islam — zakat, the tithe (öşür), kharaj, jizya) and tekâlîf-i örfiyye (based on the sultan's law — avarız levies, sheep dues (ağnam resmi), various other dues and fees). In TDV's phrase, tekâlîf covered 'obligations in cash, in kind and in physical labour' — that is, tax was not always collected as money.\n\n■ WHO COLLECTED THEM\nOfficials such as the muhassıl (tax collector), the mütesellim (deputy governor-collector), the cizyedar (jizya collector) and the bâcbân (toll collector) worked in tax collection. Before the Tanzimat, collection was mostly farmed out to private individuals under the tax-farming (iltizam) method — instead of collecting directly, the state sold the right to collect (see the separate card: the tax-farming system). TDV records that this method aroused concern about 'oppression and abuse' throughout the various periods.\n\n■ WAS IT EQUAL ACROSS THE COUNTRY — NO, IT DIFFERED ALONG TWO AXES\nThe tax burden did not depend on a single schedule; it varied both FROM REGION TO REGION and ACCORDING TO RELIGION. Non-Muslims paid the jizya (a per-head poll tax), while Muslims paid the tithe and the kharaj. In the early stages of the Tanzimat some regions, such as the Hejaz and Istanbul, held tax privileges — so geography, too, was decisive, not religion alone.\n\n■ JIZYA — THREE BRACKETS ACCORDING TO INCOME\nIt was levied on dhimmi (non-Muslim subject) men; children, women, the blind, the disabled, the unemployed and the poor were exempt. Clergy were exempt at first; after 1691 all of them except the disabled were deemed liable. There were three income brackets — a'lâ (rich), evsat (middling), ednâ (poor) — and the amount was fixed in sharia dirhams or gold coins: 48/24/12 dirhams or 4/2/1 dinars respectively. Converted into esedi kuruş (lion dollars), it rose over time: it was 9/4.5/2.25 kuruş in 1691 and rose to 60/30/15 kuruş in 1834. Collection was carried out by the haraççı/cizyedar, and the registers known as 'defter-i cizye-i gebrân' (jizya registers of the non-Muslims) were inspected every three years (the 'nev-yâfte year').\n\n■ THE END OF THE JIZYA\nThe jizya was abolished by the Reform Edict of 1856 — once the principle of equality required non-Muslims too to be subject to military service, the old distinction lost its meaning. In its place came a payment called 'bedel-i askerî' (military exemption fee) in return for exemption from service; this practice lasted until 1907.\n\n■ THE COLLECTION CALENDAR\nBefore the Tanzimat, collection was divided into two instalments called 'rûz-i hızır' and 'rûz-i kāsım' (the Day of Hızır and the Day of Kasım, roughly spring and autumn). After the Tanzimat, the tax debt was spread over twelve instalments, while the dues (rüsûmat) were collected 'immediately, in kind or as an equivalent' — that is, cash taxes and taxes in goods or services ran on different calendars.\n\n■ THE LINK WITH ESHAM (1775)\nEsham was not a TYPE of tax — it was a method of BORROWING in which the income of a mukātaa (revenue source) was divided into shares and sold; it was introduced in 1775 in the face of the 7.5 million kuruş indemnity burden of Küçük Kaynarca (1775-06-01). In other words, instead of raising taxes directly, the state turned future tax and mukātaa income into cash today — this was a financial instrument outside the classical tekâlîf system and ADDITIONAL to it.", "8e440f2c"], "kisa": ["How much tax a subject (reaya) paid depended first on religion and then on place of residence — throughout the empire there was never a single tax schedule.", "c125ce0c"]},
"teknik-sura-yi-devlet-1868-idari-yargi":{"baslik": ["The Council of State (1868) — the birth of administrative justice", "c3a19d2e"], "metin": ["■ The division\nBy the imperial decree (irade) of 5 March 1868, the Supreme Council of Judicial Ordinances (Meclis-i Ahkâm-ı Adliyye) was split in two: its administrative side became the Council of State (Şûrâ-yı Devlet) and its judicial side the Council of Judicial Ordinances (Divan-ı Ahkâm-ı Adliyye). Midhat Pasha was placed at the head of the Council of State and Ahmed Cevdet Pasha at the head of the Divan. The Council of State was opened on 10 May 1868; its internal regulation had come into force on 1 April 1868.\n\n■ WHAT IT DID\nThe body, at first divided into five departments, prepared draft laws and regulations, examined administrative matters, resolved disputes between members of the administration and the judiciary, and dealt with matters concerning officials. In today's terms it was both a law-drafting board and an administrative court.\n\n■ THE LINE THAT REACHES THE PRESENT\nThe institution took the name Devlet Şûrası in 1938 and Danıştay in the early 1940s. So the reason why 10 May 1868 is cited as the founding date of the Danıştay (Turkey's Council of State) is that the institutional line is regarded as unbroken.\n\n■ OTHER INSTITUTIONS OF THE SAME PERIOD — HOW FAR COULD THIS BE ESTABLISHED\nThe division from which the Council of State was born is also the birth certificate of the Divan-ı Ahkâm-ı Adliyye: the separation of the high judiciary from the administration came about through the same decree. By contrast, the founding date and powers of the Court of Accounts (Divan-ı Muhasebat) were not found as a separate entry among the encyclopaedia articles surveyed — no judgement is given about that institution in this card. As for a review body of the constitutional court type, none existed in the Ottoman institutional order at these dates; the system of review under the Constitution (Kanun-ı Esasî) was built on the powers of the assembly and the sultan.", "02ae6ee7"], "kisa": ["In 1868 a single council was split in two — and for the first time the Ottoman administration sent “administrative business” and “judicial business” through two separate doors.", "614b6bed"]},
"teknik-tahrir-defterleri":{"baslik": ["Tahrir registers — how did the empire count itself?", "f12eb8e1"], "metin": ["■ Purpose\nThe tahrir was a survey carried out to record taxpayers and their obligations; it enabled the state to know where the reaya (tax-paying subjects) lived, what work they did, and what their sources of goods and produce were.\n\n■ METHOD\nThe survey commissions (made up of a commissioner (emin), a clerk, a kadi and local officials) compared the old records with the current situation: they entered changes in population, added new adults to the register and struck off the dead.\n\n■ WHAT WAS RECORDED\nThe registers contained detailed information such as the taxpayer's name and occupation, household status (married or single), landholding, village name, tax categories (various tithes, household taxes), numbers of livestock and the revenues of officials.\n\n■ OVER TIME\nThe system emerged in the 14th century; the oldest tahrir register to survive to the present is dated 1431. At first it was renewed roughly every thirty years or at the accession of a new sultan; throughout the 16th century it was standard practice in most provinces.\n\n■ DECLINE\nThe classical tahrir system was increasingly abandoned from the end of the 16th century onwards. As wars, rebellions and growing dependence on the central treasury weakened the timar order, surveys for the extraordinary tax (avarız) began in the 17th century to take the place of the traditional tahrirs.", "259617b0"], "kisa": ["Every thirty years an empire counted itself anew — who had died, who had come of age, which field was in whose hands: the oldest record we have today dates from 1431.", "40996726"]},
"teknik-takvim-i-vekayi-resmi-gazete":{"baslik": ["Takvim-i Vekayi — the first official gazette (1831)", "15611346"], "metin": ["■ What is an official gazette\nA newspaper that a state publishes in its own name as its own organ: it announces laws, appointments, decisions and notices to the public at first hand. TDV defines Takvim-i Vekayi as an official gazette “that could be the voice of the state”. Its first issue appeared in Turkish on 25 Jumada al-Awwal 1247 (1 November 1831); the aim was to inform domestic and foreign public opinion in a more orderly and rapid way, in keeping with the examples in Europe.\n\n■ WHAT NEED GAVE RISE TO IT\nTDV counts three influences. In Europe, journalism had a long past going back to the beginning of the 17th century; in the Ottoman lands, meanwhile, from the end of the 18th century foreigners had been publishing newspapers in their own languages. Mehmed Ali Pasha's Vekayi-i Mısriyye, half in Turkish and half in Arabic, whose first issue appeared in Cairo on 2 December 1828, and the newspapers Spectateur oriental and Courrier de Smyrne, which Alexandre Blacque published one after the other in İzmir, spurred the Ottoman administrators into action. TDV links Mahmud II's granting of permission probably to three things: his policy of centralisation and reform, the difficulty the state had in explaining its point of view to the Western countries and to its own public in the war that ended in 1829 with the independence of Greece, and the idea of providing a kind of alternative to Mehmed Ali's newspaper.\n\n■ WHO DID THIS JOB BEFORE IT\nThere was no regular news publication. From the relevant TDV articles four instruments can be seen: town criers (dellals; the news criers under the command of the chief crier (dellalbaşı) announced a piece of news to the people, and in cases such as the death and accession of a sultan were regarded as official agents of the state), couriers (ulaks; the postal system that carried orders and news from one menzil (post station) to the next), the official court chronicler (vakanüvis; the official historian of the state) and coffee-house conversation. TDV calls none of these “the forerunner of the newspaper”; which of them served which function is the page's interpretation — for a more detailed comparison see the separate card: the birth of journalism and the first Ottoman newspapers.\n\n■ ITS FUNCTIONS\nThe gazette was divided into two sections as “Vukūât-ı Resmiyye ve Gayr-i Resmiyye” (Official and Unofficial Events); subheadings such as “Internal Affairs, Military Matters, Prices (Es'âr), Sciences, Appointments, Appointments of the Learned Hierarchy, Foreign Affairs, Curiosities” show that domestic and foreign news were also given space. That is, lists of appointments, prices (es'âr), military news and foreign events were gathered on the same pages. From time to time urgent announcements were published in supplements with names such as “zeyil, ilâve, i‘lân, tenbihnâme, varaka-i mahsûsa” (addendum, supplement, announcement, notice of admonition, special sheet).\n\n■ HOW IT WORKED\nThe content was selected from information and documents compiled by state officials in various parts of the country, above all in Istanbul, and sent to the Directorate of the Imperial Gazette Office (Takvimhane-i Amire Nezareti). The draft of each issue was sent to the palace through the grand vizier's office before printing; it was sometimes corrected at the sultan's request, but in general the drafts were printed as they stood. News from the Sublime Porte was gathered by Sarım Efendi, and news from the War Ministry (Bab-ı Seraskeri) by Said Bey. Besides Turkish, the gazette also appeared in other languages: in French as Le Moniteur ottoman on 5 November 1831, and in Armenian (Liro-Kir), Greek, Arabic and Persian editions from the beginning of 1832. It was sold by compulsory subscription at 120 kuruş a year; not counting the editions in other languages, it reached a little over 3000 subscribers. Although it was supposed to appear once a week, after the first eight issues it could not keep to its schedule (8, 19 and 24 issues a year).\n\n■ ITS LIFESPAN\nThe first period ended on 16 March 1878 with issue 2119 (TDV connects this with the war against Russia and the territorial losses); the second period lasted from 26 March 1891 to 16 May 1892; the third period published 4608 issues from 28 September 1908 until 4 November 1922, when the Istanbul government came to an end. TDV says that the gazette is not considered to have been sufficient to achieve its aim completely; in the Republican period it showed a kind of continuity under the name Ceride-i Resmiyye / Resmî Gazete (Official Gazette).", "b082ec84"], "kisa": ["The state decided to print its own voice itself — what spurred it into action were the newspaper of Mehmed Ali, governor of Egypt, and the newspapers published by foreigners in İzmir.", "3504a78f"]},
"teknik-tashih-i-sikke-mecidiye-cift-metal":{"baslik": ["The mecidiye and the bimetallic system — why was the currency rebuilt in 1844, and why did it last seventy years?", "f8a41daa"], "metin": ["■ The problem: a currency with a corrupted standard\nFor more than a century the Ottoman kuruş had continuously lost value. According to Şevket Pamuk's calculation in TDV, the pure silver in one kuruş fell from 14.5 grams in 1720 to 2.4 grams in 1824 and to 1 gram in 1844. Over the same period, from the mid-1760s to 1844, food prices in Istanbul rose ten to fifteen times; the fastest increase was between 1808 and 1840. A great many coins of different types were in circulation on the market, some of them debased (mağşûş).\n\n■ DECISION AND IMPLEMENTATION: A CHANGE THAT TOOK FOUR YEARS\nThe new monetary system was launched by a decree dated 26 Safar 1256 (29 April 1840); the equipment needed for the mint was ordered from London. The old coins were definitively withdrawn from circulation on 25 Jumada al-Akhir 1259 (23 July 1843). The first mecidiye was struck on 22 April 1844, the half mecidiye on 18 May and the quarter mecidiye on 19 May 1844. Pamuk calls the operation the 'coinage correction (tashîh-i sikke) of 1844'.\n\n■ WHAT IS BIMETALLISM?\nBoth gold and silver circulating as full-value money, with the ratio between them FIXED by the state. Under the 1844 standard, 1 kuruş contained 1 gram of pure silver and 1 gold lira 6.6 grams of pure gold, and 100 silver kuruş equaled 1 gold lira. The ratio that follows from this: 100 / 6.6 = 15.1 — that is, 1 gram of gold was worth 15.1 grams of silver. In Pamuk's words, the Ottoman state thus passed to a 'bimetallic monetary system defined by a fixed exchange rate'.\n\n■ WHAT IS THE MECIDIYE?\nThe name of the new coins struck in Abdülmecid's name. According to TDV, the name came into general use especially for the 20-kuruş SILVER coin; gold mecidiyes were also struck. By Abdülmecid's death, 15,312,329 gold and 54,987,960 silver mecidiyes had been minted.\n\n■ SIGNIFICANCE: PERMANENCE\nAccording to Pamuk, the real significance of the reform is that the coinage standards adopted, unlike earlier ones, were lasting and could be maintained until 1914: six Ottoman sultans had money struck to the same standard. After a century of debasement (tağşîş, lowering of the standard), this was the first lasting arrangement that stabilized the value of money again.\n\n■ THE END: THE FALL OF SILVER\nThe bimetallic system rested on a fixed ratio; when silver became abundant in the world in the 1870s and its value fell, this ratio could no longer be sustained. In 1879 the Ottoman state also moved to abolish the bimetallic system.", "ae8e3818"], "kisa": ["The silver in one kuruş was 14.5 grams in 1720 and had fallen to 1 gram by 1844. The coinage correction (tashîh-i sikke) halted this slide: it tied the silver kuruş and the gold lira to a fixed ratio — and this standard did not change until 1914.", "a3d5d64b"]},
"teknik-tunel-karakoy-beyoglu-1875":{"baslik": ["Tünel (1875) — the Karaköy-Beyoğlu underground railway", "1515dfc9"], "metin": ["■ Why it was built\nKaraköy (Galata) was the centre of banking and trade, Beyoğlu (Pera) that of entertainment and social life; the Yüksekkaldırım road linked the two. An article from the Faculty of Mechanical Engineering of Istanbul Technical University (İTÜ) relates that the French engineer Eugène-Henri Gavand came to Istanbul as a tourist in 1867 and saw very large numbers of people walking along this road. The same article, relying on Gavand, reports that about 40,000 people walked it daily, and that the road had a gradient of 24% and a width of six metres (in places four metres). Gavand proposed an underground railway in the form of a lift.\n\n■ CONCESSION AND OPENING\nThe project was discussed in the Council of State, and the concession was granted by an imperial decree (irade) dated 10 June 1869; the contract was signed on 6 November 1869 between the Minister of Public Works, Dâvud Pasha, and Gavand. The term of the concession was 42 years, the state's share of the takings 1.5%, with no cash aid from the state for construction. The opening ceremony was held on 17 January 1875; the next day it was opened to the public (Gavand was not at the ceremony; the article attributes this to his sense of grievance). The tunnel was 550.80 m long and the railway within it 626 m; the average journey time was 2.5 minutes and the total cost 4,125,554 francs; the cars were hauled by cable, and the cable system was worked until 1968 by a stationary steam engine at Beyoğlu station. A single ticket cost 2 piastres in 1st class and 1 piastre in 2nd class; in the first 14 days (18-31 January) 75,000 passengers were carried (İTÜ article).\n\n■ WHAT THE TDV SAYS\nA separate article called TÜNEL was `not found` in the TDV (slug HTTP 302). It is mentioned in only two articles, and the two differ on the date: the article on the Beyoğlu Cemetery writes that the Tünel, built by the French engineer Henri Gavand, was opened to service on 17 January 1875, and that during excavation Gavand took care not to damage the cemetery; the article on Galata, however, says that the removal of the graves at the upper end of the cemetery when the Tünel was opened in 1876 became an issue. 17 January 1875 agrees with the date in the article above; 1876 corresponds to the year in which Gavand published his plans in Paris (1876) — this is this site's observation; the TDV does not make this connection.\n\n■ IS IT “THE SECOND OLDEST METRO IN THE WORLD”\nThe İTÜ article describes the Tünel as the second oldest underground urban railway in the world after London's. Such rankings may vary according to the definition of “underground”, “metro” and “funicular”; there is no ranking in the TDV, and the card reports the article's statement.\n\n■ 1869: THE THREE “T”S\nThe article regards 1869 as a turning point in Istanbul's public transport: in the same year concessions were granted for the train, the tramway (Tram) and the Tunnel. This site's interpretation: the Tünel's being mentioned together with the others as one of the new means of transport may be considered a continuation of the line opened by the Şirket-i Hayriyye (1851).", "b02a76e6"], "kisa": ["In 1867 a French tourist saw the crowds walking up the Galata slope and designed an underground railway — the Tünel was opened on 17 January 1875.", "38d9f6e4"]},
"tersane-konferansi-dayatma":{"baslik": ["The Constantinople (Tersane) Conference (1876) — the programme imposed by the great powers", "bdf8c80e"], "metin": ["■ Why the conference met\nIt met to discuss the Balkan crisis, which began with the Herzegovina uprising in 1875 and continued a year later with the Bulgarian uprising and the Ottoman wars with Serbia and Montenegro. The most important phase of the crisis was the Bulgarian uprising, and this question determined the balances at the conference: the news about the suppression of the uprising was turned into political material by the Liberals in Britain through the propaganda of the \"Bulgarian horrors\" and developed into anti-Turkish campaigns. While this paralysed the British government, which favoured the territorial integrity of the Ottoman Empire, it spurred Russia into action.\n\n■ COMING TO THE TABLE\nWhen Ottoman forces routed the Serbian forces on the Morava on 29 October 1876, Russia imposed an unconditional armistice of six weeks or two months through an ultimatum demanding a reply within eight hours. At the same time it proposed that the question be discussed among the Christian powers in the absence of the Ottomans. Seeing that its interests in the East were under threat, Britain proposed on 4 November 1876 that the question be discussed at a conference, and thus regained the lead in finding a solution. The conference met on the upper floor of the Ministry of the Navy building in Kasımpaşa; the first session was held on 23 December 1876.\n\n■ THE MOMENT THE GUNS WERE FIRED\nWhile the delegates were discussing the agenda, the sound of cannon was heard outside: the constitution was being proclaimed. The Ottoman representative told the delegates that these guns announced the constitutional regime and that this regime was the guarantee of the freedoms of Muslims and Christians throughout the Ottoman lands, and then declared that there was no longer any need for such a meeting. The delegates listened in astonishment, but the outcome did not change; the Russian representative Ignatyev said that no importance should be attached to such displays and that they should move on to the agenda, and the conference continued.\n\n■ THE IMPOSED PROGRAMME\nThe subjects to be discussed and the proposals to be presented to the Turkish side had in fact been decided at preparatory meetings that the representatives of the great powers held among themselves at the beginning of December; the decisions agreed on were really the work of the Russian representative Ignatyev and the British representative Salisbury. The programme included the following: Bulgaria would be divided into two provinces, eastern and western, each governed for five years by a Christian governor appointed by the sultan with the consent of the guarantor powers; the governor would be assisted by a provincial assembly; the Turkish army would be concentrated on the frontier and in the principal places, and a national militia and gendarmerie would be established for the province; an international commission would be set up to supervise the reforms, and 5,000 Belgian soldiers would be sent to protect this commission. Bosnia-Herzegovina would be united as a single province but deprived of a militia force. With Serbia and Montenegro the status quo would be taken as the basis, but Serbia would receive Little Zvornik and Montenegro some places in Herzegovina.\n\n■ WHY IT COULD NOT BE ACCEPTED\nThe Ottoman representatives found the programme unacceptable for an independent state; they even stated that they were not authorised to discuss some of its articles. The technical basis of the objection was the constitution: many of the articles presented were contrary to the newly proclaimed Kanun-i Esasi (Basic Law).", "0bb5b411"]},
"tersane-konferansi-sonuc":{"baslik": ["The end of the Constantinople (Tersane) Conference — the assembly chose war", "b2244af6"], "metin": ["■ The final proposal\nAs the Sublime Porte resisted and the conference reached an impasse, the foreign representatives began to discuss softening their proposals. Especially through Midhat Pasha's unofficial contacts and the influence of two British statesmen, the reforms, kept to a minimum, were softened still further and presented on 15 January: the proposal for a European gendarmerie was dropped, and the powers' approval of the appointment of governors was to apply only for the first five years. But the international commission remained. A condition resembling an ultimatum was attached to the proposal: if it was rejected, the conference would end at the session of 18 January and the representatives would return to their countries.\n\n■ 18 JANUARY — THE GENERAL ASSEMBLY\nThe Ottoman side stated that the reply had to be given after taking the votes of all the dignitaries of the realm. On 18 January, under the chairmanship of Midhat Pasha, an assembly of 180 persons met, consisting of the members of the Council of State, the ministers, civil and military dignitaries and non-Muslim religious leaders. The decision was this: it was more befitting the honour of the state and the nation to accept war than to accept unjust proposals that would harm the state and to endure a situation that injured its dignity. In taking this decision, the assembly OPENLY accepted the risk that, if the war brought no benefit, it would face harsher proposals and lose more territory. In other words, the outcome was not unknown; it was a risk consciously accepted.\n\n■ THE COUNTER-PROPOSAL AND THE LAST SESSION\nThe Ottoman side also set out the principles it could accept: two commissions made up of equal numbers of Muslim and Christian members would be established, one to serve for a year for Bosnia-Herzegovina and the other for the Danube and Edirne provinces; they would supervise the implementation of the constitutional reforms and of the measures to be taken, adopt measures to help the people who had suffered harm, and ensure the people's security with the help of the gendarmerie organisation to be established. At the last session on 20 January these proposals were not even made the subject of serious negotiation; the representatives of the great powers rejected them, declared the conference over and left the city, each leaving a chargé d'affaires in his place.\n\n■ THE RESULT: WAR THREE MONTHS LATER\nWith the break-up of the conference, the door to the Ottoman-Russian war was opened. Russia declared war on 24 April 1877. The treaties to be signed a year later were far harsher than the proposals rejected at the conference: Serbia, Montenegro and Romania became independent, Bulgaria was established, Kars-Ardahan-Batum passed to Russia and the administration of Cyprus to Britain, and Bosnia-Herzegovina was left to Austrian occupation. The possibility the assembly had risked came about exactly.\n\n■ A SIDE RESULT\nThe conference also revealed a deep crack in Britain's Eastern policy: the chief British representative, Salisbury, was working in harmony with the Russian representative, and the other British representative accused him of serving Russian designs, the greatest threat to British interests. How fragile the Ottoman calculation of leaning on Britain in 1878 was had already appeared at this table, before the war even began.", "1f4f7ab4"]},
"timar-sistemi-1432-1827":{"sebep.b": ["A state whose money economy and central treasury were not sufficiently developed, and whose army consisted largely of cavalry, maintaining its soldiers on the taxes of the land", "2e012c26"], "sonuc.b": ["The conversion in 1827 of about 5,200 timariot sipahis from 53 sanjaks in Rumelia and Anatolia into cavalry of the Asâkir-i Mansûre, and the timar regime's entry on the path to liquidation", "b221aa79"], "bag": ["Why does '1432' in the chronology carry no day or month? Because the 'institutionalisation' of the timar was not a day but a process. According to the TDV article on the timar, the first documentary reference to the system goes back to the reign of Orhan Bey; 1432 is not the year in which the system was born, but the year of the oldest surviving summary (icmal) register of timars — the register of the sanjak of Arvanid in Albania, dated AH 835. The article says that this register shows that all the basic principles of the system had already taken shape by then; in other words, 1432 is not a beginning but the first photograph we have. Because the year AH 835 falls between September 1431 and August 1432, the TDV article on the cebelü refers to the same register as 'dated 1431' — the two articles do not contradict each other; they are reading the two ends of the same Hijri year. That is why no source gives a day or month, and none has been invented.", "8341973e"], "metin": ["What a timar is. According to the TDV article on the timar, the word means 'care, attention' in the dictionary, and in Turkish it is synonymous with dirlik (livelihood grant). As a term, it is a non-hereditary allocation made in the central provinces to support a cavalry force and the military-administrative hierarchy. The sipahi was given not ownership of the land but the right to collect specified taxes from specified villages. The article links the roots of the system to several traditions: the Byzantine pronoia (the word timar is the exact equivalent of the Greek pronoia), the iqta of the Islamic states, and Turco-Mongol tradition (terms such as 'cebe' are Mongolian).\n\nDIRLIK, timar, zeamet, has — the difference lies in the size of the revenue. Dirlik is the umbrella term; beneath it there are three grades according to size. The TDV article on the zeamet, citing the Kanunname of Ali Çavuş, draws the line precisely: a dirlik one akçe short of 20,000 akçes is a timar; one between 20,000 and 100,000 akçes is a zeamet, and its holder is called a zaim; one above 100,000 akçes is a has and is reserved for sanjak governors, beylerbeys and viziers (those belonging to the sultan are the 'imperial has', havâss-ı hümâyun). But this threefold division, too, became established only later: according to the TDV article on the timar, in the Arvanid register of 1432 the dirliks fall into only two classes, 'has' and 'timar'; according to the article on the zeamet, the word 'zeamet' never occurs in the registers of the reign of Murad II (the term zaim is used), whereas from the reign of the Conqueror onwards it appears frequently in the tax registers.\n\nThe SIPAHI's obligations. The timariot sipahi was obliged to be present on campaign; according to the TDV, when the campaigning season began (generally in March-April), the sipahis were assembled through the çeribaşı, the subaşı and the sanjak governor, and the beylerbey inspected them and led them to the imperial army. In peacetime the sipahi collected from the peasant the taxes fixed by law (öşür or tithe, çift resmi, ispenç) and could not take more; in the words of the TDV, the Ottoman peasant was in this sense free, and the sipahi could not make him work at his whim. He had the right to bring back a peasant who had fled within ten (later fifteen) years.\n\nThe CEBELÜ reckoning. According to the TDV article on the cebelü, cebelü means 'an armoured, fully equipped soldier', and their number depended directly on the revenue of the timar. The first examples are seen in the Arvanid register: a sipahi with an income below 1,000 akçes was 'himself the cebelü', that is, he went on campaign alone in his armour; with 1,500-2,000 akçes he brought a gulâm (retainer) with him; holders of larger timars took part with the better armour called 'bürüme' and with cebelüs according to their income. The Kanunname of the Conqueror standardised this: a graduated scale up to 15,000 akçes, and, for timars exceeding 15,000, one cebelü for every 3,000 akçes. The TDV article on the timar writes that holders of zeamets and has brought one cebelü for every 5,000 akçes. Two notes on the figures: İdris-i Bitlisi estimates the timariot army of Anatolia in 1473 at about 20,000 and that of Rumelia at about 24,000 (according to the TDV, the cebelüs must be included in this number); the official records of 1527-1528 give 37,521 timar holders, of whom 9,563 were fortress guards.\n\nGRANT and withdrawal. A timar was entered in the summary register as an indivisible unit called a 'kılıç' (sword); the document of assignment (tahvil tezkiresi) was prepared at the centre, and a berat (diploma) was issued. As a rule, a member of the reaya could not become a sipahi; but according to the TDV, volunteers, akıncıs (raiders) and fortress soldiers who distinguished themselves on campaign could receive a timar on recommendation, and during the long campaigns of 1578-1618 thousands of Anatolians entered the sipahi class in this way. Being a Muslim was not a requirement: in some sanjaks the proportion of Christian timar holders reached 35%. The chief reason for the withdrawal of a timar was failure to take part in a campaign; a serious crime or oppression of the reaya would also cause the timar to be lost. If a sipahi whose timar had been taken away (mazul) did not take part in a campaign within seven years, he lost his status as a sipahi altogether and became a tax-paying member of the reaya. For this reason the article stresses that sipahi status was 'not hereditary in the full sense'. There were also exceptions: the yurtluk-ocaklık holdings in Eastern Anatolia and, from the 17th century, the ocaklık timars in Bosnia could be inherited within the family.\n\nDISSOLUTION. The TDV article on the timar describes the dissolution as the combined effect of several forces: in the Austrian war of 1593-1606 the need for infantry armed with muskets made salaried soldiers necessary, and on the battlefield the sipahi was displaced by the Janissaries, the sekban and the saruca; after 1584, while timar revenues remained fixed, inflation reached 100%, and the sipahi could no longer go on long campaigns; the expulsion from the profession of the Anatolian sipahis who did not take part in the campaign of 1596 caused great turmoil, and those dismissed or left without timars joined the Celali bands; the government diverted timar revenues to the treasury and the palace circle — in the campaign of 1621, 2,882 çavuş, 681 müteferrika and 522 clerks were timar holders. At the Erzurum muster of 1715, 2,119 timars passed to the treasury; the regulations of 1777 and 1791 attempted reform, but without result.\n\nTHE END. According to the TDV, in 1827 about 5,200 timariot sipahis from fifty-three sanjaks in Rumelia and Anatolia were converted, together with their timar revenues, into cavalry of the Asâkir-i Mansûre; since the revenues were not enough for their livelihood, some had their timars taken away and were put on salaries, and it was envisaged that the timars would be liquidated on the death of the last sipahi. Local problems connected with timar land were handed down to the Republic. Like its beginning, its end cannot be tied to a single day and a single city: the entry for 1827 in the chronology sums up in a single line a transformation spread across fifty-three sanjaks.", "857ec8d9"]},
"tosun-pasa-film-mi-tarih-mi":{"baslik": ["Is the Tosun Paşa played by Kemal Sunal this Tosun Pasha? — where popular memory and the sources part ways", "a36e7b32"], "metin": ["The question belongs to the curious reader; the standard belongs to the source. This card does not use a film as a historical source, and the film was not watched; the plot is taken solely from the description in a peer-reviewed academic article. The reason is a rule: the atlas's source discipline is not relaxed, not even for a comedy.\n\nWhat the FILM tells (the academic description). The study by Muhsine Sekmen and Gizem Kul Kalkandelen, published in Erciyes Akademi, summarises the film as follows: “The film Tosun Paşa (Tibet, 1976) deals with the struggle for a district called Yeşil Vadi (Green Valley) between two long-established families in Alexandria in the Ottoman period: the Tellioğulları and the Seferoğulları.” The setup is this: “Daver Bey, the governor of Alexandria, has a young and beautiful daughter named Leyla. Each of the two families wishes to take Leyla as a bride in order to gain possession of Yeşil Vadi. By passing off Şaban, a servant of the house, as Tosun Pasha, one of the influential figures of the Cairo palace, the Tellioğulları seize an opportunity to gain Yeşil Vadi and Leyla. But when the real Tosun Pasha appears, things do not go as they had planned.”\n\nTHREE measurements, three separate answers.\n① Is it the same person? No — the two descriptions do not match. The film's Tosun Pasha is one of “the influential figures of the Cairo palace”, placed in the position of arbiter in a land dispute; his name works less as an office than as a figure defined by a title, and his dramatic function is to be “a high-ranking personage who can be impersonated”. The historical Ahmed Tosun Pasha, by contrast, is, in the words of the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, the man who was “appointed governor of Jeddah and commander of the army to be sent against the Wahhabis” — the son of the governor of Egypt, head of the army that set out from Egypt to the Hijaz in September 1811, a soldier who died of the plague in 1816. Not a palace dignitary arbitrating a lawsuit over land.\n② Is the event in the film historically accurate? The question cannot be answered as it stands, because what the film narrates is not a historical event: the academic description names neither a year nor an event, and the only frame is “the Ottoman period”. There is no date to test; the only things that can be tested are the names, and they are absent from the sources — neither an Egyptian household called “Tellioğulları” or “Seferoğulları”, nor a governor called “Daver Bey”, nor a district called “Yeşil Vadi” appears in the sources consulted. During the historical Tosun Pasha's years in Egypt (1811-1816), the governor of Egypt was his father, Mehmed Ali Pasha of Kavala. ⇒ The correct statement is this: the film does not tell history wrongly; it does not tell history.\n③ Is there any link between them? This was checked and none was found: none of the academic sources opened connects the film's character with the historical Tosun Pasha; no source was found that says “it was inspired by him”, nor one that says “it has nothing to do with him”. This text does not fill that gap with guesswork.\n\nIts place in CULTURAL life — the measurable part. What the card can say about the film's weight in Turkey is limited to what can be tied to figures. ① The director's first film: the study of Kartal Tibet's cinema by Yunus Namaz and Ayşegül Şahin states that “The first film made by the director Tibet is Tosun Paşa, produced in 1976, and his last film is the work entitled Hayat Güzeldir, produced in 2008. Between 1976 and 2008 Tibet directed a total of 67 films” — in other words, it was the opening film of a directing career of 67 films. ② Audience rating: in Yavuz Ağca's study describing Turkish cinema through web mining, “Tosun Paşa (1976, p. = 9.0)” appears among the highest audience ratings; the study lists this rating after that of Hababam Sınıfı (9.3). (This is a measurement of audience ratings, not an artistic judgement.) ③ The birth of the duo: the history of comedy cinema by Selda Solak and Yavuz Bayram records that Şener Şen “also acted together with Sunal in films such as ‘Süt Kardeşler’ (1976), ‘Tosun Paşa’ (1976), ‘Kibar Feyzo’ (1978), ‘Çöpçüler Kralı’ (1978) and ‘Davaro’ (1981)” and describes the pattern: “In the films in which he forms a duo with Kemal Sunal, Şen portrays the two-faced, cunning and fraudulent character, while Sunal portrays naive, well-meaning, clean-hearted characters.”\n\nAND the same academic source also offers a criticism. The study that describes the film does not do so in order to praise it: examining the nanny character in the film (Yasemin Esmergül), Sekmen and Kul Kalkandelen say that “in this film, too, Esmergül has very few scenes and lines, and she makes no contribution to the world of the film or to the development of the hero”; in the film the actress “is given no name other than that of the nanny.” The study's general verdict on the seven films it covers is harsher: “The use of negative expressions such as ‘coal bin, discoloured woman, charcoal-black woman’ for Afro-Turkish performers shows the discrimination applied to them on the basis of their skin colour” and “expressions of humiliation are presented as natural and as an element of comedy.” ⇒ A film's place in culture is measured not only by how much it is loved, but also by what it normalises.\n\nCONCLUSION. In popular memory, “Tosun Paşa” evokes not a solemn send-off but a burst of laughter; in the sources, it is the name of a young man at the head of the army that retook the Two Holy Places (Haramayn), whom his commanders regarded “as a child”, and who died of the plague five years later. This is exactly where the work of this atlas becomes visible: the chronology entry shows September 1811, the map draws what changed in the Hijaz on that date — and both together tell of a different human being from the namesake on the cinema screen.", "42380f8c"], "kisa": ["In Turkey the name “Tosun Paşa” calls to mind not a commander but a comedy. The card sets two accounts side by side: the film's Tosun Pasha is, in the academic description, one of “the influential figures of the Cairo palace” and the arbiter in a lawsuit over land; history's Ahmed Tosun Pasha is the son of the governor of Egypt, governor of Jeddah and commander of the Hijaz army. The same name, two different people — one of them fictional.", "4f900341"]},
"tosun-pasa-kimdir":{"baslik": ["Who was Tosun Pasha — his father brought him onto the stage in the same stroke as the Mamluk danger", "c7d333b5"], "metin": ["Biographical details — and why they are scattered. There is no article called \"Tosun Pasha\" in the TDV (the measurement of the search is in this card's internal note); the biography was compiled from inside five separate articles. His full name: İlhan Şahin's article `abbas-hilmi-i`, in introducing Abbas Hilmi, governor of Egypt, says that he \"is the son of Ahmed Tosun Pasha\". Mustafa L. Bilge's article `diriye` gives the assignment of duties: \"Mehmed Ali Pasha sent one of his sons, Tosun Ahmed Pasha, to the Jeddah and Taif districts, and after his death sent İbrahim Pasha to the Medina and northern Arabia districts.\" And the place that gives both offices in a single sentence is, strangely, the sentence about a massacre — `kavalali-mehmed-ali-pasa`: \"At the great feast he gave on 1 March 1811 in honour of his son Tosun, who had been appointed governor of Jeddah and commander of the army to be sent against the Wahhabis, he had the Mamluk beys, with a few exceptions, killed.\" ⇒ Tosun Pasha enters history with two offices and a banquet.\n\nWHY him, why not his father. Kamuran Şimşek's study states the reason openly: Mehmed Ali, \"in order to remove the possibility of a rising breaking out behind his back, did not go on the campaign himself but placed his son Tosun Pasha at the head of the army.\" The same article also says that the campaign was part of the same plan as the Mamluk question: the army had been assembled \"in order to remove completely the Mamluk danger in Egypt\"; \"it was announced through town criers that Tosun Pasha would set out on the Hejaz Campaign on 1 March, and a feast was held to celebrate this campaign.\" The centre also consented: \"Mehmet Ali Pasha's assigning his son Tosun to settle the Wahhabi question was received favourably by the Sublime Porte.\"\n\nBY SEA or by land — both. The source's answer to a question that comes to mind about the map stands in a single sentence (Şimşek): \"In this campaign, which began in September 1811, the infantry travelled by sea aboard ships, while the cavalry unit travelled by land.\" Kevser Değirmenci gives the composition and strength of the army: \"a force of 3,500 men, mostly Albanians and Turks, with some regular soldiers and infantry trained by Frenchmen.\" The objective was a port town, and why it was that town is written in the TDV's article `yenbu`: Yanbu is \"the port of Medina\", and the Wahhabis, \"cutting the link between Medina's grain store and Yanbu\", had besieged the town. The same article records the outcome with year precision: \"Under the leadership of Tosun and İbrahim pashas, sons of Mehmed Ali Pasha of Kavala, governor of Egypt, Yanbu was brought back under Ottoman rule (1811).\" Şimşek also gives the month: \"The Egyptian forces seized Yanbu in October and used it as a base for the operations to be carried out.\" ⇒ The campaign began in September, the base was established in October. (For the atlas's day for this entry, see the internal note.)\n\nA commander looked on as a CHILD — and the first rout. Tosun did not want to wait; in a letter he wrote to his father he said that \"after taking control of Yanbu, he preferred to take action instead of waiting for the reinforcements to come from Egypt\" (Şimşek). The attack on 23 November 1811 on the entrenchments in the foothills ended in a rout. The cause comes to the same point in both sources. Şimşek: \"The reason for the defeat of the army led by Tosun Pasha arose from the soldiers' failure to obey the orders given by their commanders.\" Değirmenci says the same thing more bluntly: \"This defeat arose from the commanders' looking upon Tosun Pasha as a child and not carrying out his orders.\" Most of the defeated returned to Egypt in 1812 and pointed to \"one another as the cause of the defeat\"; Mehmed Ali punished those responsible.\n\nTHE RETURN: Medina, Mecca, Taif. Reinforcements arrived, and the forces of the Sharif of Mecca, Sharif Ghalib, and of the Harb Bedouin who opposed the Wahhabis joined them. Medina was besieged for two weeks; battering the city with cannon \"was not deemed fitting.\" The news of the conquest reached Egypt on 15 December, and Süleyman Pasha, governor of Damascus, also reported in an official letter that \"Tosun Pasha had conquered Medina and succeeded in driving the Wahhabis out of these places\" (Şimşek). Then came Jeddah, and after it Mecca and Taif. The reward was concrete: \"The recovery of the holy places was greeted with satisfaction by the Ottoman State, and after this victory Ahmet Tosun Pasha was granted the Province of Habesh, the Sanjak of Jeddah and the office of Shaykh al-Haram of Mecca, with the rank of vizier.\" Mahmud II, \"upon the capture of Medina, Mecca and Taif one after another, sent Mehmet Ali Pasha and his son Tosun Pasha swords, kaftans and robes of honour.\"\n\nPEACE — and the father's refusal to recognise that peace. In January 1815, after the great battle he won against Faisal b. Saud at Bisl, east of Taif, Tosun Pasha brought Abdullah b. Saud to the negotiating table; according to Şimşek, by the agreement \"Najd and Qasim were left to the Wahhabis, and the Hejaz to the Ottomans.\" Değirmenci puts the treaty in June 1815 and tells what followed: \"Mehmet Ali Pasha rejected the terms of the Wahhabi leader; as a result the treaty made by Tosun Pasha was left void, and both sides had begun preparations for war.\" The TDV's article `abdullah-b-suud` (Feridun Emecen) gives the same verdict: \"During the Najd operation of Tosun Pasha, son of Mehmed Ali Pasha, he made a truce with him; however, the peace negotiations produced no result.\"\n\nDEATH — not at the front but back home. Emecen ends the same sentence thus: \"When Tosun Pasha died in 1816 and his brother İbrahim Pasha came to Arabia in his place, the struggle intensified.\" Şimşek writes the cause: \"Upon Tosun Pasha's losing his life from plague in 1816, Mehmet Ali Pasha's elder son İbrahim Pasha took his place\" — and the detail is even more touching: Mehmed Ali reported to the centre that, after the wars in the Hejaz, \"Tosun Pasha, who was in the Rosetta district of Egypt for a change of air and some rest, fell ill and lost his life the next day.\" The provinces of Jeddah and Habesh were given to his brother İbrahim; İbrahim Pasha set out on 23 September 1816, and the citadel of Diriyah fell on 15 September 1818. One of İbrahim's first acts is telling: he \"made efforts to improve the health conditions that had been one of the chief causes of the difficulties his brother Tosun had suffered.\"\n\nAND the line continued. The name of this young commander, who was on the stage for barely five years, remained in the dynasty: one of the last lines of the article `kavalali-mehmed-ali-pasa` introduces the governor who took Mehmed Ali's place in 1848 — \"Tosun's son Abbas Hilmi Pasha took his place.\"", "2ff2fb6d"], "kisa": ["His full name was Ahmed Tosun Pasha: son of Mehmed Ali of Kavala, governor of Jeddah and commander of the Hejaz army. Because his commanders \"looked upon him as a child\", he was routed in his first campaign; he then recovered Medina, Mecca and Taif, and died of plague in 1816. His son Abbas Hilmi became governor of Egypt in 1848.", "831790fe"]},
"varvar-ali-pasa-perihan-hanim-1648":{"soru": ["Which orders did Varvar Ali Pasha refuse before rebelling?", "b228f039"], "baslik": ["The pasha who would not send a woman to the palace died at the hands of her husband", "4f8ec1f0"], "metin": ["Varvar Ali Pasha, who as a Bosnian village boy was taken in the devshirme, rose in the palace through falconry and told the story of his life in a verse work of his own, received two orders from Istanbul in the autumn of 1647, while he was beylerbeyi of Sivas. The first demanded 30,000 kuruş under the name of 'holiday pocket money'; the pasha had the revenue of the province calculated and reported that this sum could not be paid. The second was that Perihan Hanım, the lawful wife in Sivas of İpşir Mustafa Pasha, beylerbeyi of Anatolia, should be sent to Istanbul to be presented to the sultan; according to Kâtib Çelebi, the pasha rejected this too. He was dismissed, did not let those sent to kill him into the city, proclaimed that the affairs of state were left in the hands of women, and decided to march on Istanbul. Evliya Çelebi claims that some circles in the capital, even Kösem Sultan, encouraged him with letters. Having defeated and captured Köprülü Mehmed Pasha, Varvar Ali was surprised on 20 May 1648 in a raid by İpşir Mustafa Pasha, whom he thought had come to join him; the man whose wife he had refused to send to the palace had him seized and executed.", "f9b087d7"], "not": ["The secret letters of support from Istanbul appear only in Evliya Çelebi's account.", "abc67e71"]},
"yavuz-baba-zehir-soylentisi":{"soru": ["Did Selim the Grim have his father poisoned?", "8b655700"], "baslik": ["The suspicious death of the deposed Bayezid II", "e96748d4"], "metin": ["Under pressure from the Janissaries, Bayezid II left the throne to his son Selim on 24 April 1512 and departed from Istanbul to go to Didymoteicho (Dimetoka). According to the TDV article on Bayezid, he was taken ill on the way, in the village of Abalar near Çorlu, and died on 10 June 1512; the encyclopaedia finds the cause of death extremely suspicious and notes that the possibility of poisoning, based on certain local and foreign records, is debated. The same encyclopaedia's article on Selim the Grim, however, states that the rumours that Selim had his father poisoned appear especially in Western sources but could not be confirmed by other sources.", "dbb08b2a"], "not": ["The sources differ in emphasis: one leaves open the possibility of poisoning, while the other does not regard the attribution of it to Selim as supported by other sources. The card takes no side.", "2ea6cd5f"]},
"yavuz-olum-ur":{"soru": ["Which illness killed Selim the Grim?", "67505564"], "baslik": ["The tumour on his back: the last two months of Selim the Grim", "1ca2abb6"], "metin": ["In the summer of 1520 Selim I set out from Istanbul towards Edirne; the TDV considers it highly probable that this journey was connected with his failing health and the plague epidemic in the city. Because of a large tumour that appeared on his back, he could not go beyond Çorlu; two months of treatment brought no result, and he died on the night of 21–22 September 1520. The encyclopaedia writes that the tumour was 'probably a plague bubo'; that is, the diagnosis is a possibility, not a certainty. According to what his close attendant Hasan Can told his son Hoca Sâdeddin, the sultan drew his last breath while repeating the Sura Yasin that Hasan Can was reciting.", "a3bf4938"], "not": ["The account of the final moments rests on a single witness, Hasan Can, and has reached us through his son's work.", "00bb0c1e"]},
"yemen-elden-cikis-geri-donus-ve-birinci-dunya-savasi":{"ad": ["Yemen: the Ottomans who entered twice and twice tried to hold on", "45614a7f"], "metin": ["The first entry and exit (briefly). The Ottomans' interest in Yemen arose at the beginning of the sixteenth century from the Portuguese danger in the Red Sea and the Indian Ocean. Zabid was taken on 20 June 1516; on his Indian expedition of 1538-39 Hadım Süleyman Pasha put Aden and Zabid in order and laid the foundation of the beylerbeylik of Yemen (27 February 1539); the resistance of the Zaydis in the mountainous interior continued until Sanaa was taken in 1547 and, after 1568, retaken in Sinan Pasha's second conquest (1569-1571). In the face of the advance of the Zaydi imams (the Qasimis) between 1626 and 1635, the Ottomans gave up first Sanaa (1629), then Zabid and Mocha; the last Ottoman forces left Mocha on 22 October 1635. The details are written on the card 'dunya-yemende-osmanli-hakimiyeti'. TDV writes that even after 1635 some administrators and soldiers remained in Yemen, that the coffee trade continued and that there was an attempt to re-establish rule in 1672, but that an expedition was abandoned in 1694, and that one cannot speak of an Ottoman presence in Yemen until the beginning of the nineteenth century.\n\nTHE SECOND coming (nineteenth century). (1) 1833: a bey named Türkçe Bilmez, who had rebelled against Mehmed Ali Pasha, entered Yemen with soldiers he had gathered from Jeddah and took several towns but could not hold on. (2) 1835: Mehmed Ali Pasha sent an army and naval force under the command of Emin Bey and took al-Luhayyah and Hodeidah; İbrahim Pasha's new forces seized the Tihama. With the departure of the Egyptian army, Hodeidah passed in 1840 into the hands of Sharif Husayn, to whom the Tihama was surrendered (the TDV article 'Tihâme' speaks for the same year of 'the control of the Saudis'; the two articles diverge on this point). (3) 1839: Britain occupied Aden; the Ottoman attempt to bring Yemen under control again falls in the same period. (4) 1849: the Sharif of Mecca Ibn Awn and Tevfik Pasha the Cypriot landed by sea on Kamaran Island and at Hodeidah with a force of about 3000 men, and the town was surrendered; they wanted to take Sanaa, but because of a popular uprising they were forced to return to Hodeidah. The Tihama was taken back by the Ottomans in 1849. When Tevfik Pasha died in 1850, the part of Yemen in Ottoman hands began to be administered by governors or district governors (mutasarrıf); efforts were made to suppress the revolts around Hodeidah and Asir. (5) 1871: Ahmed Muhtar Pasha suppressed the revolt in Asir and took Sanaa, re-establishing state authority, and made Yemen a vilayet; in 1872 the sanjaks of Asir, Hodeidah and Taiz were formed, with Sanaa as the centre. The Seventh Army, set up in Yemen in 1869, had 25,000 soldiers.\n\nREVOLTS and Imam Yahya. The order secured by Ahmed Muhtar Pasha lasted about twenty years; in 1889 the Zaydis revolted because of the inadequacy of Ottoman officials and administrative injustice. A new uprising was suppressed in 1895-1897. In the revolt begun by Imam Yahya Hamid al-Din in 1902, the Governor Faik Pasha was besieged in Sanaa; when the revolt intensified in 1905 and famine began in besieged Sanaa, the city had to be surrendered to the rebels; afterwards Ahmed Feyzi Pasha, in an operation between 15 July and 1 September 1905, entered Sanaa and removed the tribal chiefs. When the revolt of Sheikh Idrisi in Asir in 1909 combined with the revolt of Imam Yahya in 1911, Ahmed İzzet Pasha was sent to Yemen on 28 February 1911 aboard the cruiser Hamidiye; with an army of about 50,000 men drawn from the Seventh Army and from Istanbul, he raised the siege of Sanaa. The agreement made with Imam Yahya on 13 October 1911 (22 open and 5 secret articles) left to the imam the administration of the mountain regions of the Zaydis, including Sanaa; the imam would remain in his position without the right to make treaties with foreign states, would give up the claim to be 'amir al-mu'minin' (commander of the faithful) and would receive 20,000 Ottoman gold liras a year.\n\nITS IMPORTANCE for the Ottomans. TDV records only the source's own sentence: in the sixteenth century the justification of the Ottoman presence was not 'tahsîl-i mâl' (collecting revenue) but was directed at the 'protection (sıyânet) of the Noble Kaaba' (document: BA, MD, nr. 7, hk. 2738). Because Yemen lay at the junction of trade routes, first Portugal and the Netherlands, and then from the nineteenth century Britain, France and Italy took an interest in it: Britain took Aden in 1839, supported the Idrisi revolt in Asir in 1912 and in 1913 concluded protection agreements with the Nine Cantons (Nawahi-i Tis'a) sheikhdoms around Aden; France tried to buy Kamaran Island in 1851 and occupied the island of Shaykh Said in 1865; Italy came to the region in 1905 and in 1911 bombarded al-Qunfudhah, Hodeidah and Shaykh Said. With their armies in Yemen the Ottomans prevented the colonial powers from entering Yemen (TDV's assessment).\n\nTHE FIRST World War question. TDV has no direct answer to the question 'Why was there fighting in Yemen while they could not protect their own core lands?'; but the article gives these facts: the peace secured by the 1911 agreement lasted throughout the war; Imam Yahya helped in meeting the needs of the Ottoman army in Yemen; the soldiers of the 7th Corps in Sanaa undertook operations against the Idrisi forces in Asir and against the British base at Aden; Imam Yahya contributed soldiers to these operations; British ships bombarded the ports of Hodeidah and al-Luhayyah from time to time. After the Armistice of Mudros, the commander at Taiz, Ali Said Pasha, surrendered at Aden with the 39th Division; thanks to the fact that Galib Bey, commander of the 40th Division in the Tihama, acted together with Ahmed Tevfik Pasha, the heavy weapons and ammunition were left to Imam Yahya. On 5 March 1919 the contingent in Yemen, consisting of 3883 soldiers and civil officials, surrendered to the British at Hodeidah. The last governor, Mahmud Nedim Bey, left Yemen in May 1924; legally, Yemen ceased to be Ottoman territory as a result of the Treaty of Lausanne.\n\nTHIS PAGE'S interpretation. A reading that can be drawn from TDV's facts: the Ottoman armed force in Yemen was not a new build-up dispatched with the First World War but a force that had been there since 1869 (the Seventh Army) and the expedition of 1911; this force took part in a regional war against the British base at Aden and the Idrisi force in Asir. As for the 'why was Yemen defended' part of the question (the security of the Hejaz and the pilgrimage route, control of the Red Sea), this is not a sourced answer but an interpretation made as the continuation, centuries later, of the justification of 'protecting the Kaaba' mentioned above.", "f1c7cdce"], "kisa": ["Yemen, effectively abandoned in 1635, was regained in the nineteenth century through three separate attempts (1835, 1849, 1871-72); it remained in hand until the end of the First World War.", "fcb43b60"], "not": ["The 'return to the Tihama coast' entry to which this card is linked in the chronology lists Hodeidah, Zabid and Moha; the TDV articles on Yemen and Hodeidah give for 1849 only Kamaran Island and Hodeidah, and the capture of Zabid and Mocha in the same year does not appear in these articles.", "5a526205"], "bag": ["On the first conquest of Yemen and the reasons it could not be held see the card 'dunya-yemende-osmanli-hakimiyeti'; on Mehmed Ali Pasha's Arabian campaign see the Egyptian campaign cards.", "5a93bd2d"]},
"yeniceri-ocagi-kurulusu":{"sebep.b": ["The need of the principality's army, from Orhan Gazi onward, with the crossing into Rumelia and the growing conquests, for a standing, salaried infantry force tied to the center", "cfd3ee6b"], "sonuc.b": ["With the accession of Murad I, the establishment of the pençik system and of the Novice Corps (Acemi Ocağı) at Gallipoli — the nucleus of the Janissary organization", "88edaafc"], "bag": ["According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, the idea of taking one-fifth (pençik) of the prisoners of war, training them and adding them to the standing army matured gradually between the end of Orhan's reign and the time of Murad I; historians still debate the exact moment of foundation, and some point to the reign of Bayezid I the Thunderbolt.", "6ddd1be6"], "metin": ["The Janissaries were the infantry branch of the kapıkulu (the sultan's salaried household troops): youths recruited through the devshirme and pençik systems were trained in the Novice Corps and then enrolled in companies called 'orta'. The number of ortas reached 165 in the reign of Suleiman the Lawgiver and later 196; the corps was organized with the ranks of çorbacı and agha and was an institution receiving a regular salary (ulufe) from the center. Mahmud II abolished the corps by turning cannon on it in the Auspicious Incident (Vak'a-i Hayriyye) of 15-16 June 1826.", "b53f43ad"]},
"yenilesme-karsiti-ayaklanmalar-etiket":{"baslik": ["“Uprisings of Those Who Did Not Want Innovation” — What the Sources Say in Five Cases", "ff766e99"], "metin": ["In Ottoman history the uprisings that broke out right after a move toward modernization form a series, and they are often told with a single formula: a rising by those opposed to innovation. Below, five cases are listed with their reasons as far as the source states them; let the reader weigh for himself or herself how far the formula holds in each case. (The scope of this card is internal to the Ottoman state; for a world comparison see the card `kiyas-yenilige-tepki-ayaklanmalari`, and for a typology of the depositions of sultans the card `tartisma-osmanli-darbeleri-tipoloji` — neither has been repeated here.)\n\n① 1622 · The Incident of Young Osman (Osman II). The revolt began on 8 Rajab 1031 / 19 May 1622, and the sultan was strangled at Yedikule the next day. The trigger was the preparation the sultan was making to go on pilgrimage: a rumor spread that this preparation was a plan to gather troops from Anatolia and do away with the Janissaries. Here the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam enters an important caveat: the reports that the sultan had given up on Istanbul, that he would march against the Janissaries with troops from Damascus and Egypt and that he would move the capital are “the propaganda of those opposed to him, which is reflected even in the sources of the period”; the account of Hüseyin Tuği, who narrated the event through the eyes of the kapıkulu troops hostile to Osman II, passed into later chronicles and led modern historiography to regard him as a ruler who was “a reformer, an innovator, wishing to carry out wide-ranging reforms”. In other words, both the portrait of the “innovative sultan” and that of a “revolt against innovation” may have been fed by the same hostile source. And the reason for the ulema's resentment was not reform but the cutting off of their arpalık (stipendiary revenue assignments).\n\n② 1730 · The Patrona Halil revolt. It began in Beyazıt on 12 Rabi al-Awwal 1143 / 25 September 1730; on the night of 1 October Damad Ibrahim Pasha was surrendered for execution, and on 2 October Ahmed III gave up the throne. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam's list of causes is long, and innovation is not at the head of it: a political, economic and social reaction going back to Passarowitz, the loss of Belgrade and Temeşvar, the failure suffered in Iran, the burden of the imdadiyye (emergency aid) taxes, the granting of mukataas (revenue farms) for life, the tax burden on the artisans' guilds, and the luxurious spending of the statesmen, verging on extravagance. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam states explicitly that activities such as the opening of the printing press, the translation committee, the libraries, tile-making, the paper and textile workshops and the fire brigade “did not present a situation of concern to the broad masses of the people”; what attracted attention was the pavilions in the French architectural style, the luxury and the dissipation. Moreover, the preparation of the revolt had been undertaken by the grand vizier's own son-in-law, the kaymakam Mustafa Pasha, who had his eye on office.\n\n③ 1807 · The Kabakçı revolt. This is the case in the series that best fits the formula of “opposition to modernization”: the spark is directly the Nizam-i Cedid — the bringing of the yamaks into this system and the incitement that they would be made to wear Nizam-i Cedid uniforms (TDV `kabakci-isyani`). Yet even here the deputy grand vizier and the Shaykh al-Islam stand behind the scene of the revolt; for details see the card `kabakci-isyani-niye-cikti`.\n\n④ 1859 · The Kuleli Incident. A conspiratorial society that was broken up on 14 September 1859, through an informer, before it had even gone into action. The causes counted by the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam are: the discontent that the Tanzimat measures produced in some quarters, the financial shock after the Crimean War, the reaction against European-style manners among the upper class, and unease over the rights granted to non-Muslims by the Reform Edict of 1856 — but at the same time soldiers and officials who “had not been brought to the positions and offices they wanted”. Here too the label is not single: Namık Kemal interpreted the Kuleli Incident as a movement for liberty and found that the secret trial of the accused by the council of ministers was contrary to the legal principles of the Rescript of Gülhane. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam also writes that the movement later influenced the Young Ottomans and the Young Turks.\n\n⑤ 1909 · The Thirty-First of March Incident. 31 March 1325 in the Rumi calendar / 13 April 1909; it lasted eleven days, the Action Army took control of the city on 24 April, and on 27 April Abdülhamid II was deposed. The soldiers of the 4th Rifle (Avcı) Battalion who mutinied rose with a demand for the sharia; among the demands the rebels conveyed to parliament was also the full application of the provisions of the sharia. But the list of causes counted by the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam is far broader: excessive politicization in the army, the conflict between officers risen from the ranks and academy-trained officers and the purge of the former, the loss of privileges by the old cadres, a sweeping reorganization and reduction of the bureaucracy, the struggle for power between the Committee of Union and Progress and the Ahrar (Liberal) Party, the repressive attitude of the Unionists, and assassinations by unknown perpetrators. And the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam makes the label itself a matter of debate: it was the Unionists who applied the description “reactionary uprising”; according to the article, in both Unionist and Islamist literature this description “has been seen as an instrument of the desire to bring back the era of despotism”, and “in our recent history the reactionary aspect of the 31 March Incident has been brought to the fore as more important than the incident itself”, serving as historical evidence for later politics. Moreover, Islamist figures of the period such as Elmalılı Muhammed Hamdi, Manastırlı İsmail Hakkı and Mehmed Akif openly declared that what had happened was unacceptable from the standpoint of Islam and that constitutional government could not be given up.\n\n📌 three patterns that emerge from the five cases — not a verdict, but an observation:\n· None has a single cause. In every case grievances over pay, arpalık, office and taxes are found within the same crowd as the complaint about innovation; it is often impossible to separate the two.\n· The crowd's reasons and the plotters' reasons may not be the same: in 1730 the grand vizier's son-in-law, in 1807 the deputy grand vizier and the Shaykh al-Islam, and in 1909 party struggle stand behind the scene.\n· It is most often the victor who has applied the label. The descriptions “reaction” in 1909 and “revolt against a reforming sultan” in 1622 come from sources that were themselves parties to the conflict. This does not mean that the uprisings were not opposed to innovation; it means that the description and the reasons must be read separately.", "5d0d6e5c"]},
"yirmisekiz-elciligin-onemi":{"baslik": ["Why Yirmisekiz Çelebi's embassy to Paris matters", "a6d4b16e"], "bag": ["Significance: TDV highlights this embassy because it regards it as a contribution to the development of Turkish-French relations and to the gradual erasing of the deep-rooted false image of the Turk in the West.", "da644279"], "metin": ["① A new job description. The instructions given to the envoy required him, apart from political business, to learn thoroughly about France's means of development and education and to report on those that could be applied. According to the article on the Tulip Era, the envoys of the period in Paris, Vienna and Moscow were likewise expected to gather information on European diplomacy and military power. The envoy was no longer only a bearer of letters but also an observer.\n\n② Repairing an image. At the reception of Müteferrika Süleyman Agha, sent to Louis XIV, problems of protocol had arisen, and at the king's wish scenes belittling the Turkish envoy had been put into Molière's comedy 'The Would-Be Gentleman'. According to TDV, Mehmed Efendi's embassy was of great importance in demolishing this negative image. He also played a major role in the spread of the 'Turquerie' fashion in Paris.\n\n③ The Sefâretnâme. It helped form the first knowledge of the West in the Ottoman lands. Assessments diverge: some say that he did not compare the two worlds; Tanpınar, however, writes that in its every line there is a hidden comparison and 'a whole programme'. TDV also notes that a connection can be drawn with the comparative outlook in Montesquieu's Persian Letters of the same years.\n\n④ Architecture. According to the Venetian ambassador Emo, the palaces and gardens in Istanbul were built according to the pictures and plans that Mehmed Efendi brought from Paris; the interpreter Lenoir had procured these plans for him. TDV says there can be no doubt that the Versailles pools and animal statues described in the Sefâretnâme served as models for the pools and public fountains (sebil) of Kâğıthane (see lale-devri-mimari-ve-sehir, mimari-sadabad).\n\n⑤ The printing press. According to TDV, one of the most important results of the embassy was the effort of his son Said Efendi to set up the first Turkish printing press together with İbrahim Müteferrika. Mehmed Efendi had told Saint-Simon, whom he came to know in Paris, that a printing press would soon be opened in Istanbul.\n\n⑥ The result that did not come about. The effort to form an offensive-defensive alliance with France against Austria came to nothing.", "4a343410"]},
"yirmisekiz-merak-kabul-toreni":{"baslik": ["Before the twelve-year-old king: a painting, a medallion and a newspaper", "1a2f9de8"], "metin": ["According to TDV, the reception at the Tuileries Palace on 21 March 1721 turned into a ceremony the like of which had not been seen in Paris. The envoy presented the imperial letter of Ahmed III, İbrahim Pasha's letter and the gifts to the twelve-year-old Louis XV and spoke of the strengthening of friendship between the two states.\n\nITS TRACES. The famous pictures showing the envoy's entry into the palace from the Tuileries garden and his departure over the Swing Bridge after the audience bear the signature of the painter Charles Parrocel. A silver medallion was struck showing the reception, with a portrait of Louis XV on its reverse. The ceremony was reported in detail in the newspaper Le Nouveau Mercure.\n\nAFTERWARDS. The envoy was invited to the king's hunts, also visited the king privately (Marshal Villeroi was present) and had the opportunity to see the king's treasury.", "7d37be94"]},
"yirmisekiz-merak-moliere":{"baslik": ["The 'Turkish envoy' on Molière's stage — and the embassy that made him forgotten", "b39c240b"], "metin": ["At the reception of Müteferrika Süleyman Agha, who went to Paris in the reign of Louis XIV, problems of protocol had arisen. According to TDV, at the king's request, scenes belittling the Turkish envoy were put into Molière's comedy 'Bourgeois gentilhomme' (The Would-Be Gentleman).\n\nThe entry into Paris of Yirmisekiz Çelebi Mehmed Efendi, who came half a century later, was magnificent, and he became the focus of public interest. TDV writes that this embassy was of great importance in demolishing that negative image.", "bb13ed6b"]},
"yirmisekiz-merak-opera":{"baslik": ["An Ottoman envoy at the opera: Thésée and Omphale", "376e64fc"], "metin": ["According to TDV, the opera that left Mehmed Efendi amazed was the lyric tragedy 'Thésée', with a libretto by Quinault and music by Lully; he watched this performance at the palace together with the king. The opera he went to the second time was 'Omphale', with words by La Motte and music by Destouches. His son Said Efendi also watched the ballet 'Les Fêtes Vénitiennes'.\n\nThe envoy also toured the fortresses and fortifications of Paris.", "3329dc49"]},
"yirmisekiz-merak-paris-sokaklari":{"baslik": ["Paris from the Sefâretnâme: seven-storey houses, crowded streets, islands in the Seine", "31d3cfba"], "metin": ["Observations summarized by TDV:\n• The city has 'wondrous and strange' buildings, palaces and gardens.\n• Houses are three or four storeys high; there are also very many houses built with seven storeys.\n• The city and its streets are crowded; the envoy attributes this to men and women being out in the street together.\n• The streets are wide and paved with paving stones.\n• The Seine runs through the middle of the city and forms three islands. Notre-Dame is also among the places he saw.\n\nINSTITUTIONS. In Paris he examined the house of physicians, the pharmacy (devahane), the observatory, the Gobelins tapestry works and the mirror factory.\n\nAccording to TDV, his describing Paris so extensively shows that he toured the city very thoroughly.", "72a849ea"]},
"yirmisekiz-merak-said-efendi":{"baslik": ["The envoy's son who was lost for three days in the corridors of Versailles", "38325595"], "metin": ["The Duc d'Orléans's elderly mother, Princess Charlotte Elisabeth, did not meet the envoy, but according to TDV she took a close interest in the safety of the mission and especially in Said Efendi's health. For Said Efendi, who moved about quite independently of his father, had got lost in the corridors of Versailles together with a few women and reappeared only three days later.\n\nA few years later this same Said Efendi was to set up the first Turkish printing press together with İbrahim Müteferrika (see lale-devri-yenilikler).\n\nNOTE. TDV gives the incident as plain information but does not show its basis in this sentence; the article's bibliography includes the 1981 Paris edition expanded with French archival documents (Le paradis des infidèles).", "80993aea"]},
"yirmisekiz-merak-versailles":{"baslik": ["A consolation in the face of Versailles: 'The world is the believer's prison and the unbeliever's paradise'", "82217276"], "metin": ["Mehmed Efendi toured the Duc d'Orléans's Château de Saint-Cloud and attended the dinner given in his honour. He then saw Versailles, the place he describes in the greatest detail in the Sefâretnâme, followed by the palaces of Trianon and Marly.\n\nAccording to TDV, he felt great admiration for the palaces, gardens, pools and fountains and described them in his own elegant style. He wrote that in the face of what he saw he found consolation in the subtlety of the saying 'The world is the prison of believers and the paradise of unbelievers'. He was aware that he had encountered a different culture; along with his admiration, he also took with him pictures and plans of the palaces and gardens.\n\nOUTCOME. TDV writes that the Versailles pools and animal statues in the Sefâretnâme served as models for the pools and public fountains at Kâğıthane (see mimari-sadabad).", "a3f64028"]},
"yirmisekiz-merak-yolculuk":{"baslik": ["From Istanbul to Paris: galleon, quarantine, canals", "bdec72c8"], "metin": ["The mission left Istanbul on 7 October 1720 aboard a merchant galleon assigned by the French ambassador and reached Toulon on the forty-sixth day.\n\nBecause there was an epidemic in Marseille, it was put into quarantine on an island near Montpellier. It then followed the road through Toulouse and Bordeaux, the canals of southwestern France and the river Garonne, and made its way to Paris by way of Orléans.\n\nTDV refers to this epidemic as 'cholera'; no separate source was read on the name of the disease.", "8c5f6ce2"]},
"zimmi-cizye-millet-duzeni":{"sebep.b": ["The fact that with the conquest of Istanbul the empire's most populous non-Muslim communities became subjects in a single center, and the need for an institutional framework to administer this population", "e3883d25"], "sonuc.b": ["The abolition of the jizya by the Reform Edict of 1856 (Islahat Fermanı) and the imposition of the obligation of military service on non-Muslims — the legal end of the four-century-old dhimma order", "117c6dd5"], "bag": ["The TDV article on the dhimmi (Zimmî) states that the classical order was “to a significant extent abolished after the Tanzimat”; the article on the jizya (Cizye) records that the Reform Edict of 1856 abolished the jizya and that it was replaced by the military exemption tax (bedel-i askerî), which lasted until 1907. According to the article on the millet, the Armenian millet had been established in 1461 when Mehmed the Conqueror appointed a patriarch in Istanbul; after 1856 lay elements entered communal administration and the powers of the patriarchates were reduced.", "9ca5302e"], "metin": ["A dhimmi is a non-Muslim who lives as a citizen in the abode of Islam and has been given a guarantee of protection: his life and property are inviolable, his worship is free, and he has the right to take disputes within his own community to his own court; in return he recognizes the sovereignty of the state and pays the jizya. The jizya was levied only on free, adult males who were able to work and able to pay — women, children, the elderly, the blind, the disabled, the poor and clergy who had withdrawn to a life of worship were exempt — and in the Ottoman state it was divided into three classes: âlâ, the highest (48 dirhams), evsat, the middle (24), and ednâ, the lowest (12). The communities were governed through the millet organization; this organization was based not on ethnic or linguistic affiliation but on religion and denomination, which is why Gregorian, Catholic and Protestant Armenians were counted as three separate millets. The patriarch and the chief rabbi collected taxes on behalf of the state, governed the community, applied the law and supervised education; but this authority was under the control of the state — in the 17th century alone the Greek Orthodox patriarch was changed fifty-eight times.", "4b065364"]}
});
